-- Logical Reasoning: longer explanations for all 490 live questions (LR-0001 to LR-0490).
-- Longer explanations: each question's "why" now says in two or three sentences why the key is right, and each wrong
-- option gets a sentence or two on the mistake behind it. Every question was re-solved from scratch and every
-- number in the explanations recomputed. Stems, options and answer keys are unchanged: every UPDATE also matches on the current key, so a
-- question whose key was edited since is left alone. Safe to run twice.

UPDATE questions SET why = 'In critical thinking a belief is reasonable when it is supported by evidence and sound logic. Evidence is something that can be checked, such as observations or test results, and logic links that evidence to the claim. A belief built this way is the one most likely to be true.', wrong = '{"0": "Popularity is not evidence. Many people once believed the Sun moved around the Earth, and they were wrong.", "1": "Feelings tell us what we want to be true, not what is true. A belief can feel right and still be false.", "3": "The age of a source does not make it correct. Old books can contain errors that later evidence has corrected."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0001' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The accident happened because unknown chemicals were mixed without supervision, which is a breach of lab safety rules. The best course of action fixes that cause. Clear, strictly enforced safety rules stop it happening again while still allowing students to learn through practicals.', wrong = '{"0": "Cancelling all practicals is too extreme. It removes necessary learning instead of making the lab safe.", "1": "Expelling one student punishes him but does nothing to stop the next student from making the same mistake.", "3": "Punishing the teacher alone does not prevent future accidents. The rules and their enforcement still need fixing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0002' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In three pairs the second number is 5 times one more than the first: 6 : 5 × 7 = 35, 2 : 5 × 3 = 15, 4 : 5 × 5 = 25. For 7 the rule gives 5 × 8 = 40, not 45. So 7 : 45 is the odd one out.', wrong = '{"0": "This pair fits the rule: 5 × (6 + 1) = 5 × 7 = 35.", "1": "This pair fits the rule: 5 × (2 + 1) = 5 × 3 = 15.", "2": "This pair fits the rule: 5 × (4 + 1) = 5 × 5 = 25."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0003' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Write each letter as its place in the alphabet. U (21) to W (23), H (8) to J (10) and Q (17) to S (19) are all gaps of 2, skipping one letter. D (4) to G (7) is a gap of 3, skipping two letters, so D : G is the odd one out.', wrong = '{"1": "U (21) to W (23) is a gap of 2, the same as most pairs.", "2": "H (8) to J (10) is a gap of 2, the same as most pairs.", "3": "Q (17) to S (19) is a gap of 2, the same as most pairs."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0004' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cousins are children of siblings. Since X''s son and Y''s son are cousins, X and Y are brother and sister or two sisters (the usual reading of this question). Y has no brother, so X cannot be Y''s brother and must be Y''s sister.', wrong = '{"0": "If X and Y were cousins, their sons would be second cousins, not first cousins.", "1": "A nephew is a sibling''s son, one generation below Y. X has a son of the same generation as Y''s son, so X is Y''s generation.", "2": "An uncle is one generation above Y. X and Y must be the same generation for their sons to be cousins."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0005' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Look at the differences between terms: 48 − 45 = 3, 45 − 40 = 5, 40 − 33 = 7, 33 − 24 = 9. The amount taken away grows by 2 each time (odd numbers). The next step is −11, so 24 − 11 = 13.', wrong = '{"0": "11 would mean subtracting 13 from 24. The pattern needs the next odd number after 9, which is 11.", "1": "8 would mean subtracting 16. The subtractions are odd numbers growing by 2, so the next one is 11.", "3": "1 would mean subtracting 23, which breaks the steady growth of 2 in the differences."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0006' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The answer depends on the original price, which is not given. At $200, 25% off is $50 off, so A ($150) is less than B ($175). At $50, 25% off is $12.50 off, so A ($37.50) is more than B ($25). At $100 they are equal, so the relation cannot be determined.', wrong = '{"0": "A is greater only when the price is below $100, because then 25% is less than $25. For higher prices it is not.", "1": "B is greater only when the price is above $100, because then 25% is more than $25. For lower prices it is not.", "2": "They are equal only at exactly $100, where 25% equals $25. The price is not given, so this is not certain."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0007' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Black is not at 1, and yellow must sit directly above black. Blue is at 4, so black cannot be at 3 or 4, and at 5 nothing is above it. So black is at 2 and yellow at 3. Positions 1 and 5 remain, and red above green gives green 1 and red 5.', wrong = '{"0": "Red must be above green, and only positions 1 and 5 are left for them, so red is at 5.", "1": "Green takes position 1, the bottom, because red has to be above it.", "2": "Yellow is at 3, directly above black at 2, not at position 2 itself."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0008' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Both quantities must be in the same unit first. 90 minutes = 90 × 60 = 5400 seconds. Percentage = (54 ÷ 5400) × 100 = 1%. A quick check: 1% of 5400 seconds is exactly 54 seconds.', wrong = '{"1": "6% of 5400 seconds is 324 seconds, not 54. This comes from not converting minutes to seconds correctly.", "2": "3% of 5400 seconds is 162 seconds, three times too large. 54 ÷ 5400 gives only 1%.", "3": "10% of 5400 seconds is 540 seconds, ten times too large. 54 ÷ 5400 × 100 gives 1%."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0009' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An assumption is an unstated idea that the argument needs to be true. The argument goes from ''Ali graduated from a famous college'' straight to ''Ali is a good doctor''. That jump only works if graduates of famous medical colleges are good doctors, so this is the hidden assumption.', wrong = '{"0": "The argument never mentions how hard Ali studied, so it does not depend on this.", "1": "Fees have nothing to do with whether Ali is a good doctor. The argument does not rely on cost.", "3": "Where good doctors work is not part of the reasoning. The argument is about Ali''s college, not hospitals."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0010' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The argument claims that breakfast causes higher scores. If breakfast-eaters also sleep longer and study more, those habits could explain the higher scores instead. This gives another possible cause, so it most weakens the claim that breakfast itself improves scores.', wrong = '{"0": "This is only a definition of breakfast. It does not affect whether breakfast improves scores.", "1": "Whether students drink tea or milk is irrelevant to the link between breakfast and test scores.", "2": "How tests are marked does not challenge the claim that breakfast causes higher scores."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0011' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The speaker rejects the research because of Dr. Kamal''s manners, not because of any problem with the evidence. Attacking the person instead of the argument is called an ad hominem fallacy. Whether he is rude says nothing about whether his research on smoking is sound.', wrong = '{"1": "That describes a hasty generalisation, which draws a broad rule from a few cases. No cases are counted here.", "2": "That describes a false dilemma, which offers only two choices. No choices are offered here.", "3": "That describes an appeal to popularity. Nothing in the statement mentions what most people believe."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0012' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A hasty generalisation draws a conclusion about a whole group from too few examples. Here a claim about all shopkeepers in a city is based on just two rude shopkeepers in one market. Such a small, narrow sample cannot represent the whole city.', wrong = '{"0": "In a valid deduction the conclusion must follow from the premises. Two rude shopkeepers do not make all shopkeepers rude.", "2": "An appeal to authority relies on an expert''s word. No expert or authority is quoted here.", "3": "Circular reasoning uses the conclusion as its own reason. Here the reason (two shopkeepers) is different from the conclusion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0013' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'This is a false dilemma: it pretends only two choices exist. In reality a person could support some parts of the timetable and not others, or oppose it while still wanting the college to succeed. Ignoring these other options is the flaw.', wrong = '{"0": "No one''s character is attacked. The statement is about the choices offered, not about a person.", "1": "No famous person or expert is quoted, so this is not an appeal to authority.", "3": "The conclusion is not used as its own reason. The flaw is the narrowing of options to two."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0014' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The argument says the cure works because millions use it. This is an appeal to popularity: the number of users is not evidence that something is effective. Only proper testing, such as a controlled trial, can show whether a cure works.', wrong = '{"0": "This is false, since many real medicines come from plants. It is also not the flaw in the reasoning.", "1": "The exact number is not the problem. Even if millions really use it, popularity does not prove it works.", "2": "The statement does not attack anyone. It praises the cure because of how many people use it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0015' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Circular reasoning uses the claim being proved as its own evidence. The book is said to be reliable only because the book itself says it is true. A source cannot prove its own reliability, so the argument goes round in a circle.', wrong = '{"1": "A hasty generalisation draws a broad rule from a few examples. No sample of cases is used here.", "2": "A false dilemma forces a choice between two options. No such choice is offered here.", "3": "A claim cannot be its own support, so this is not a valid argument. Independent evidence is needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0016' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A straw man fallacy twists someone''s view into a weaker or more extreme one that is easier to attack. A only suggested reducing sugar, but B replies as if A wanted to ban every tasty food. B is attacking a position A never took.', wrong = '{"0": "An appeal to popularity says something is true because many believe it. B makes no such claim.", "2": "A slippery slope predicts a chain of steps leading to disaster. B does not describe such a chain.", "3": "A hasty generalisation draws a broad rule from a few examples. B does not generalise from cases."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0017' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'This is an appeal to an unqualified authority. A famous cricketer is an expert in cricket, not in dental health, so his word gives no real evidence about toothpaste. The claim needs support from dentists or scientific tests instead.', wrong = '{"0": "This is false: fluoride toothpastes do help prevent decay. The flaw is who is giving the evidence.", "1": "Nothing in the statement attacks dentists. It simply relies on a cricketer''s opinion.", "3": "The claim is about teeth, as it should be. The problem is that the person making it has no dental expertise."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0018' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A slippery slope argument claims that one small step will lead, through a chain of events, to an extreme result, without evidence for each link. Allowing calculators is said to lead to forgetting addition, failing exams and a whole country unable to count. None of these steps is shown.', wrong = '{"0": "A straw man distorts an opponent''s view. Here nobody''s position is being twisted.", "1": "Circular reasoning uses the conclusion as its own reason. Here each step is a new, unsupported claim.", "2": "Each step in the chain is assumed, not proven, so the argument is not valid."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0019' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A fact is a statement that can be checked and proved true or false. The boiling point of pure water at sea level can be measured with a thermometer, and it is 100 °C. The other statements express personal taste, so they are opinions.', wrong = '{"1": "''Most interesting'' is a personal judgement. Different students find different subjects interesting.", "2": "''Best season'' is a matter of taste. Some people prefer winter, others summer.", "3": "''Tastiest'' is a matter of personal taste, and cannot be measured or proved."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0020' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The conclusion of an argument is the main claim that the reasons are meant to support. Words like ''so'' and ''therefore'' often introduce it. Here the wet roads and umbrellas are the evidence, and ''it must have rained recently'' is what they are used to prove.', wrong = '{"0": "The wet roads are a premise, one of the pieces of evidence given to support the conclusion.", "2": "People carrying umbrellas is a premise, evidence used to support the conclusion.", "3": "This is not stated in the argument at all. It is a general fact about umbrellas."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0021' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The argument treats a link between hospitals and deaths as cause and effect. Bigger cities have more people, so they have more hospitals and more deaths. Also, seriously ill people go to hospitals. These facts explain the pattern without hospitals causing deaths.', wrong = '{"0": "The cost of building hospitals has nothing to do with whether they cause deaths.", "1": "Cities without hospitals do not explain why hospital numbers and death numbers rise together.", "3": "Who keeps the records does not explain the link or expose the cause-and-effect mistake."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0022' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A sample must be large enough and represent the whole group it describes. Twenty students from a single academy cannot stand for all of Pakistan''s students, who differ by city, age and type of school. So the headline generalises far beyond its evidence.', wrong = '{"0": "18 out of 20 is 90%, which is far more than half. The arithmetic is not the problem.", "1": "This is an unsupported claim of its own, not the weakness in the headline.", "2": "Anyone can run a survey. The problem is who was surveyed, not who ran it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0023' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A necessary condition is one that must be met for a result to happen. If passing MDCAT is necessary for admission, nobody gets admitted without passing it. So every student admitted to a public medical college must have passed MDCAT.', wrong = '{"1": "This treats a necessary condition as a sufficient one. Passing is required, but it does not guarantee admission.", "2": "''Enough'' means sufficient. The statement only says passing is needed, not that it is all that is needed.", "3": "This directly contradicts the statement, which says no one is admitted without passing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0024' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The claim uses recovery within a week as proof that Vitamin X works. If most colds clear up within a week without any treatment, then recovering in a week is what would happen anyway. So the evidence shows nothing about Vitamin X, which weakens the claim.', wrong = '{"0": "Being sold in many pharmacies says nothing about whether the vitamin actually cures colds.", "2": "The colour of the tablets is irrelevant to whether they work.", "3": "When people catch colds does not test whether Vitamin X cures them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0025' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The claim is that cycle lanes reduced traffic jams. If car journeys fell after the lanes opened, while similar nearby towns without lanes saw no change, other causes such as weather or fuel prices become less likely. This comparison points to the lanes as the cause.', wrong = '{"0": "Cycling being good exercise is a health benefit. It does not show any effect on traffic.", "1": "The colour of the lanes is irrelevant to traffic jams.", "3": "If anything, this slightly weakens the claim, since people without bicycles cannot switch from cars."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0026' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Antibiotics kill or stop bacteria, but they have no effect on viruses. The common cold is caused by viruses, so antibiotics cannot cure it. Evidence and reasoning therefore show this belief to be false, while the other three are well supported.', wrong = '{"0": "This belief is well supported. Hand washing removes germs and reduces their spread.", "1": "This belief is well supported. Vaccines train the immune system to recognise a germ.", "2": "This belief is well supported. Large studies link smoking to a much higher risk of lung cancer."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0027' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is 3 times the one before: 2 × 3 = 6, 6 × 3 = 18, 18 × 3 = 54. The next term is 54 × 3 = 162. This is a geometric series with a common ratio of 3. A quick check: 162 ÷ 54 = 3, the same ratio as every earlier step.', wrong = '{"1": "108 is 54 × 2. The series multiplies by 3, not 2.", "2": "72 is 54 + 18, adding the previous term. The rule is to multiply by 3.", "3": "216 is 54 × 4. The multiplier stays 3, it does not grow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0028' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is double the previous one plus 1: 3 × 2 + 1 = 7, 7 × 2 + 1 = 15, 15 × 2 + 1 = 31, 31 × 2 + 1 = 63. So the next is 63 × 2 + 1 = 127. The gaps 4, 8, 16, 32 also double, and 63 + 64 = 127.', wrong = '{"0": "126 is 63 × 2. It forgets to add 1, which every step of the series does.", "2": "95 is 63 + 32, repeating the last gap. The gaps double each time, so the next gap is 64.", "3": "125 is 63 × 2 − 1. The rule adds 1, it does not subtract 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0029' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The terms are perfect squares: 1 = 1², 4 = 2², 9 = 3², 16 = 4², 25 = 5². The next is 6² = 36. A quick check: the gaps 3, 5, 7, 9 are odd numbers, and 25 + 11 = 36.', wrong = '{"0": "30 is 25 + 5. The gaps grow by 2 each time, so the next gap is 11, not 5.", "1": "35 is not a perfect square, so it does not fit the pattern of squares.", "3": "49 is 7². It skips 6² = 36, which must come first."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0030' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Find the differences: 10 − 7 = 3, 16 − 10 = 6, 25 − 16 = 9, 37 − 25 = 12. They are multiples of 3, growing by 3 each time. The next difference is 15, so 37 + 15 = 52. A quick check: the term after 52 would be 52 + 18 = 70.', wrong = '{"0": "49 is 37 + 12, repeating the last difference. It should grow to 15.", "1": "50 is 37 + 13. The differences grow by 3, not by 1.", "2": "55 is 37 + 18, which skips the difference of 15."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0031' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'These are prime numbers in order. A prime number has exactly two factors, 1 and itself. After 13, the number 14 is even, 15 = 3 × 5 and 16 is even, so the next prime is 17.', wrong = '{"1": "15 is not prime because 15 = 3 × 5, so it has factors other than 1 and itself.", "2": "19 is prime, but it skips 17, which comes first.", "3": "16 is not prime. It is even, so it divides by 2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0032' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'This is the Fibonacci series: each term is the sum of the two terms before it. 1 + 1 = 2, 1 + 2 = 3, 2 + 3 = 5, 3 + 5 = 8. So the next term is 5 + 8 = 13. A quick check: 8 + 13 = 21 would then be the term after.', wrong = '{"0": "11 is 8 + 3, adding a fixed amount. The rule adds the two previous terms, 5 + 8.", "2": "12 is 8 + 4. It does not follow the rule of adding the last two terms.", "3": "16 is 8 × 2. The series adds the last two terms; it does not double."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0033' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is divided by a number that grows by 1 each time: 120 ÷ 2 = 60, 60 ÷ 3 = 20, 20 ÷ 4 = 5. So the next step is divide by 5: 5 ÷ 5 = 1. A quick check: 1 × 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 = 120, the first term.', wrong = '{"0": "No step of dividing gives 0. Dividing 5 by 5 gives 1.", "1": "2.5 is 5 ÷ 2, going back to the first divisor. The divisor should now be 5.", "3": "4 is 5 − 1, a subtraction. The series works by division."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0034' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The terms are perfect cubes: 1 = 1³, 8 = 2³, 27 = 3³, 64 = 4³. The next is 5³ = 5 × 5 × 5 = 125. A quick check: the gaps 7, 19, 37 keep growing, so the next gap is 61, and 64 + 61 = 125.', wrong = '{"0": "100 is 10², a square, not a cube. It does not fit the cube pattern.", "1": "81 is 3⁴ (or 9²). It is not the next cube.", "2": "128 is 64 × 2. The series is cubes, not doubling."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0035' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is one third of the one before: 81 ÷ 3 = 27, 27 ÷ 3 = 9, 9 ÷ 3 = 3. So the next is 3 ÷ 3 = 1. This is a geometric series with a common ratio of one third. A quick check: 1 × 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 81.', wrong = '{"1": "Dividing by 3 never reaches 0. 3 ÷ 3 is 1.", "2": "−3 comes from subtracting 6, which breaks the rule of dividing by 3.", "3": "1/9 comes two terms later: 1, then 1/3, then 1/9."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0036' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is double the previous one minus 1: 3 × 2 − 1 = 5, 5 × 2 − 1 = 9, 9 × 2 − 1 = 17, 17 × 2 − 1 = 33. So the next is 33 × 2 − 1 = 65. The gaps 2, 4, 8, 16 also double, and 33 + 32 = 65.', wrong = '{"0": "66 is 33 × 2. It forgets to subtract 1.", "2": "49 is 33 + 16, repeating the last gap. The gaps double, so the next gap is 32.", "3": "64 is 2⁶, a power of 2. The terms are each one more than a power of 2, so the next is 64 + 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0037' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Divide each term by the one before: 4 ÷ 2 = 2, 12 ÷ 4 = 3, 48 ÷ 12 = 4, 240 ÷ 48 = 5. The multiplier grows by 1 each time. So the next is 240 × 6 = 1440. These are 2 × 1, 2 × 2, 2 × 6, 2 × 24, 2 × 120, so the next is 2 × 720 = 1440.', wrong = '{"0": "480 is 240 × 2. The multiplier should be 6.", "1": "960 is 240 × 4. The multiplier should be 6.", "3": "1200 is 240 × 5, repeating the last multiplier. It should increase to 6."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0038' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The gap from 27 to 38 is 11, so the gaps are likely odd numbers rising by 2: 5, 7, 9, 11. Then 6 + 5 = 11, 11 + 7 = 18, 18 + 9 = 27, 27 + 11 = 38. Every step checks out, so the missing number is 18.', wrong = '{"0": "With 16, the gaps would be 5, 5, 11, 11, which has no steady pattern.", "1": "With 17, the gaps would be 5, 6, 10, 11, which has no steady pattern.", "2": "With 20, the gaps would be 5, 9, 7, 11, which jumps up and down."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0039' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The terms follow n² + 1: 1² + 1 = 2, 2² + 1 = 5, 3² + 1 = 10, 4² + 1 = 17, 5² + 1 = 26, 6² + 1 = 37, 7² + 1 = 50. The eighth term should be 8² + 1 = 65, so 64 does not fit.', wrong = '{"1": "17 = 4² + 1, so it fits the pattern.", "2": "37 = 6² + 1, so it fits the pattern.", "3": "50 = 7² + 1, so it fits the pattern."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0040' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two series are mixed together, taking turns. The odd places hold 1, 3, 5 (adding 2) and the even places hold 10, 20, 30 (adding 10). The seventh term is an odd place, so it continues the first series: 5 + 2 = 7.', wrong = '{"0": "40 is the next term of the second series, but it comes after 7, in the eighth place.", "2": "6 is 5 + 1. The first series rises by 2, so it gives 7.", "3": "35 fits neither series. One rises 1, 3, 5 and the other 10, 20, 30."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0041' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is multiplied by −3: 2 × (−3) = −6, −6 × (−3) = 18, 18 × (−3) = −54. So the next term is −54 × (−3) = +162. The signs alternate because the multiplier is negative.', wrong = '{"0": "−162 has the right size but the wrong sign. A negative times −3 gives a positive number.", "1": "108 is 54 × 2. The size of each term grows by a factor of 3, not 2.", "3": "−108 has both the wrong sign and the wrong factor (2 instead of 3)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0042' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The differences are 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, the even numbers in order. The next difference is 12, so 30 + 12 = 42. Another way to see it: each term is n(n + 1), and 6 × 7 = 42.', wrong = '{"0": "40 is 30 + 10, repeating the last difference. It should grow to 12.", "1": "36 is 30 + 6, which does not continue the growing even differences.", "2": "44 is 30 + 14, which skips the difference of 12."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0043' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is multiplied by the next whole number: 1 × 2 = 2, 2 × 3 = 6, 6 × 4 = 24, 24 × 5 = 120. So the next is 120 × 6 = 720. These numbers are called factorials: 720 = 6 × 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1.', wrong = '{"1": "240 is 120 × 2. The multiplier should now be 6.", "2": "600 is 120 × 5, repeating the last multiplier instead of increasing it to 6.", "3": "840 is 120 × 7, which skips the multiplier 6."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0044' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Write each letter as its place in the alphabet: A = 1, C = 3, E = 5, G = 7. Each step adds 2, skipping one letter. So the next is 7 + 2 = 9, which is I. These are the 1st, 3rd, 5th and 7th letters, so the odd-numbered letters in order.', wrong = '{"0": "H (8) is only one step after G. The pattern moves two steps each time.", "2": "J (10) is three steps after G. The pattern moves two steps.", "3": "K (11) is four steps after G. The pattern moves two steps."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0045' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Write each letter as its place in the alphabet: B = 2, E = 5, H = 8, K = 11. Each step adds 3, skipping two letters. So the next is 11 + 3 = 14, which is N. A quick check: 2, 5, 8, 11, 14 all rise by exactly 3.', wrong = '{"0": "M (13) is K + 2. The pattern adds 3 each time.", "1": "O (15) is K + 4. The pattern adds 3 each time.", "3": "L (12) is K + 1. The pattern adds 3 each time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0046' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Write each letter as its place in the alphabet: Z = 26, X = 24, U = 21, Q = 17. The steps back are 2, 3, 4, growing by one each time. The next step is 5, so 17 − 5 = 12, which is L.', wrong = '{"0": "M (13) is Q − 4, which repeats the last step instead of increasing it to 5.", "1": "N (14) is Q − 3. The steps back are growing, so the next is 5.", "2": "K (11) is Q − 6, which skips the step of 5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0047' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each pair has two parts. The first letter moves forward one place: A, B, C, so next is D. The second letter moves back one place: Z, Y, X, so next is W. Together the next pair is DW. Each pair''s letters are also mirror partners in the alphabet.', wrong = '{"1": "DX keeps X, but the second letter must move back one place, from X to W.", "2": "EW moves the first letter two places. It should move one place, from C to D.", "3": "DV moves the second letter back two places. It moves back only one, to W."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0048' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Write each letter as its place in the alphabet: A = 1, B = 2, D = 4, G = 7, K = 11. The gaps are +1, +2, +3, +4, growing by one each step. The next gap is +5, so 11 + 5 = 16, which is P.', wrong = '{"0": "O (15) is K + 4. It repeats the last gap instead of increasing it to +5.", "2": "Q (17) is K + 6, which skips the gap of +5.", "3": "N (14) is K + 3. The gaps grow, so the next one is +5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0049' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The letters move forward two places each time: A, C, E, G, then I. The number is the letter''s place in the alphabet: A = 1, C = 3, E = 5, G = 7. I is the 9th letter, so the next term is I9.', wrong = '{"0": "H is only one step after G, and the letters move two steps. H8 also breaks the odd-number pattern.", "1": "The letter I is right, but I is the 9th letter, so its number must be 9, not 8.", "3": "J is the 10th letter and is three steps after G. The letters move two steps."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0050' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each group''s letters move forward by one place from the previous group: A→B→C→D, C→D→E→F, E→F→G→H. Inside each group the letters skip one (C, E, G). So after CEG comes DFH.', wrong = '{"0": "DEF runs three letters in a row. Inside each group the letters skip one, as in D, F, H.", "1": "DGH has G in the middle. The middle letter should move from E to F.", "2": "EFG starts with E. The first letter should move one place, from C to D."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0051' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'These are the first letters of the months in order: January, February, March, April, May, June, July. The month after July is August, so the next letter is A. This kind of series uses a familiar list, not a number rule, so think of common sequences such as months or days.', wrong = '{"1": "S stands for September, which comes after August, not straight after July.", "2": "O stands for October, which comes two months after August.", "3": "No month after July starts with J. June and July were the last two J months."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0052' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Compare CAT with DBU: C→D, A→B, T→U. Each letter moves one place forward in the alphabet. Applying the same rule to COG gives C→D, O→P, G→H, so the code is DPH. This is a simple shift code, in which every letter moves by the same amount.', wrong = '{"0": "EPH moves C two places to E. The first letter should move only one place, to D.", "2": "DOH leaves O unchanged. Every letter must move one place forward, so O becomes P.", "3": "CNF moves letters backwards (O→N, G→F) and leaves C unchanged. The code moves forwards."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0053' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Compare ROSE with TQUG: R→T, O→Q, S→U, E→G. Each letter moves two places forward. So L→N, I→K, L→N, and Y→A, because after Z the alphabet wraps round to A. The code is NKNA.', wrong = '{"0": "NKNZ moves Y only one place. Two places from Y is Z, then A.", "1": "MJMZ moves each letter only one place. The code moves two places.", "3": "NKNB moves Y three places. Two places from Y lands on A, not B."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0054' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The block ''triangle, triangle, circle'' repeats over and over. The last block so far has two triangles, so the next symbol completes it with a circle. Counting confirms it: the ninth symbol is the third of a block, and every third symbol so far is a circle.', wrong = '{"0": "A third triangle in a row breaks the repeating block of two triangles and one circle.", "1": "A square never appears in the series, so it cannot be the next symbol.", "2": "A diamond never appears in the series, so it cannot be the next symbol."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0055' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The block + − × ÷ repeats. The third block has started with + and −, so the next two symbols complete it with × and then ÷. The eleventh and twelfth symbols are the third and fourth in the block of four, which are × and ÷.', wrong = '{"1": "This reverses the order. In the block, × always comes before ÷.", "2": "+ − has just appeared. The block continues with × ÷ before + − returns.", "3": "The pair − + never occurs in this order within the block."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0056' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every surgeon is a doctor, and every doctor is a graduate. So every surgeon falls inside the group of graduates. This chain is a valid syllogism: all surgeons are graduates.', wrong = '{"0": "This reverses the statements. Many graduates are not doctors, let alone surgeons.", "2": "This may or may not be true in real life, but it does not follow from the statements.", "3": "This contradicts the statements, which place every surgeon among the graduates."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0057' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All roses are flowers, but only some flowers fade quickly. The quickly fading flowers may or may not include any roses. Since nothing certain can be said about roses, none of the conclusions must be true.', wrong = '{"0": "The quickly fading flowers might all be other kinds of flowers, so this is possible but not certain.", "1": "Only some flowers fade quickly, so nothing says that all roses do.", "2": "Nothing rules out roses fading quickly, so this cannot be concluded either."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0058' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every puppy is a dog, and no dog is a cat. Since all puppies are inside the dog group and that group shares nothing with cats, no puppy can be a cat. This is a valid syllogism: all puppies are dogs, no dog is a cat, so no puppy is a cat.', wrong = '{"0": "This contradicts the statements. A puppy is a dog, and no dog is a cat.", "1": "The statements say every puppy is a dog, not that every dog is a puppy.", "3": "This contradicts the statements, since no cat can be a dog, and every puppy is a dog."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0059' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some doctors are teachers, and all teachers are graduates. So the doctors who are teachers must be graduates. That means at least some doctors are graduates. It does not follow that all doctors are graduates, because only some doctors are teachers.', wrong = '{"0": "Only the doctors who are teachers are known to be graduates. Nothing is said about the other doctors.", "1": "The statements say all teachers are graduates, not that all graduates are teachers.", "2": "This contradicts the statements, since the doctors who teach are graduates."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0060' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All P are inside Q, and Q shares nothing with R. So nothing in P can be in R. The valid conclusion is that no P is R. Drawn as circles, the P circle sits inside the Q circle, and the R circle lies completely outside Q, so it cannot touch P.', wrong = '{"1": "This contradicts the statements. Every P is a Q, and no Q is R.", "2": "This contradicts the statements. If any R were a P, it would also be a Q, which is not allowed.", "3": "The statements say all P are Q, not the reverse. Q may contain many things that are not P."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0061' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two ''some'' statements do not connect A to C. The B''s that are A and the B''s that are C may be completely different B''s. So no definite conclusion about A and C follows. Drawn as circles, A and C may overlap or may be completely apart.', wrong = '{"0": "Some A being C is possible, but not certain, because the two groups of B''s may not overlap.", "1": "Nothing supports ''all''. The statements only say ''some''.", "2": "Nothing rules out some A being C, so ''no A is C'' cannot be concluded."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0062' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All mammals breathe air, and whales are mammals. Anything in the group ''mammals'' has the property of breathing air, so whales breathe air. This is a valid syllogism: whatever is true of every member of a group is true of each member.', wrong = '{"0": "This contradicts the statement that whales are mammals. A whale''s shape does not make it a fish.", "2": "The statements say whales breathe air, not that only whales do. Many other animals breathe air too.", "3": "All mammals breathe air, whether they are whales or not, so this conclusion is false."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0063' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Only doctors can prescribe'' means anyone who prescribes this medicine must be a doctor. Rehan prescribed it, so Rehan must be a doctor. ''Only'' sets a necessary condition: being a doctor is required for anyone to prescribe the medicine.', wrong = '{"0": "Only doctors can prescribe it, so Rehan cannot be a pharmacist who is not a doctor.", "1": "''Only doctors can'' limits who may prescribe. It does not mean every doctor actually does.", "3": "This contradicts the statements, since only doctors can prescribe the medicine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0064' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If rain always leads to cancellation, and the match was not cancelled, then it cannot have rained. This form of reasoning is called denying the consequent (modus tollens) and is valid.', wrong = '{"0": "If it had rained, the match would have been cancelled. It was not, so it did not rain.", "1": "The statements say nothing about postponement, so this cannot be concluded.", "2": "Rain is ruled out, because rain would have caused a cancellation that did not happen."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0065' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Rain makes the ground wet, but other things can too, such as a burst pipe or someone washing the yard. So a wet ground does not prove it rained. Concluding that it rained is the error called affirming the consequent.', wrong = '{"0": "Something other than rain might have made the ground wet, so rain is not certain.", "1": "Rain is still possible. The wet ground simply does not prove it either way.", "3": "The statements say nothing about the ground drying, so this does not follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0066' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sara is taller than Hina, and Hina is taller than Amna. So the order from tallest to shortest is Sara, Hina, Amna. Amna is the shortest. Linking the two comparisons through Hina, who appears in both, fixes the full order.', wrong = '{"1": "Sara is taller than Hina, who is taller than Amna, so Sara is the tallest.", "2": "Hina is shorter than Sara but taller than Amna, so she is in the middle.", "3": "The two statements fix the full order, so the shortest can be found."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0067' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The statements give the order from oldest to youngest: Mona, Tariq, Nida, Omar. Omar is the youngest of all, so he cannot be older than Mona. The statement is false.', wrong = '{"0": "Omar is younger than Nida, who is younger than Tariq and Mona, so he is not older than Mona.", "2": "The three statements fix the full order, so nothing is uncertain here.", "3": "There is no ''partly'' here. The order is fixed and the statement is simply false."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0068' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Both Kashif and Mehr scored more than Laila, but the statements never compare Kashif with Mehr. Either could have scored more. So the statement ''Kashif scored more than Mehr'' is uncertain.', wrong = '{"0": "Kashif and Mehr are never compared, so we cannot say Kashif scored more.", "1": "Kashif and Mehr are never compared, so we cannot say Kashif scored less either.", "3": "Laila having the lowest score says nothing about who scored more between Kashif and Mehr."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0069' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some trainees are left-handed, and every trainee passed. So those left-handed trainees passed the test. That means some left-handed people passed, and the statement is true.', wrong = '{"1": "It follows directly: the left-handed trainees are trainees, and all trainees passed.", "2": "Nothing is uncertain. The left-handed trainees must have passed with the rest.", "3": "This mixes up the groups. The statement is about left-handed people, and the left-handed trainees passed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0070' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If all seats were occupied, there was no empty seat, so Conclusion I follows. The statement says nothing about extra passengers, so we cannot tell whether anyone was standing. Conclusion II does not follow.', wrong = '{"1": "Full seats do not prove that extra passengers were standing. The bus could have had exactly one person per seat.", "2": "Only I follows. II goes beyond what the statement says.", "3": "I follows directly, because ''all seats occupied'' means no seat was empty."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0071' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ali is Sana''s brother, so they share parents. Sana is Kamran''s daughter, so Kamran is her father. Ali is therefore also Kamran''s child, his son. Assuming they are full siblings, as is usual in such questions, Kamran is the father of both.', wrong = '{"0": "Ali is one generation below Kamran, so he cannot be Kamran''s brother.", "2": "Ali is one generation below Kamran, so he cannot be Kamran''s father.", "3": "A nephew is a sibling''s son. Ali is Kamran''s own son."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0072' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fatima''s father''s father is her grandfather. His only son must be her father. The young man is the son of her father, so he is her brother. Short phrases like ''father''s father''s only son'' are easiest to solve by working from the inside out, one step at a time.', wrong = '{"0": "A cousin would be the child of her father''s brother, but her father is his father''s only son.", "1": "An uncle would be a brother of her father, but her father is the only son.", "3": "A nephew is a sibling''s son. The young man is her father''s son, so he is her brother."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0073' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Huma''s mother is Bilal''s only sister, so Bilal is the brother of Huma''s mother. A mother''s brother is called a maternal uncle (mamoon in Urdu). A father''s brother would instead be a paternal uncle (chacha).', wrong = '{"0": "Huma''s father is her mother''s husband. Bilal is her mother''s brother.", "1": "A paternal uncle (chacha) is the father''s brother. Bilal is related through Huma''s mother.", "2": "Bilal is one generation above Huma, so he cannot be her brother."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0074' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Split the code into parts. A ÷ B means A is the mother of B. B × C means B is the brother of C. So B and C are siblings, and A, the mother of B, is also the mother of C.', wrong = '{"1": "A is B''s mother, one generation above C, so A cannot be C''s sister.", "2": "This reverses the relation. A is the mother, not C.", "3": "A is only one generation above C. A grandmother would be two generations above."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0075' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Total = rank from top + rank from bottom − 1. Hamid is counted in both ranks, so one is taken away: 7 + 14 − 1 = 20. Check: 6 students are above him and 13 below him, plus Hamid himself, gives 20.', wrong = '{"0": "21 is 7 + 14, which counts Hamid twice.", "2": "19 subtracts 2 instead of 1, leaving Hamid out completely.", "3": "22 adds an extra 1 on top of counting Hamid twice."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0076' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Facing north, left is west. C is in the middle (3rd), A is directly to its left (2nd), and E is at the right end (5th). B and D fill places 1 and 4. B cannot be 4th, next to E, so B is 1st. The order is B, A, C, D, E.', wrong = '{"0": "D is in 4th place, next to E, because B is not allowed there.", "1": "A sits directly to the left of C, in 2nd place.", "3": "E sits at the right end, not the left end."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0077' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From the clues, C is 3rd, A is 2nd and E is 5th. B cannot sit next to E, so B is 1st and D is 4th. The order is B, A, C, D, E, so D sits between C and E. Every clue checks: A is directly left of C, E is at the right end, and B is not next to E.', wrong = '{"0": "B is at the left end, in 1st place.", "1": "A is to the left of C, in 2nd place.", "2": "C is 3rd and E is 5th, so there is one seat between them, taken by D."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0078' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Let Ali''s age be x, so Sara''s is 2x. In 5 years their ages will be x + 5 and 2x + 5. Their sum is 3x + 10 = 40, so 3x = 30 and x = 10. Check: Ali 15 and Sara 25 in five years, which add to 40.', wrong = '{"1": "If Ali were 15, Sara would be 30. In five years they would be 20 and 35, a sum of 55.", "2": "If Ali were 12, Sara would be 24. In five years they would be 17 and 29, a sum of 46.", "3": "20 is Sara''s present age, not Ali''s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0079' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Let the son be x, so the father is x + 30. In 10 years: x + 40 = 2(x + 10) = 2x + 20, so x = 20. Check: now 20 and 50; in ten years 30 and 60, and 60 is twice 30.', wrong = '{"0": "With a son of 10, the father is 40. In ten years they would be 20 and 50, which is not double.", "2": "With a son of 15, the father is 45. In ten years they would be 25 and 55, which is not double.", "3": "30 is the age gap between father and son, not the son''s age."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0080' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Days of the week repeat every 7 days. Divide 100 by 7: 100 = 14 × 7 + 2. After 14 full weeks it is Wednesday again, and 2 more days gives Thursday, then Friday. A quick check: 98 days is exactly 14 weeks, so day 98 is Wednesday, day 99 Thursday and day 100 Friday.', wrong = '{"0": "Thursday is only 1 day after Wednesday. The remainder of 100 ÷ 7 is 2, not 1.", "1": "Saturday is 3 days after Wednesday. The remainder of 100 ÷ 7 is 2.", "3": "Wednesday again would need a whole number of weeks, but 100 is not a multiple of 7."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0081' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If the day before yesterday was Monday, then yesterday was Tuesday and today is Wednesday. Tomorrow is Thursday, so the day after tomorrow is Friday. Counting forward: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday (today), Thursday, Friday.', wrong = '{"0": "Thursday is tomorrow, not the day after tomorrow.", "1": "Saturday is three days after today, Wednesday. The day after tomorrow is only two days on.", "2": "Wednesday is today, not the day after tomorrow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0082' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From 1 January to 1 March in a non-leap year is 31 days of January plus 28 days of February = 59 days. 59 = 8 × 7 + 3, so it is 3 days after Monday: Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday.', wrong = '{"1": "Wednesday is only 2 days after Monday. The remainder of 59 ÷ 7 is 3.", "2": "Friday is 4 days after Monday. That would happen in a leap year, with 60 days.", "3": "Monday again would need a whole number of weeks, but 59 is not a multiple of 7."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0083' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each handshake is between a pair of students. Each of the 6 students shakes hands with 5 others, giving 6 × 5 = 30, but this counts every handshake twice. So the number of handshakes is 30 ÷ 2 = 15.', wrong = '{"0": "30 is 6 × 5, which counts each handshake twice, once for each person.", "2": "36 is 6 × 6, as if each student also shook his or her own hand.", "3": "12 is 6 × 2, which does not come from any correct way of counting pairs."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0084' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'There are only two colours. Two socks could be one black and one white, but the third sock must match one of them. So 3 socks always guarantee a matching pair. This is the pigeonhole idea: 3 socks in 2 colour groups.', wrong = '{"0": "Two socks could be one black and one white, so a pair is not certain.", "1": "4 socks would also give a pair, but 3 is already enough, so 4 is not the smallest number.", "3": "20 is every sock in the drawer, far more than needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0085' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Asad and Kamil say the same thing, so they are either both truthful or both lying. Exactly one person lies, so they cannot both lie. They are both telling the truth, which makes Bilal the liar. So Bilal broke the vase.', wrong = '{"0": "Asad and Kamil both name Bilal, and with only one liar they must both be telling the truth.", "1": "Asad and Kamil both name Bilal, and with only one liar they must both be telling the truth.", "2": "Only one arrangement leaves exactly one liar, so the answer can be determined."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0086' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'T finished first. P came after R but before Q, and S came after Q. So R, P, Q and S finished in that order after T: T, R, P, Q, S. P is third. Every clue checks: P is after R and before Q, and S is after Q.', wrong = '{"1": "R finished before P, right after T, so R is second.", "2": "Q finished after P, so Q is fourth.", "3": "S finished after Q, so S is last, in fifth place."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0087' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Find the shared words. ''Apple'' is in lines 1 and 3, and their only shared code is ''ki''. ''Sweet'' is in lines 1 and 2, and their only shared code is ''lo''. The remaining code in line 1, ''pa'', must mean ''red''.', wrong = '{"0": "''ki'' is shared by lines 1 and 3, which share only the word ''apple''. So ''ki'' means apple.", "2": "''lo'' is shared by lines 1 and 2, which share only the word ''sweet''. So ''lo'' means sweet.", "3": "''se'' appears in lines 2 and 3, which share only the word ''tea''. So ''se'' means tea."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0088' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Logical Reasoning is 1st (top) and Biology is 5th (bottom). Physics, English and Chemistry fill places 2 to 4. English is directly below Physics, and Chemistry is below Physics, so Physics must be 2nd. That gives Physics 2, English 3, Chemistry 4. English is in the middle.', wrong = '{"0": "Physics must be above both English and Chemistry, so it is 2nd.", "1": "Chemistry is 4th, below Physics and English.", "3": "Biology is at the bottom, 5th, not in the middle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0089' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Facing north, a right turn means walking east. North and east are at right angles, so the path forms a right-angled triangle. By Pythagoras, the distance from the start is √(4² + 3²) = √(16 + 9) = √25 = 5 km.', wrong = '{"0": "7 km is 4 + 3, the total distance walked, not the straight-line distance from the start.", "1": "1 km is 4 − 3. That would only apply if he had walked back the way he came.", "2": "12 km is 4 × 3, which has no meaning for distance here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0090' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Start facing north. Turning 90° clockwise faces east. Turning 180° from east faces west. Turning 90° anticlockwise from west faces south. So the final direction is south.', wrong = '{"1": "North would need the turns to cancel out completely. Here they add up to a net 180° turn.", "2": "East is the direction after the first turn only.", "3": "West is the direction after the second turn, before the last 90° anticlockwise turn."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0091' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Adding 25 and 20 counts the 10 students who study both subjects twice. So students taking at least one subject = 25 + 20 − 10 = 35. Those studying neither = 40 − 35 = 5.', wrong = '{"0": "0 treats 25 + 20 = 45 as covering everyone. It forgets that the 10 who study both are counted twice.", "2": "15 is 25 − 10, the number who study only Biology.", "3": "10 is the number who study both subjects, not neither."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0092' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Split the 9 coins into three groups of 3. Weigh one group against another. The heavier pan holds the heavy coin; if they balance, it is in the third group. Then weigh 1 coin against 1 from that group in the same way. So 2 weighings are enough.', wrong = '{"0": "3 weighings would work, but splitting into groups of three needs only 2, so 3 is not the smallest.", "1": "4 weighings are more than needed. Groups of three find the coin in 2.", "3": "One weighing only narrows the search to a group of 3 coins, not to 1 coin."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0093' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''All but 7 were sold'' means every strip was sold except 7. So exactly 7 strips are left. The number 24 is not needed for the answer. The phrase ''all but'' means ''all except'', a common trap in reasoning tests.', wrong = '{"0": "17 is 24 − 7, the number of strips sold, not the number left.", "1": "24 is the number of strips at the start.", "2": "31 is 24 + 7. It adds the numbers instead of reading the phrase correctly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0094' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A clock face is 360° with 12 hour marks, so each mark is 360° ÷ 12 = 30° apart. At 3:00 the minute hand is at 12 and the hour hand is at 3, which is 3 marks apart: 3 × 30° = 90°.', wrong = '{"1": "45° would mean the hands are only 1.5 hour marks apart.", "2": "180° is the angle at 6:00, when the hands point in opposite directions.", "3": "120° is the angle at 4:00, four marks apart."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0095' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Dengue is spread by mosquitoes, which breed in standing water left by heavy rain. Removing breeding sites tackles the cause of the outbreak, so Course I follows. Closing the hospital would deny care to the very patients who need it, so Course II does not follow.', wrong = '{"1": "Shutting the hospital would leave dengue patients without care and make things worse.", "2": "II does not follow, because closing the hospital does nothing to stop dengue.", "3": "I is a sensible, practical step that attacks the source of the outbreak."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0096' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Students did well in other subjects and failed only mathematics. That points to a problem with how mathematics is taught, so reviewing the teaching follows. Expelling every student is extreme and would not solve the problem.', wrong = '{"1": "Expelling all the students is extreme and punishes them for what may be a teaching problem.", "2": "II does not follow, because expulsion does not fix the cause.", "3": "I is a reasonable step that targets the likely cause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0097' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two needs arise. The sick guests must be treated quickly, which deals with the immediate problem. The caterer''s kitchen should be inspected to find the cause and protect future customers. Both courses are reasonable, so both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "II is also needed. Guests with food poisoning need medical treatment now.", "1": "I is also needed. The cause should be found so others are not harmed.", "3": "Both steps are sensible and proportionate, so neither can be rejected."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0098' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A broken signal at a busy crossing is dangerous right now and must also be fixed. Traffic police can control traffic in the meantime, and repairing the signal removes the cause. Both courses follow.', wrong = '{"0": "II is also needed. Without a repair, the police would have to stay there indefinitely.", "1": "I is also needed to keep the crossing safe until the repair is done.", "3": "Both steps are sensible and proportionate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0099' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The student may be ill or facing a family problem, so the first step is to find out why. Contacting the parents does that, so Course I follows. Expelling the student without knowing the reason is too harsh, so Course II does not follow.', wrong = '{"1": "Expelling the student without any inquiry is too harsh and ignores possible good reasons.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "Contacting the parents is a sensible first step, so I does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0100' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The problem is drivers using hand-held phones while driving. A ban with fines on exactly that behaviour addresses the cause, so Course I follows. Banning all phones across the country is extreme and affects uses that have nothing to do with driving.', wrong = '{"1": "A nationwide ban on all phones is extreme and goes far beyond the driving problem.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "I directly targets the cause of the accidents, so it does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0101' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The problem is false information causing parents to refuse the vaccine. The right answer to misinformation is accurate information, so an awareness campaign follows. Stopping vaccination would leave children unprotected and give in to the rumour.', wrong = '{"1": "Stopping vaccination would harm children''s health and reward a false rumour.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "I is a sensible step that tackles the cause of the refusals."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0102' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two things are needed. Action against the factory stops the pollution at its source. Warning people and giving them safe water protects their health while the problem is being fixed. Both courses follow.', wrong = '{"0": "II is also needed. People drinking the water must be protected right now.", "1": "I is also needed. Without action, the factory would keep polluting the river.", "3": "Both steps are sensible and proportionate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0103' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cracks in a month-old bridge raise two concerns. Engineers must check whether it is safe and close it if needed, to protect the public now. An inquiry into construction quality finds the cause and who is responsible. Both courses follow.', wrong = '{"0": "II is also needed. Public safety comes first while the inquiry goes on.", "1": "I is also needed to find out why a new bridge cracked.", "3": "Both steps are sensible and proportionate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0104' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The child must be rescued and treated immediately, which deals with the emergency. Covering the open manholes prevents other people from falling in. Both courses follow.', wrong = '{"0": "II is also needed so that no one else falls into an open manhole.", "1": "I is the most urgent step of all. The child must be rescued first.", "3": "Both steps are sensible and necessary."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0105' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The wallet belongs to someone else, and its identity card makes the owner easy to trace. Handing it to the college office or contacting the owner is the honest and practical course, so II follows. Keeping the money is dishonest, so I does not follow.', wrong = '{"0": "Keeping someone else''s money is dishonest and is not a reasonable course of action.", "2": "I does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "II is the right course, so it does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0106' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Workers were hurt because they did not wear helmets. Making safety gear compulsory and checking its use tackles that cause, so II follows. Stopping all construction in the country is extreme and out of proportion to one site''s problem.', wrong = '{"0": "Stopping all construction nationwide is extreme and goes far beyond the problem.", "2": "I does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "II addresses the cause of the injuries, so it does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0107' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cheating was reported in only a few halls. A targeted investigation and better invigilation in those halls fits the size of the problem, so II follows. Cancelling the whole exam nationwide without inquiry would punish thousands of honest students.', wrong = '{"0": "Cancelling the exam across the country without inquiry is extreme and unfair to honest students.", "2": "I does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "II is a sensible, proportionate step, so it does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0108' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The tank is the problem, not drinking water itself. Cleaning the tank and testing the water makes it safe again, so II follows. Telling students to stop drinking water altogether is impossible and dangerous.', wrong = '{"0": "People cannot stop drinking water. This would harm the students'' health.", "2": "I does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "II is the sensible step, so it does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0109' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only one chair out of 500 has a small scratch. That is a minor fault, and at most that one chair should be replaced. Returning all 500 chairs or cancelling the whole order is out of proportion, so neither course follows.', wrong = '{"0": "Returning all 500 chairs because of one small scratch is out of proportion.", "1": "Cancelling the entire order because of one scratched chair is out of proportion.", "2": "Neither course follows, so both cannot be correct."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0110' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ten minutes of light rain is an ordinary event that causes no real harm. Closing schools for a week or declaring a flood emergency would be drastic overreactions. Neither course follows.', wrong = '{"0": "Closing schools for a week after light rain is out of proportion.", "1": "Light rain for ten minutes gives no sign of a flood, so an emergency is not justified.", "2": "Neither course follows, so both cannot be correct."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0111' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every batsman is sometimes out for zero. One poor innings is a normal part of sport and does not justify any punishment. A life ban for the player or disqualifying his team would be absurd, so neither follows.', wrong = '{"0": "A life ban for one low score is completely out of proportion.", "1": "The team has broken no rule, so there is no reason to disqualify it.", "2": "Neither course follows, so both cannot be correct."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0112' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'One complaint about a cold meal is a small service issue. Ending meal service on all flights is an overreaction, and the pilot flies the plane and has nothing to do with meals. Neither course follows.', wrong = '{"0": "Stopping meals on all flights because of one cold meal is out of proportion.", "1": "The pilot does not prepare or serve meals, so dismissing him makes no sense.", "2": "Neither course follows, so both cannot be correct."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0113' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'More candidates means more seats and test centres are needed. Arranging extra centres if needed solves this, so I follows. Cancelling the test would harm every candidate and does nothing about the real problem.', wrong = '{"1": "Cancelling the test because many students applied is unreasonable and harms all of them.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "I is a sensible step, so it does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0114' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The problem is that elderly people struggle to stand in long queues. A separate counter for senior citizens shortens their wait and solves it, so I follows. Banning them from banks is unfair and stops them using a service they need.', wrong = '{"1": "Banning elderly people from banks is unfair and makes their situation worse.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "I is a sensible step, so it does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0115' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A catchment area is the land that drains into a river. A week of heavy rain there pours extra water into the river until it overflows. So Statement I is the cause and Statement II is its effect.', wrong = '{"1": "The river overflowing cannot cause rain that fell before it.", "2": "There is no separate common cause. The rain itself directly causes the flood.", "3": "They are clearly linked, because rain directly raises river levels."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0116' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a bus overturns, many passengers are injured and taken to the nearest hospital. That explains why the emergency ward suddenly became crowded. So Statement II is the cause and Statement I is its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "A crowded ward cannot make a bus overturn on the motorway.", "2": "The accident itself directly explains the crowding, so no separate common cause is needed.", "3": "The two are clearly linked, because accident victims are taken to hospital."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0117' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Floods that destroy crops reduce the supply of vegetables. When supply falls and demand stays the same, prices rise. So Statement II is the cause and Statement I is its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "Higher prices in markets cannot destroy crops in the fields.", "2": "The floods directly explain the price rise, so no separate common cause is needed.", "3": "Supply and price are closely linked, so the two are related."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0118' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Buying umbrellas does not stop people watching cricket, and fewer spectators do not make people buy umbrellas. Both are best explained by a third factor, such as a spell of rainy weather. So both are effects of a common cause.', wrong = '{"0": "Buying umbrellas does not, by itself, stop people going to cricket matches.", "1": "Fewer people at cricket matches do not make others buy umbrellas.", "3": "They are linked, through a shared cause such as rainy weather."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0119' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sewage contains germs that cause gut infections. When it gets into drinking water, people who drink it can develop diarrhoea. So Statement II is the cause and Statement I is its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "Students falling ill cannot contaminate the water supply with sewage.", "2": "The contamination directly explains the illness, so no separate common cause is needed.", "3": "The two are clearly linked through germs in the water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0120' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hazardous air is harmful to breathe, especially for children. Schools were closed to protect them from the polluted air. So Statement I is the cause and Statement II is its effect.', wrong = '{"1": "Closing schools does not cause air pollution to reach hazardous levels.", "2": "The pollution itself explains the closure, so no separate common cause is needed.", "3": "The closure was a response to the pollution, so they are linked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0121' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Buying sweaters does not cause chest infections, and infections do not make people buy heaters. Both are effects of a common cause: a spell of cold winter weather. Cold makes people buy warm things, and chest infections are more common in winter.', wrong = '{"0": "Buying sweaters and heaters does not cause chest infections.", "1": "Chest infections in hospitals do not make other people buy heaters.", "3": "They are linked, through a shared cause: cold weather."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0122' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A hockey team selection and rain in Quetta on the same day have no link. Neither can cause the other, and no single event would cause both. They simply happened on the same day, so they are not causally related.', wrong = '{"0": "A team selection cannot cause rain in Quetta.", "1": "Rain in Quetta did not cause Ahmed''s selection for the team.", "2": "Nothing suggests one event that would cause both a selection and rain."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0123' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Modern machinery works faster and more efficiently, so the factory can make more goods. That explains why production doubled. So Statement I is the cause and Statement II is its effect.', wrong = '{"1": "Higher production came after the new machines; it did not install them.", "2": "The machinery directly explains the rise, so no separate common cause is needed.", "3": "The two are clearly linked, because better machines raise output."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0124' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Toxic waste poisons the water and kills the fish living in it. So the factory dumping waste explains why so many fish died. Statement II is the cause and Statement I is its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "Dead fish cannot make a factory dump waste.", "2": "The waste directly explains the deaths, so no separate common cause is needed.", "3": "The two are clearly linked, because poison kills fish."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0125' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fuel is a major running cost for buses and rickshaws. When petrol prices rise sharply, transport operators raise fares to cover the extra cost. So Statement I is the cause and Statement II is its effect.', wrong = '{"1": "Fares charged to passengers do not set the price of petrol.", "2": "The petrol price directly explains the fare rise, so no separate common cause is needed.", "3": "The two are clearly linked through fuel costs."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0126' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Flight cancellations do not cause road accidents, and road accidents do not cancel flights. Both are likely effects of a common cause, such as dense fog, which reduces visibility for both pilots and drivers. Lahore is well known for winter fog.', wrong = '{"0": "Cancelled flights do not cause accidents on the motorway.", "1": "Road accidents do not make airlines cancel flights.", "3": "They are linked, through a likely shared cause such as fog."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0127' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Plastic waste was blocking the city''s drains. To solve this, the government banned plastic bags. So the blocked drains (Statement II) were the reason for the ban (Statement I). II is the cause and I is its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "The ban came as a response; it cannot have caused the earlier blockages.", "2": "The blockages directly explain the ban, so no separate common cause is needed.", "3": "The ban was a response to the blockages, so they are linked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0128' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Revision helps students remember and practise what they learned. Hamza did not revise, which explains his poor score. So Statement I is the cause and Statement II is its effect.', wrong = '{"1": "The poor score came after the test, so it cannot cause the lack of revision before it.", "2": "Not revising directly explains the score, so no separate common cause is needed.", "3": "The two are clearly linked, because preparation affects results."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0129' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A library buying computers and a rare bird appearing in the Margalla Hills are unrelated events. Neither could cause the other, and no single cause would explain both. So they are not causally related.', wrong = '{"0": "Buying computers cannot bring a rare bird to the hills.", "1": "A bird sighting cannot make a library buy computers.", "2": "Nothing suggests a single event that would cause both."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0130' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Neither statement causes the other. Both are effects of a common cause: a heatwave. Hot weather makes people run fans and air conditioners, pushing electricity demand to a record, and it also makes them buy more cold drinks.', wrong = '{"0": "Using more electricity does not make people buy cold drinks.", "1": "Buying cold drinks does not raise electricity demand to record levels.", "3": "They are linked, through a shared cause: hot weather."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0131' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The student sees two things happening together, the green pen and good marks, and assumes one causes the other. That is the flaw: things that occur together are not necessarily cause and effect. Good preparation is a far more likely cause of the marks.', wrong = '{"0": "The colour of the pen is not the problem. The flaw is in how the student explains the marks.", "2": "The student says he does well. The flaw is in explaining why, not in the results.", "3": "Too many tests is not the problem. The issue is treating a coincidence as a cause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0132' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The cat crossing the path and missing the bus only happened close together in time. One event following another does not prove it caused it, and a cat has no way to change a bus timetable. Leaving home late is a far more likely cause.', wrong = '{"0": "Whether cats are allowed on roads is irrelevant to whether the cat caused the delay.", "1": "This is false, because buses can run late or early. It also does not test Bilal''s belief.", "3": "Whose cat it was does not matter to whether it could cause him to miss the bus."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0133' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ice-cream sales and drownings rise together, but neither causes the other. Both are driven by hot weather: in hot months people buy more ice cream and also swim more, and more swimming leads to more drownings. Heat is the common cause.', wrong = '{"0": "There is no way eating ice cream could cause drowning.", "1": "This reverses the link and makes no sense. Drowning does not make people buy ice cream.", "2": "They are related, through a shared cause: hot weather."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0134' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An open valve lets gas leak out and build up in the kitchen overnight. When it meets a spark or flame, the gas ignites and explodes. This explanation fits the evidence found by the investigators.', wrong = '{"1": "Paint colour has no effect on whether gas explodes.", "2": "A clean kitchen does not cause a gas explosion.", "3": "The valve was open overnight, before the explosion, so the explosion could not have opened it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0135' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Constructive reasoning looks for the real causes of a result and acts on them. Finding which sections went wrong and making a study plan uses evidence from the first attempt to improve the next one. The other responses either give up or blame things the student cannot change.', wrong = '{"0": "One test result does not prove a fixed lack of ability. Scores can improve with better preparation.", "2": "Preparation clearly affects scores, so this belief is false and stops the student from trying.", "3": "Blaming the test without evidence gives the student no way to improve next time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0136' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The argument says a new college will fix the district''s shortage of doctors. That only works if the doctors trained there stay and work in the district. If they all moved to big cities, the shortage would remain, so this is the hidden assumption.', wrong = '{"0": "The argument says nothing about cost, so it does not depend on colleges being cheap.", "1": "The argument is about a shortage of doctors, not hospitals, and does not need the district to have none.", "3": "The plan does not need every student to want to be a doctor, only enough to fill the college."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0137' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The argument credits the advertisement for the 20% rise in sales. That only holds if nothing else, such as a festival season, a price cut or a rival shop closing, caused the rise. The argument quietly assumes all such causes are absent.', wrong = '{"0": "The cost of advertisements has nothing to do with whether this one worked.", "1": "The argument is about this month''s rise, not about what will happen in future months.", "2": "If customers disliked advertisements, the claim would be weakened, not supported."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0138' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a randomised controlled trial, people are assigned to groups by chance, so the groups are alike except for the green tea. If the tea group then has lower blood pressure, the tea is the most likely cause. This gives the strongest support for the claim.', wrong = '{"1": "Popularity in China says nothing about the effect of green tea on blood pressure.", "2": "Exercise is another possible cause of lower blood pressure, so this weakens the claim.", "3": "Price has nothing to do with whether green tea lowers blood pressure."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0139' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If students who join academies already had higher marks before joining, their higher MDCAT scores may simply reflect their ability. The academy may not have added anything. This offers another explanation, so it weakens the conclusion.', wrong = '{"0": "Charging fees does not affect whether academies actually raise scores.", "2": "The location of some academies does not challenge the claim that they improve scores.", "3": "The number of questions in MDCAT is irrelevant to the argument."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0140' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An opinion is a personal judgement that cannot be proved true or false. ''Most rewarding branch of medicine'' depends on what each person values, so it is an opinion. The other statements can be checked and are facts.', wrong = '{"0": "This is a fact. The human heart has two atria and two ventricles, which can be checked.", "1": "This is a fact. The beta cells of the pancreas produce insulin.", "3": "This is a fact that can be checked: Punjab, Sindh, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa and Balochistan."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0141' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The conclusion is the claim that the reasons are meant to support, often introduced by ''so''. The facts that all samples were in the fridge and this one went bad are the reasons. ''The fridge is not working'' is the claim they lead to.', wrong = '{"0": "This is a premise, a reason given to support the conclusion.", "1": "This is a premise, a reason given to support the conclusion.", "2": "This is not stated in the argument, although the speaker may be relying on it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0142' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A necessary condition must be met by everyone who gets the result. If passing MDCAT is necessary for admission, then anyone admitted to a medical college has passed MDCAT. It does not mean that passing guarantees admission.', wrong = '{"1": "This treats passing as sufficient, as if it guaranteed admission. It is only required.", "2": "This contradicts the statement, which says passing is needed for admission.", "3": "''Necessary'' does not mean ''the only requirement''. Other conditions, such as merit, may also apply."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0143' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The speaker assumes the shirt caused the win only because the win came after wearing it. This is the post hoc fallacy (''after this, therefore because of this''). One win after wearing a shirt does not show the shirt had any effect.', wrong = '{"0": "No person is attacked. The flaw is about cause and effect.", "2": "No expert is quoted, so this is not an appeal to authority.", "3": "No forced choice between two options is offered."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0144' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The speaker says the guidebook is the best because everyone is buying it. Claiming something is good or true because many people choose it is an appeal to popularity, also called the bandwagon fallacy. Popularity does not prove quality.', wrong = '{"0": "A straw man twists someone''s argument. No one''s argument is distorted here.", "1": "Circular reasoning uses the conclusion as its own reason. Here the reason is popularity.", "3": "A slippery slope predicts a chain of bad results. No such chain is described."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0145' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The only evidence comes from the company that sells the drink. It earns money if people believe the claim, so it has a financial interest and is a biased source. A claim like this needs independent clinical trials.', wrong = '{"0": "Newspapers often print health news. That is not the weakness here.", "1": "This is false, since diabetes can be treated and controlled. The issue is the evidence.", "2": "This is a sweeping overgeneralisation and not the weakness in this evidence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0146' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An ''all'' statement is disproved by a single counterexample. Ostriches are birds that cannot fly, so not all birds can fly. One clear exception is enough to show the statement is false.', wrong = '{"1": "Sparrows are birds that fly, so they agree with the statement rather than disprove it.", "2": "Bats are mammals, not birds, so their flying says nothing about birds.", "3": "Migration shows that some birds fly, which does not challenge the statement."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0147' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The library opens only on weekdays, so if it is open, the day must be a weekday. Sana found it open today, so today is a weekday. This is valid reasoning: being a weekday is a necessary condition for the library to be open.', wrong = '{"0": "Saturday is not a weekday, so the library would be closed. It was open, so today is not Saturday.", "2": "Nothing in the statement says Sana works in the library.", "3": "This contradicts ''only on weekdays'', which means it is closed at weekends."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0148' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Anyone who failed the practical did not pass the course. Ahmed passed the course, so he cannot be someone who failed the practical. Therefore Ahmed did not fail the practical.', wrong = '{"0": "Not failing the practical does not mean scoring full marks. He may have passed with any score.", "1": "Nothing is said about the theory paper, so this cannot be concluded.", "3": "Passing the practical may not be enough to pass the course. The statement does not say it is."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0149' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is double the previous one plus 1: 5 × 2 + 1 = 11, 11 × 2 + 1 = 23, 23 × 2 + 1 = 47. So the next is 47 × 2 + 1 = 95. The gaps 6, 12, 24 also double, and 47 + 48 = 95.', wrong = '{"0": "94 is 47 × 2. It forgets to add 1.", "1": "71 is 47 + 24, repeating the last gap. The gaps double, so the next gap is 48.", "2": "96 is 47 × 2 + 2. The rule adds 1, not 2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0150' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is one more than a cube: 2 = 1³ + 1, 9 = 2³ + 1, 28 = 3³ + 1, 65 = 4³ + 1. So the next term is 5³ + 1 = 125 + 1 = 126. A quick check: 126 − 1 = 125 = 5 × 5 × 5.', wrong = '{"1": "125 is 5³. It forgets to add 1, which every term in the series has.", "2": "100 is not one more than any cube, so it does not fit the pattern.", "3": "129 is 65 + 64, adding 4³. The rule is 5³ + 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0151' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is 2 times a prime number: 2 × 2 = 4, 2 × 3 = 6, 2 × 5 = 10, 2 × 7 = 14, 2 × 11 = 22, 2 × 13 = 26. The next prime after 13 is 17, so the next term is 2 × 17 = 34.', wrong = '{"0": "30 = 2 × 15, and 15 is not prime (15 = 3 × 5).", "2": "28 = 2 × 14, and 14 is not prime (14 = 2 × 7).", "3": "38 = 2 × 19. 19 is prime, but it skips 17, which comes first."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0152' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Find the differences: 3 − 2 = 1, 5 − 3 = 2, 8 − 5 = 3, 12 − 8 = 4. They grow by 1 each time. The next difference is 5, so 12 + 5 = 17. A quick check: the next term after 17 would be 17 + 6 = 23.', wrong = '{"0": "16 is 12 + 4, repeating the last difference instead of increasing it to 5.", "1": "18 is 12 + 6, which skips the difference of 5.", "3": "20 is 12 + 8, which breaks the pattern of differences growing by 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0153' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Find the differences: 100 − 96 = 4, 96 − 88 = 8, 88 − 76 = 12, 76 − 60 = 16. They are multiples of 4, growing by 4 each time. The next is 20, so 60 − 20 = 40. A quick check: the term after 40 would be 40 − 24 = 16.', wrong = '{"0": "44 is 60 − 16, repeating the last difference instead of increasing it to 20.", "1": "36 is 60 − 24, which skips the difference of 20.", "2": "50 is 60 − 10, which breaks the pattern of differences growing by 4."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0154' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Find the differences: 3, 5, 7, 9, the odd numbers in order. The next difference is 11, so 27 + 11 = 38. Another view: each term is n² + 2, and 6² + 2 = 38.', wrong = '{"1": "36 is 27 + 9, repeating the last difference instead of increasing it to 11.", "2": "39 is 27 + 12. The differences are odd numbers, so the next is 11.", "3": "40 is 27 + 13, which skips the difference of 11."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0155' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The steps alternate between +3 and −1: 2 + 3 = 5, 5 − 1 = 4, 4 + 3 = 7, 7 − 1 = 6, 6 + 3 = 9. The last step was +3, so the next is −1: 9 − 1 = 8. Seen another way, the odd places hold 2, 4, 6, 8 and the even places hold 5, 7, 9, so the seventh term is 8.', wrong = '{"0": "11 is 9 + 2, which follows neither of the two alternating steps.", "2": "10 is 9 + 1. After a +3 step the pattern subtracts 1, it does not add 1.", "3": "12 is 9 + 3, but the +3 step has just been used. It is now time for −1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0156' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Divide each term by the one before: 14 ÷ 7 = 2, 42 ÷ 14 = 3, 168 ÷ 42 = 4. The multiplier grows by 1 each time. So the next is 168 × 5 = 840. A quick check: 840 ÷ 168 = 5.', wrong = '{"0": "336 is 168 × 2. The multiplier should now be 5.", "1": "672 is 168 × 4, repeating the last multiplier instead of increasing it to 5.", "3": "1008 is 168 × 6, which skips the multiplier 5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0157' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Write each letter as its place in the alphabet: D = 4, G = 7, J = 10, M = 13, P = 16. Each step adds 3, skipping two letters. So the next is 16 + 3 = 19, which is S. These letters are evenly spaced, three apart.', wrong = '{"0": "R (18) is P + 2. The pattern adds 3 each time.", "1": "T (20) is P + 4. The pattern adds 3 each time.", "2": "Q (17) is P + 1. The pattern adds 3 each time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0158' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two series take turns. One goes forward from the start of the alphabet: A, B, C. The other goes back from the end: Z, Y, X. The seventh place belongs to the forward series, so the next letter is D.', wrong = '{"1": "W is the next letter of the backward series, but it comes in the eighth place, after D.", "2": "E skips D, which comes first in the forward series.", "3": "V skips W in the backward series, and it is also the forward series'' turn."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0159' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Write each letter as its place in the alphabet: Y = 25, W = 23, T = 20, P = 16. The steps back are 2, 3, 4, growing by one each time. The next step is 5, so 16 − 5 = 11, which is K.', wrong = '{"0": "L (12) is P − 4, which repeats the last step instead of increasing it to 5.", "2": "J (10) is P − 6, which skips the step of 5.", "3": "M (13) is P − 3. The steps back are growing, so the next is 5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0160' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each pair is the next two letters of the alphabet in order: A B, C D, E F, G H. After H come I and J, so the next pair is IJ. So the whole alphabet is being written out in pairs.', wrong = '{"0": "HI repeats H, which was already used in GH.", "1": "IK skips J. The second letter should come right after I.", "3": "JK skips I, which must come right after H."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0161' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The numbers rise by 2: 2, 4, 6, 8, then 10. Each letter is the letter at that place in the alphabet: B is 2nd, D is 4th, F is 6th, H is 8th. The 10th letter is J, so the next term is 10J.', wrong = '{"0": "The number 10 is right, but I is the 9th letter. The 10th letter is J.", "1": "The numbers rise by 2, so after 8 comes 10, not 9.", "2": "12 skips 10. The numbers rise by 2 each time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0162' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each word is coded as the sum of its letters'' places in the alphabet. For DOG: D = 4, O = 15, G = 7. So 4 + 15 + 7 = 26. A quick check of the rule with CAT: 3 + 1 + 20 = 24, as given.', wrong = '{"1": "24 is the code for CAT, the example, not for DOG.", "2": "27 is one more than the correct sum of 4 + 15 + 7.", "3": "25 is one less than the correct sum of 4 + 15 + 7."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0163' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Compare BOOK with DQQM: B→D, O→Q, O→Q, K→M. Each letter moves two places forward. So P→R, E→G, N→P, and PEN is written RGP. The same rule works for every letter of BOOK, so it is a steady shift of two.', wrong = '{"0": "QFO moves each letter only one place. The code moves two places.", "2": "RGO codes P and E correctly, but N moves two places to P, not one place to O.", "3": "SHQ moves each letter three places. The code moves two places."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0164' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '16 = 4², 25 = 5² and 36 = 6² are all perfect squares. 27 is not a perfect square; it is a cube, 3³ = 27. So 27 is the odd one out. The other three numbers can each be written as a whole number multiplied by itself.', wrong = '{"0": "16 = 4 × 4 is a perfect square, like 25 and 36.", "1": "25 = 5 × 5 is a perfect square, like 16 and 36.", "3": "36 = 6 × 6 is a perfect square, like 16 and 25."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0165' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The femur (thigh bone), tibia (shin bone) and humerus (upper arm bone) are all bones of the skeleton. The kidney is an organ of the excretory system, not a bone. So kidney is the odd one out.', wrong = '{"0": "The femur is the thigh bone, so it belongs with the other bones.", "1": "The tibia is the shin bone, so it belongs with the other bones.", "2": "The humerus is the upper arm bone, so it belongs with the other bones."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0166' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The heart is the main organ of circulation, pumping blood around the body. In the same way, the lungs are the main organs of respiration, where oxygen enters the blood and carbon dioxide leaves it. So heart is to circulation as lungs are to respiration.', wrong = '{"1": "The liver works mainly in digestion and metabolism, for example making bile.", "2": "Bones belong to the skeletal system, giving support and protection.", "3": "Skin is a protective covering, not the main organ of respiration in humans."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0167' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A cardiologist is a specialist in the heart. The specialist who studies birds is an ornithologist (from Greek ornith, meaning bird). So cardiologist is to heart as ornithologist is to birds.', wrong = '{"0": "A botanist studies plants, not birds.", "2": "An entomologist studies insects, not birds.", "3": "A zoologist studies all animals in general, not birds in particular."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0168' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All surgeons are doctors, and all doctors are graduates, so every surgeon is a graduate. Conclusion I follows. Conclusion II reverses this: many graduates are not doctors at all, so not all graduates are surgeons. Only I follows.', wrong = '{"1": "II reverses the relationship. The statements say surgeons are graduates, not the other way round.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "I follows directly from the two statements."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0169' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some students are athletes, and all athletes are healthy. So those students who are athletes are healthy, and Conclusion I follows. Nothing is known about the students who are not athletes, so Conclusion II does not follow.', wrong = '{"1": "II goes too far. Only the students who are athletes are known to be healthy.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "I follows, because the students who are athletes must be healthy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0170' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All whales are mammals, and no fish is a mammal. So no whale can be a fish, and Conclusion I follows. Conclusion II says some fish are whales, which contradicts this. Only I follows.', wrong = '{"1": "II contradicts I. If no whale is a fish, then no fish is a whale.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "I follows, because whales are mammals and fish are not."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0171' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two ''some'' statements give no definite link between books and pencils. The books that are pens may or may not be the pens that are pencils. So ''some books are pencils'' is not certain, and neither is ''no book is a pencil''. Neither follows.', wrong = '{"0": "The books that are pens need not be the pens that are pencils, so I is not certain.", "1": "It is possible that some books are pencils, so II is not certain either.", "2": "The two conclusions contradict each other, so both cannot follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0172' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only some flowers fade quickly, and these may or may not be roses, so Conclusion I is not certain. ''All roses are flowers'' cannot be reversed to ''all flowers are roses'', so Conclusion II does not follow either. Neither follows.', wrong = '{"0": "The quickly fading flowers might not include any roses, so I is not certain.", "1": "II reverses the first statement. Many flowers are not roses.", "2": "Neither conclusion follows, so both cannot be correct."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0173' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The statement says check-ups are free for everyone over 60 this Sunday. So people over 60 can have their eyes checked without paying on that day. This is a direct restatement of the announcement.', wrong = '{"0": "The number of eye doctors is never mentioned.", "1": "The offer is limited by age, but nothing is said about the eyes of younger people.", "3": "The offer is for ''this Sunday'' only, not every Sunday."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0174' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ali''s mother''s father is his maternal grandfather. Ali''s mother is herself a daughter of this man, and he has only one daughter. So the woman Ali is pointing to must be his mother.', wrong = '{"0": "An aunt would be another daughter of the grandfather, but the woman is his only daughter.", "1": "A sister would be the daughter of Ali''s mother, one generation lower.", "2": "A grandmother would be the wife of his mother''s father, not his daughter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0175' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A is B''s brother, and B is C''s sister, so A, B and C are siblings. C is D''s father. A is a male sibling of D''s father, so A is D''s uncle (paternal uncle). A father''s brother is called a paternal uncle (chacha).', wrong = '{"1": "C is D''s father, not A. A is C''s brother.", "2": "A is one generation above D, so he cannot be D''s brother.", "3": "A is C''s brother, not his father, so A cannot be D''s grandfather."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0176' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rule is: if the temperature is above 38 °C, the nurse calls the doctor. The nurse did not call, so the condition was not met. This is denying the consequent (modus tollens): the temperature was not above 38 °C.', wrong = '{"0": "If it were above 38 °C, the rule says the nurse would have called. She did not.", "2": "The rule says nothing about the doctor being busy.", "3": "Taken as true, the rule always applies, so forgetting is not allowed for."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0177' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Rain is one reason the match would be cancelled, but not the only possible one. It could have been cancelled for a power cut or a team not arriving. So the cancellation does not prove it rained. Assuming it did is the error of affirming the consequent.', wrong = '{"0": "Rain is one possible cause of the cancellation, not the only one.", "1": "Rain is still possible, so we cannot say it did not rain.", "3": "Nothing is said about tomorrow, so this cannot be concluded."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0178' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bilal is older than Hamza, Usman is older than Bilal, and Zain is older than Usman. So from youngest to oldest: Hamza, Bilal, Usman, Zain. Zain is the oldest. Joining the clues through Bilal and Usman, who appear in two comparisons each, fixes the full order.', wrong = '{"0": "Usman is younger than Zain, so he is not the oldest.", "1": "Bilal is younger than Usman, so he is not the oldest.", "2": "Hamza is younger than Bilal, so he is the youngest."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0179' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Rank from bottom = total − rank from top + 1. So 40 − 12 + 1 = 29. Check: 11 students are above Bilal and 28 below him, so he is 29th from the bottom. Check: 11 students rank above Bilal and 28 below him, so 11 + 1 + 28 = 40.', wrong = '{"1": "28 is 40 − 12, which forgets to add 1. It counts only the students below him.", "2": "30 is 40 − 12 + 2, which adds one too many.", "3": "27 is 40 − 12 − 1, which subtracts instead of adding 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0180' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '5 km north and then 9 km south leaves him 4 km south of the start. He is also 3 km east. These two distances are at right angles, so by Pythagoras the distance is √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5 km.', wrong = '{"0": "7 km is 3 + 4, adding the two parts instead of using Pythagoras.", "2": "4 km is only the north-south distance. It ignores the 3 km east.", "3": "17 km is 5 + 3 + 9, the total distance walked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0181' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Start facing north. Turning right faces east. Turning right again faces south. Turning left from south faces east. So she ends up facing east. Each right turn is 90° clockwise and each left turn 90° anticlockwise, so two rights and one left leave a net single right turn from north.', wrong = '{"0": "West would need a left turn from north or a right turn from south. Her last turn was left from south.", "1": "She faces north only at the start, before any turns.", "3": "South is where she faces after the second turn, before turning left."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0182' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The minute hand moves 6° per minute and the hour hand 0.5° per minute. At 3:30 the minute hand is at 30 × 6° = 180°. The hour hand is at 3 × 30° + 30 × 0.5° = 105°. The angle between them is 180° − 105° = 75°.', wrong = '{"0": "90° is the angle at 3:00. By 3:30 the hour hand has moved halfway towards 4.", "1": "60° does not match the hands'' positions of 180° and 105° at 3:30.", "2": "105° is the hour hand''s position from 12, not the angle between the two hands."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0183' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From 5 March to 26 March is 26 − 5 = 21 days. 21 = 3 × 7, exactly three weeks. So 26 March falls on the same day as 5 March: Tuesday. Any date a multiple of 7 days later falls on the same weekday.', wrong = '{"1": "Monday, one day before Tuesday, would need a gap of 20 days. The gap is 21 days.", "2": "Wednesday would need 22 days. The gap is 21 days, exactly three weeks.", "3": "Sunday would need 19 days. The gap is 21 days, exactly three weeks."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0184' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The sister is 7 now, so in 2 years she will be 9. Hamza will then be twice her age: 2 × 9 = 18. His present age is 18 − 2 = 16. Check: in 2 years Hamza is 18 and his sister is 9, and 18 = 2 × 9.', wrong = '{"0": "14 is twice her present age. The condition is about their ages in 2 years.", "2": "18 is Hamza''s age in 2 years, not now.", "3": "With 12, Hamza would be 14 in 2 years while his sister is 9, which is not double."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0185' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '5 like neither, so 30 − 5 = 25 like at least one subject. Adding 18 + 15 = 33 counts the students who like both twice. So the number who like both is 33 − 25 = 8. Check: 10 like only biology, 7 only chemistry, 8 both and 5 neither, which adds to 30.', wrong = '{"0": "3 is 33 − 30, which forgets to remove the 5 students who like neither subject.", "1": "5 is the number who like neither subject, not both.", "3": "13 is 18 − 5, which has no meaning here. The correct overlap is 33 − 25 = 8."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0186' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A diagonal joins two corners that are not next to each other. From each of the 6 corners, 3 diagonals can be drawn, giving 6 × 3 = 18, but each is counted twice. So a hexagon has n(n − 3) ÷ 2 = 6 × 3 ÷ 2 = 9 diagonals.', wrong = '{"0": "6 is the number of sides, not diagonals.", "1": "12 does not come from the formula n(n − 3) ÷ 2, which gives 9.", "2": "15 counts every line joining two corners, including the 6 sides: 15 − 6 = 9."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0187' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Count squares of each size. A 4 × 4 grid holds 16 squares of size 1 × 1, 9 of size 2 × 2, 4 of size 3 × 3 and 1 of size 4 × 4. Total = 16 + 9 + 4 + 1 = 30. In general, an n × n grid holds 1² + 2² + ... + n² squares.', wrong = '{"1": "16 counts only the smallest 1 × 1 squares.", "2": "20 is 16 + 4, which misses the nine 2 × 2 squares and the one 4 × 4 square.", "3": "25 is 16 + 9, which misses the four 3 × 3 squares and the one 4 × 4 square."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0188' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Suppose C tells the truth. Then A and B both lie, but A''s claim ''B is a liar'' would be true, a contradiction. So C lies. Then B''s claim ''C is a liar'' is true, so B is truthful, and A''s claim ''B is a liar'' is false, so A lies. Only B tells the truth.', wrong = '{"0": "A says B is a liar, but B tells the truth, so A''s claim is false and A is a liar.", "2": "C says A and B are both liars, but B is truthful, so C''s claim is false.", "3": "Both A and C make false claims, so neither tells the truth."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0189' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'T finishes first. The other clues form a chain: P before Q, Q before S, S before R. So the order is T, P, Q, S, R, and Q finishes third. Every clue checks: T is first, and P, Q, S, R follow in the order the clues require.', wrong = '{"0": "P comes right after T, so P is second.", "1": "S comes after Q, so S is fourth, not third.", "3": "R comes after S, so R is last, in fifth place."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0190' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'T finishes first, and the other clues form a chain: P before Q, Q before S, S before R. So the full order is T, P, Q, S, R. R comes after everyone else, so R finishes last.', wrong = '{"0": "S finishes before R, so S is fourth, not last.", "1": "Q finishes before S and R, so Q is third.", "2": "P finishes right after T, so P is second."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0191' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Number the seats 1 to 6 going in the direction of A''s right, with A in seat 1, so D (opposite) is in seat 4. B, on A''s right, is in seat 2, and C, opposite B, is in seat 5. D''s left is the direction back towards seat 3, so E is in seat 3. F takes seat 6, which is opposite seat 3: E.', wrong = '{"1": "A sits opposite D, so A cannot also be opposite F.", "2": "B sits opposite C, so B cannot also be opposite F.", "3": "C sits opposite B, so C cannot also be opposite F."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0192' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Blue'' is the only word that appears in both phrases. ''Ri'' is the only code that appears in both coded phrases. So ''ri'' means blue. Shared words and shared codes must match, which is the key method for code-language questions.', wrong = '{"0": "''Ta'' appears only in the first phrase, so it must stand for ''sky'' or ''is''.", "2": "''Na'' appears only in the first phrase, so it must stand for ''sky'' or ''is''.", "3": "''Lo'' appears only in the second phrase, so it must mean ''sea''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0193' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A large crack in an old building may mean it is unsafe. Engineers should inspect it to judge how serious the damage is. Until then, keeping students out of the damaged part protects them. Both courses are sensible, so both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "II is also needed. Students should be kept safe while the inspection is done.", "1": "I is also needed. Only an inspection can show whether the building is safe.", "3": "Both steps are reasonable and proportionate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0194' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Stomach upsets after meals point to contaminated food or water. Checking the kitchen and water supply tackles the likely cause, so I follows. Closing the hostel permanently is extreme when the problem can probably be fixed.', wrong = '{"1": "Permanent closure is out of proportion when the cause can be found and fixed.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "I is a practical step that targets the cause, so it does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0195' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The accidents happen at one sharp bend. Warning signs and speed limits near the bend address that specific danger, so I follows. Banning all vehicles would make the whole road useless and is far too extreme.', wrong = '{"1": "Banning all vehicles from the road is extreme and ignores a simple fix at the bend.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "I is a practical step that targets the danger, so it does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0196' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Selling expired medicines puts patients at risk. Suspending this pharmacy''s licence while an inquiry is done protects the public and is fair, so I follows. Telling people to avoid all pharmacies punishes many for one offender and would stop patients getting medicines.', wrong = '{"1": "Avoiding all pharmacies is an overreaction that would harm patients who need medicines.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "I is a proportionate step, so it does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0197' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Dengue is spread by mosquitoes that breed in standing water. More fogging and removing standing water attacks the cause, and preparing extra hospital beds readies the system for more patients. Both courses are reasonable, so both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "II is also reasonable, since doubling of cases means more patients will need beds.", "1": "I is also reasonable, since controlling mosquitoes slows the spread.", "3": "Both steps are sensible responses to the rise in cases."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0198' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A student broke the exam rules by using a phone. Dealing with the student under those rules is the fair and correct response, so I follows. Banning mobile phones across the whole country because of one student is far out of proportion.', wrong = '{"1": "A nationwide ban on phones because of one exam case is extreme.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "I follows, because the rules exist for exactly this situation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0199' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Students are struggling with English in their textbooks. Extra English support classes help them cope directly, so I follows. Translating every book and stopping English teaching is extreme and would leave students less prepared.', wrong = '{"1": "Removing English entirely is extreme and would weaken students'' skills further.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "I is a reasonable step, so it does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0200' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A sudden shortage of a blood pressure medicine needs two responses. The authorities should find out why supply stopped, so it can be restored. Meanwhile doctors should guide patients to safe alternatives so their treatment is not interrupted. Both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "II is also needed. Patients cannot safely stop their blood pressure treatment.", "1": "I is also needed. Without finding the cause, the shortage may continue.", "3": "Both steps are sensible and proportionate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0201' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The website crashed because too many people visited it at once. Increasing its capacity before the next result fixes that cause, so I follows. Stopping online results altogether would be an overreaction that removes a useful service.', wrong = '{"1": "Stopping online results is extreme when the problem is only capacity.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "I is a practical fix, so it does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0202' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two workers were injured and need urgent treatment, so taking them to hospital follows. The explosion also shows gas safety may be poor, so inspecting restaurant kitchens helps prevent repeats. Both courses follow.', wrong = '{"0": "II is also needed, to prevent similar explosions in other kitchens.", "1": "I is the most immediate need. The injured workers must be treated.", "3": "Both steps are sensible and necessary."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0203' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Low marks in one weekly test suggest some topics were not understood. Finding out which topics were difficult lets the teacher help, so II follows. Expelling students for one poor test is far too harsh.', wrong = '{"0": "Expulsion for one low test score is extreme and does not help the students learn.", "2": "I does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "II is a sensible step, so it does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0204' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The breathing problems come from brick kiln smoke. Requiring kilns to switch to cleaner technology cuts the smoke at its source, so I follows. Stopping all construction in the country is far out of proportion to the problem.', wrong = '{"1": "Stopping all construction nationwide is extreme and does not target the kilns.", "2": "II does not follow, so both cannot be correct.", "3": "I is a practical step that targets the source, so it does follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0205' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the price of something rises, people usually buy less of it. The canteen raised its prices, so fewer students bought lunch there. Statement I is the cause and Statement II is its effect.', wrong = '{"1": "Fewer buyers came after the price rise; they did not cause it.", "2": "The price rise directly explains the drop, so no separate common cause is needed.", "3": "Price and demand are closely linked, so the statements are related."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0206' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Typhoid is spread through food or water contaminated with sewage. A leaking sewage pipe in the drinking water supply explains why many people fell ill. So Statement II is the cause and Statement I is its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "People falling ill cannot make a sewage pipe leak.", "2": "The leak directly explains the illness, so no separate common cause is needed.", "3": "The two are clearly linked through contaminated water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0207' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Closing schools does not cause heatstroke, and heatstroke cases do not by themselves close schools. Read together, both are most likely effects of a common cause: a severe heatwave, which makes schools close to protect children and sends many people to hospital.', wrong = '{"0": "Closing schools does not cause people to get heatstroke.", "1": "Hospital cases do not by themselves close schools. The heat behind both is the cause.", "3": "They are linked, through a likely shared cause: a heatwave."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0208' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A cricketer opening a restaurant and a rise in gold prices are unrelated events. Neither can cause the other, and no single cause would explain both. So they are not causally related.', wrong = '{"0": "One new restaurant cannot change the price of gold.", "1": "Gold prices do not make cricketers open restaurants.", "2": "Nothing suggests one event that would cause both."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0209' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Weekly tests give students a regular deadline, which pushes them to revise every week instead of only before exams. So introducing the tests explains the more regular revision. Statement I is the cause and Statement II is its effect.', wrong = '{"1": "Students revising did not introduce the tests; the college did.", "2": "The tests directly explain the change, so no separate common cause is needed.", "3": "The two are clearly linked through the effect of regular testing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0210' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Mosquitoes lay their eggs in stagnant water. After the floods left such water in many places, mosquitoes multiplied and spread diseases such as dengue and malaria. So Statement II is the cause and Statement I is its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "Diseases cannot create stagnant water.", "2": "Stagnant water is itself the cause here, through mosquito breeding.", "3": "They are clearly linked through mosquito breeding."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0211' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Buying warm clothes does not make people buy heaters, and buying heaters does not make them buy clothes. Both are effects of a common cause: unusually cold weather, which makes people want to keep warm in every way.', wrong = '{"0": "Buying warm clothes does not cause people to buy gas heaters.", "1": "Buying gas heaters does not cause people to buy warm clothes.", "3": "They are linked, through a clear shared cause: cold weather."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0212' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A better seed variety can grow stronger plants that produce more grain. That explains why the wheat harvest was much bigger than last year. So Statement I is the cause and Statement II is its effect.', wrong = '{"1": "The bigger harvest came after the seeds were sown, so it cannot have changed them.", "2": "The seeds directly explain the larger harvest, so no separate common cause is needed.", "3": "Seed quality and yield are clearly linked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0213' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Diarrhoea is often spread by germs in contaminated drinking water. A filtration plant removes many of these germs, so people drink cleaner water and fewer get infected. This is the most likely reason for the fall in cases.', wrong = '{"0": "Nothing suggests people changed their diet, and vegetables do not explain the timing.", "1": "A larger population would not reduce illness; if anything, cases might rise.", "3": "Hot weather usually increases diarrhoea, since germs grow faster in heat."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0214' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Children who own more books may come from homes with more family support, more educated parents or more money. These factors could raise both book ownership and marks. So simply buying books may not raise a child''s marks; the argument confuses a link with a cause.', wrong = '{"0": "The cost of books is a practical concern, not the logical flaw in the argument.", "1": "Marks can be measured, so this is false and not the flaw.", "2": "This is an unsupported generalisation, not the flaw in the reasoning."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0215' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Building mosques does not create shops, and shops do not lead to mosques. A growing population needs more of both: more places of worship and more shops. So population growth is the common cause that explains both rises.', wrong = '{"1": "Building mosques does not directly cause shops to open.", "2": "Opening shops does not directly cause mosques to be built.", "3": "There is a clear common cause, so calling it a coincidence is not the best explanation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0216' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The premise says divisibility by 6 leads to divisibility by 3, but the argument runs it backwards, from 3 to 6. This is the error of affirming the consequent. 21 shows the problem: it is divisible by 3 (3 × 7) but not by 6.', wrong = '{"0": "True premises do not make an argument valid; the form must be correct. Here 21 ÷ 6 = 3.5, not a whole number.", "1": "Being odd is irrelevant to validity, and it actually shows 21 cannot be divisible by 6.", "2": "21 is divisible by 3 (21 = 3 × 7), so this reason is false."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0217' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first argument denies the consequent (modus tollens), which is a valid form. If the fuse had blown, the lights would be out. The lights are not out, so the fuse cannot have blown.', wrong = '{"1": "This affirms the consequent. The lights could go out for another reason, such as a power cut.", "2": "This denies the antecedent. The lights could still go out for another reason.", "3": "''Some fuses blow'' does not link this power loss to any fuse, so the conclusion does not follow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0218' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a valid argument, the missing step must place Ayesha in the group of surgeons. With ''Ayesha is a surgeon'' the argument reads: all surgeons are doctors, Ayesha is a surgeon, so Ayesha is a doctor. The conclusion now follows necessarily.', wrong = '{"0": "This does not say anything about Ayesha, so the conclusion still does not follow.", "2": "Many people who work in hospitals are not doctors, such as nurses and clerks.", "3": "Wanting to be a surgeon does not make her a surgeon or a doctor."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0219' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To prove an ''every'' claim false, one counterexample is enough. 2 is a prime number, since its only factors are 1 and 2, and it is even. So not every prime number is odd.', wrong = '{"0": "An odd number that is not prime does not contradict ''every prime is odd''.", "1": "''Most primes are odd'' agrees with the claim; it does not refute it.", "3": "1 is not prime, so it says nothing about whether all primes are odd."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0220' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ships disappearing hull-first over the horizon and the Earth''s round shadow on the Moon during every lunar eclipse are observations anyone can check. A sphere explains both. A good belief rests on such evidence, not on popularity or authority alone.', wrong = '{"0": "What most people believe is not evidence. Popular beliefs can be wrong.", "1": "A famous person''s word is weaker than evidence that anyone can check.", "2": "Globes are made to match the belief, so they cannot be evidence for it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0221' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Independent tests compared the machine with trusted hospital equipment on many units and found the readings matched. This is real, unbiased evidence of accuracy. It best justifies Asim''s belief.', wrong = '{"1": "An advertisement comes from the seller, who benefits from the claim, so it is not independent evidence.", "2": "A friend liking its colour has nothing to do with whether its readings are accurate.", "3": "A high price does not prove accuracy; expensive products can still be faulty."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0222' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Large trials showed that liver test changes are common with drug Q. That signals a real risk worth watching, so regular liver checks are the reasonable response. The evidence does not show every patient is harmed or that the drug must be banned.', wrong = '{"0": "The evidence shows changes in tests, not that the drug''s harm outweighs its benefits.", "2": "''Often'' is not ''everyone'', and changes in liver tests are not the same as liver failure.", "3": "Large trials over many years found a repeated pattern, which suggests a real link."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0223' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Antibiotics kill or stop bacteria but have no effect on viruses. The common cold is caused by viruses, so antibiotics cannot cure it. This belief is false, while the other three are supported by evidence.', wrong = '{"0": "This is true. Vaccines prompt the immune system to make antibodies against a germ.", "1": "This is true. Boiling water kills most disease-causing germs.", "3": "This is true and well supported. Hand washing removes germs from the hands."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0224' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The belief claims that exams on the 13th lead to worse results. Results over many years showing no difference between the 13th and other dates test this claim directly. That evidence shows the belief is false.', wrong = '{"0": "Some people fearing 13 does not test whether the date affects exam results.", "1": "This suggests another cause for this student, but it does not test the general belief about 13.", "2": "Buildings skipping the 13th floor shows the belief is common, not whether it is true."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0225' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The original argument has the form: all A are B; X is an A; so X is B. The metals argument follows exactly the same form: all metals conduct electricity, copper is a metal, so copper conducts electricity. Both are valid.', wrong = '{"1": "This starts with ''some'', so its form is different, and the conclusion does not follow from it.", "2": "This goes from the B group back to A, an invalid form. Frogs living in water does not make them fish.", "3": "This uses ''no'' and then concludes about a third group (mammals), a different form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0226' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The original argument affirms the consequent: it assumes that because the pet has whiskers, it must be a cat. The prize argument makes the same error: Hina got a prize, so she must have topped the class. In both, the result could come from other causes.', wrong = '{"0": "This affirms the antecedent: it rained, so the road is wet. That form is valid.", "2": "This denies the consequent: the bank did not close early, so it is not Friday. That form is valid.", "3": "This is a valid ''all A are B; X is A; so X is B'' argument."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0227' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A factual statement can be checked against evidence. Population can be counted in a census, and Karachi''s population is far larger than Quetta''s. The other statements depend on personal taste, so they are opinions.', wrong = '{"0": "''Most exciting'' is a personal opinion that cannot be proved.", "1": "''Most pleasant weather'' depends on what each person likes.", "3": "''Better'' is a value judgement that depends on personal preferences."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0228' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The argument links morning exams to better performance through the idea that students are fresh. That only works if students really are fresher in the morning than in the afternoon. If they were not, the reason would not support the change.', wrong = '{"0": "Cost is not part of the argument, which is only about student performance.", "2": "The argument needs students to be fresher, not to like mornings. ''Every student'' is also far too strong.", "3": "The length of the exam is never mentioned in the argument."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0229' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Spraying parks can reduce dengue only if a large share of the mosquitoes that spread it live or breed in parks. The plan does not need parks to be the only source, just an important one. This is the assumption the plan depends on.', wrong = '{"0": "''Only in parks'' is too strong. The plan can still help if some mosquitoes breed elsewhere.", "1": "How crowded parks are is not linked to whether spraying them reduces dengue.", "3": "The plan is about reducing dengue, not about comparing costs."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0230' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The conclusion jumps from a 95% mock score to certain admission. That only works if the mock test score reliably predicts the real admission result. If mock scores were a poor guide, the conclusion would fail.', wrong = '{"0": "Her study hours are not needed for the argument, which relies only on the mock score.", "1": "The argument does not need the mock to be harder. It needs the mock to be a fair predictor.", "2": "This is far too strong and is not required for the argument."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0231' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The only reason given for appointing Dr Kamal is that he has published the most papers. That supports the recommendation only if the number of papers shows how well someone would run a lab. This is the hidden assumption.', wrong = '{"1": "Previous work at the lab is never mentioned, so the argument does not rely on it.", "2": "The argument says he has more papers, not that the others have none.", "3": "The cost of publishing papers is irrelevant to choosing the head of the lab."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0232' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A faster journey can save lives on the way only if the long journey was helping cause those deaths. If patients died on the way for reasons unrelated to time, halving the journey would not help. So the argument assumes long journeys contributed to deaths.', wrong = '{"0": "The road speeds up every vehicle, including ambulances and buses, so car ownership is not needed.", "2": "Better hospital services do not affect deaths that happen before patients arrive.", "3": "The cost of building the road is irrelevant to the argument."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0233' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The argument moves from doubled syrup sales to more people having coughs. That works only if the extra sales were not mainly caused by something else, such as a price cut or a nearby pharmacy closing. The argument needs those other explanations to be ruled out.', wrong = '{"0": "Whether syrup is the best treatment does not matter. People buy it for coughs either way.", "1": "The argument can work even if only some people use this pharmacy.", "3": "''Always'' is too strong and is not needed. The argument is only about this December."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0234' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A worry with the study is that better students may simply choose to read more. If both groups had the same vocabulary scores before the study, this worry is removed. The later gap is then more likely due to the daily reading, which strengthens the conclusion.', wrong = '{"0": "Letting readers choose their own books does not show that reading caused the vocabulary gain.", "1": "Disliking the test could affect both groups and says nothing about the cause.", "2": "Whether reading is a popular hobby is irrelevant to the study''s result."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0235' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If similar nearby hospitals without the rule saw no fall in infections, then shared causes such as the season or a weaker germ are less likely. The difference points to the hand-washing rule. This comparison most strengthens the claim.', wrong = '{"1": "A rule being popular with nurses does not show that it reduced infections.", "2": "Soap prices do not show whether the rule reduced infections.", "3": "Painting the wards is a rival explanation, so it weakens rather than strengthens the claim."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0236' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If streets in the same city without new trees showed no fall in temperature that summer, then a cooler summer cannot explain the change. The trees are then the most likely cause, which strengthens the claim.', wrong = '{"0": "Who planted the trees has nothing to do with whether they cooled the street.", "2": "Shade for birds says nothing about the temperature on the street.", "3": "Traffic on the road does not show whether the trees lowered temperatures."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0237' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Comparing girls from similar families, with and without the scholarship, isolates the effect of the scholarship. If those who received it were far more likely to finish school, the scholarship is the likely cause. This most strengthens the claim.', wrong = '{"0": "How often the scholarship is paid says nothing about its effect on finishing school.", "1": "New girls'' schools are a rival explanation for the rise, so this weakens the claim.", "3": "Boys'' results are not about the scholarship for girls."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0238' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If students at the same college who took in-person classes this year did not see lower results, then a harder exam or a weaker batch cannot explain the drop. The difference is linked to online classes, which strengthens the claim.', wrong = '{"0": "The cost of the platform has nothing to do with exam results.", "1": "Teachers'' preferences do not show what caused the lower results.", "2": "Exam length alone does not compare online and in-person students."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0239' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The argument credits the gym for fewer heart attacks. If the clinic began free blood-pressure checks and treatment for all adults at the same time, that is another strong cause of the fall, since high blood pressure is a major risk factor. So the gym may not be the reason.', wrong = '{"1": "The monthly fee does not tell us whether the gym caused the fall.", "2": "Opening hours do not offer another explanation for fewer heart attacks.", "3": "Heart attacks being serious is true but irrelevant to what caused the fall."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0240' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The argument assumes library time causes higher grades. If students who already have high grades are the ones who choose to study in the library, the link may run the other way. Good students go to the library, rather than the library making them good students.', wrong = '{"0": "Air-conditioning does not challenge the claim that library time raises grades.", "2": "The library''s opening time is irrelevant to the argument.", "3": "Some students reading at home does not explain the link between library time and grades."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0241' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The survey only asked people visiting hospitals, who are much more likely to be ill than people in general. This sample is biased, so its 80% figure cannot be applied to the whole city. This most weakens the conclusion.', wrong = '{"0": "The season alone does not make the sample unrepresentative of the city.", "1": "The number of questions does not change who was surveyed.", "3": "Private or public, everyone surveyed was still a hospital visitor."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0242' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The claim uses a 70% recovery rate as proof the medicine works. If about 70% of patients with this cough recover within a week without any medicine, then the medicine shows no benefit over doing nothing. This most weakens the claim.', wrong = '{"0": "A pleasant taste does not show whether the medicine works.", "1": "How often some patients took it does not weaken the claim that it works.", "2": "Where the medicine is made is irrelevant to whether it works."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0243' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The argument credits the new method for scores 15% higher than last year''s class. If this year''s board exam was much easier, that alone could explain the higher scores. So the new method may not be better at all.', wrong = '{"1": "Using more group work describes the method; it does not offer another cause for the rise.", "2": "Class size does not explain why scores rose compared with last year.", "3": "Teachers needing training does not explain the higher marks."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0244' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If the highway was closed for repairs for most of the year, far fewer vehicles used it. Fewer vehicles means fewer accidents anyway, so the fall may have nothing to do with the cameras. This most weakens the argument.', wrong = '{"0": "Where the cameras were made has nothing to do with whether they reduced accidents.", "2": "Drivers disliking cameras does not explain why accidents fell.", "3": "This is a general fact and does not offer another cause for this year''s fall."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0245' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ahmed''s honesty is the conclusion, yet the only evidence offered for it is Ahmed''s own word, which can be trusted only if he is already honest. The argument assumes what it sets out to prove, so it is circular reasoning, also called begging the question.', wrong = '{"0": "Attacking the author (ad hominem) means criticising a person instead of the argument. Here nobody is criticised; Ahmed is actually being defended.", "1": "Hasty generalisation draws a broad rule from a few cases. No examples or cases are given here, only Ahmed''s own claim about himself.", "3": "A false dilemma pretends only two choices exist. The argument does not offer any choice between options."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0246' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Whether research is correct depends on its evidence and methods, not on the habits of the person who did it. Rejecting Dr Rehan''s findings because he once smoked attacks the man instead of his evidence, which is the ad hominem fallacy.', wrong = '{"0": "Circular reasoning uses the conclusion as its own proof. This argument does not assume the research is wrong in order to show it is wrong.", "1": "A slippery slope predicts a chain of worse and worse events from one step. No such chain of events is described here.", "2": "An appeal to popularity says something is true because most people believe it. The argument says nothing about what most people think."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0247' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A false dilemma presents only two choices when others exist. The canteen does not have to close completely, nor must students eat unhealthy food forever: it could offer healthier food or change its menu. Leaving out these middle options is the flaw.', wrong = '{"1": "An ad hominem attack targets a person. No canteen owner or any other person is criticised in the statement.", "2": "An appeal to authority relies on someone famous or expert. No person''s opinion is quoted here.", "3": "Circular reasoning assumes its own conclusion as proof. The statement does not do this; it wrongly limits the choices to two."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0248' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Being famous in one field does not make a person an expert in another. A cricketer has no medical training, so his word cannot show that a powder cures diabetes; that needs clinical evidence. Relying on him is an appeal to an irrelevant authority.', wrong = '{"0": "Confusing cause and effect means getting the direction of a cause backwards. No such reversed pair appears in the statement.", "2": "Generalising from one patient would need a single case result to be mentioned. No patient or result is described, only the cricketer''s opinion.", "3": "An attack on character would criticise the cricketer. Here he is trusted and praised, not attacked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0249' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The argument moves from the parts (each player is excellent) to the whole (the team is excellent). This is the fallacy of composition. A team also depends on teamwork, so excellent individuals can still form a poor team if they do not play well together.', wrong = '{"0": "Assuming that what is true of the whole is true of each part is the reverse error, called division. This argument goes from parts to whole.", "1": "An appeal to authority quotes an expert or famous person. No authority is used in this argument.", "3": "Reversing cause and effect requires a causal claim. The argument makes no claim about what causes what."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0250' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The argument assumes that because good marks came after wearing the shoes, the shoes caused them. This is the post hoc error, meaning ''after this, therefore because of this''. Preparation and the paper itself explain marks; one coincidence proves nothing.', wrong = '{"0": "An ad hominem attack criticises a person. The shoemaker, or anyone else, is not criticised at all.", "1": "A false dilemma offers only two choices. No choice between options is presented in this reasoning.", "2": "An appeal to popularity relies on what most people believe. The speaker relies on one personal experience, not on popular opinion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0251' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only the 240 users who said the tests helped can be counted as saying so. Half of 500 is 250, and 240 is less than that, so fewer than half of all candidates said the tests helped. As a percentage, 240 ÷ 500 = 48%.', wrong = '{"1": "The survey records what candidates said, not their actual scores. It cannot prove the tests improve MDCAT scores.", "2": "300 − 240 = 60 users did not say the tests helped, but some may have been unsure or given no answer. It is not certain all 60 said the tests did not help.", "3": "Most would mean more than 250. Only 240 out of 500, which is 48%, said the tests helped."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0252' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rule is: if a ward has air-conditioning, it is on an upper floor. Ward 7 is on the ground floor, not an upper floor, so it cannot be one of the air-conditioned wards. Therefore Ward 7 does not have air-conditioning.', wrong = '{"0": "The statements say nothing about which patients Ward 7 serves, so its type cannot be inferred.", "2": "This reverses the rule. All air-conditioned wards are upstairs, but some upstairs wards may still have no air-conditioning.", "3": "Nothing is said about future plans, so this cannot be concluded."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0253' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Medicine X helps most patients but carries a risk of dizziness. Giving it only to patients with very high blood pressure suggests doctors use it where the benefit is greatest and judge that it outweighs the risk. This is the most reasonable inference.', wrong = '{"0": "The passage does not say the medicine fails in mild cases. Doctors may simply feel the dizziness risk is not worth it when the problem is mild.", "1": "The passage says the medicine causes the dizziness, not the high blood pressure.", "3": "The passage says dizziness happens in only some patients, not in every patient."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0254' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first rule says: score above 180, then interview. Zain was not offered an interview, so by the contrapositive he cannot have scored above 180. His documents were submitted, so they do not explain the outcome, but the score rule is enough.', wrong = '{"0": "Zain''s score could be any mark of 180 or below. Nothing fixes it at exactly 180.", "1": "We are told only that he submitted his documents. Nothing says they were incomplete.", "2": "Zain submitted his documents and was not interviewed, so this statement is false."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0255' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Before the hospital opened, many illnesses went unseen by any doctor and were never recorded. Once a hospital is close by, more sick people come in and are diagnosed and counted. The recorded number rises even if people are no sicker than before.', wrong = '{"1": "Who built the hospital has no link to how many illnesses are recorded.", "2": "The farmers lived there before and after the hospital opened, so their work cannot explain the change.", "3": "The size of the car park has nothing to do with the number of recorded illnesses."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0256' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Waiting time depends on the number of patients compared with the number of doctors. If news of the extra doctors drew many more patients from nearby towns, patient numbers could rise faster than doctor numbers. Then waits grow longer despite the new hires.', wrong = '{"0": "Well qualified doctors would, if anything, shorten waits. This deepens the puzzle instead of explaining it.", "2": "Repainting the waiting room has no effect on how long patients wait.", "3": "A preference for mornings could have existed before the new doctors came, so it does not explain why waits became longer."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0257' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Income = price × number sold. If the price is halved, income rises only if the number sold more than doubles. Many more patients being able to afford the medicine gives exactly that, so total income goes up. For example, half price × 3 times the sales = 1.5 times the income.', wrong = '{"0": "Income from other medicines is separate. It cannot explain a rise in income from this one medicine.", "1": "A packaging change alone does not explain why income rose after the price was cut.", "3": "Higher raw material costs would reduce profit. They do not explain a rise in income from sales."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0258' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Necessary'' means nobody can sit the exam without a valid ID card, so everyone who sits it must have one. ''Not sufficient'' means the card alone is not enough, as other things may also be required. Only the statement about everyone who sits having a card must be true.', wrong = '{"0": "If anyone with an ID card could sit the exam, the card would be sufficient. The statement says it is not sufficient.", "1": "This contradicts ''necessary''. Without a valid ID card nobody can sit the exam.", "2": "''Not sufficient'' means something else is also required, so the card cannot be the only document needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0259' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A sufficient condition guarantees the result: whenever it rains, the roads become wet. So rain with dry roads is impossible. The reverse is not claimed, so wet roads without rain can still happen, for example from a burst water pipe.', wrong = '{"1": "Rain is sufficient but not necessary for wet roads. Something else, such as a burst pipe or street washing, could wet them.", "2": "No rain and dry roads is perfectly possible; the statement says nothing against it.", "3": "Rain followed by wet roads is exactly what the statement predicts, so it is certainly possible."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0260' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To show the well water caused the outbreak, the evidence must link illness to drinking that water. Families who boiled their water, which kills germs, had far fewer cases than those who drank it straight. This comparison points directly at the untreated water as the cause.', wrong = '{"0": "Many villagers being ill shows there was an outbreak, but says nothing about what caused it.", "2": "Water being essential for health is true, but it does not connect the well to the diarrhoea.", "3": "The season tells us when the outbreak happened, not that the well water caused it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0261' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A large controlled trial compares people who got the vaccine with a similar group who did not, so differences can be linked to the vaccine. Independent review before publication means other experts checked the methods and results. This makes it the most reliable evidence of safety.', wrong = '{"0": "Personal stories are few, unchecked and not compared with any control group, so they are weak evidence.", "1": "The maker profits from sales, so its advertisement is biased and is not independent evidence.", "3": "Popularity on social media shows how many people shared a post, not whether its claim is true."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0262' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The figure ''9 out of 10 dentists'' sounds strong, but its meaning depends on hidden details. The dentists may have been chosen to favour Brand Z, or asked only whether Brand Z is acceptable rather than compared with other brands. Without this, the survey cannot show Brand Z is the best.', wrong = '{"0": "The number of flavours has no bearing on whether the survey supports the claim of ''best''.", "1": "Whether some people visit dentists does not affect how reliable the dentist survey is.", "2": "Dentists being busy does not weaken the survey''s result or its link to the claim."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0263' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The plan''s goal is to end traffic jams. If widened roads usually attract more cars, so that jams return to the same level within a few years, then extra lanes will not achieve that goal. This attacks the plan at its core, so it weakens it most.', wrong = '{"1": "Construction jobs are a side benefit. They say nothing about whether the jams will end.", "2": "The age of the road does not show whether widening it would end the jams.", "3": "Extra road markings are a small cost, not a reason the plan would fail to end jams."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0264' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a geometric progression each term is multiplied by the same number, the common ratio. Here 1.5 ÷ 0.5 = 3, 4.5 ÷ 1.5 = 3 and 13.5 ÷ 4.5 = 3, so the ratio is 3. The next term is 13.5 × 3 = 40.5.', wrong = '{"1": "27 is 13.5 × 2. The common ratio of the series is 3, not 2.", "2": "39 adds 25.5 to 13.5, but the series grows by multiplying by 3, not by adding.", "3": "40 is close but not exact. 13.5 × 3 = 40.5, and the series uses exact multiplication."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0265' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The ratio between terms is constant: 48 ÷ 64 = 3/4, 36 ÷ 48 = 3/4 and 27 ÷ 36 = 3/4. So each term is three-quarters of the previous one, and the next term is 27 × 3/4 = 81/4 = 20.25.', wrong = '{"0": "18 comes from subtracting 9 again. The differences 16, 12, 9 are not equal; they also shrink by 3/4, so the next one is 6.75.", "2": "21 subtracts 6 from 27, which follows no consistent rule. The terms are multiplied by 3/4 each time.", "3": "20 is a rounded value. 27 × 0.75 is exactly 20.25, and the series has no rounding."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0266' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The nth term of a geometric progression is a × rⁿ⁻¹, where a is the first term and r is the common ratio. Here a = 3 and r = 6 ÷ 3 = 2. So the 8th term = 3 × 2⁷ = 3 × 128 = 384.', wrong = '{"0": "192 = 3 × 2⁶ is the 7th term, one step short of the 8th.", "1": "768 = 3 × 2⁸ is the 9th term. It uses the power n instead of n − 1.", "3": "48 = 3 × 2⁴ is only the 5th term of the series."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0267' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The 5th term is the 2nd term multiplied by r three times, so r³ = 162 ÷ 6 = 27 and r = 3. The 1st term is the 2nd term divided by r: 6 ÷ 3 = 2. Check: 2, 6, 18, 54, 162.', wrong = '{"0": "3 is the common ratio r, not the first term.", "1": "A first term of 1 with ratio 3 gives 1, 3, 9, 27, 81. The 2nd term would be 3 and the 5th 81, not 6 and 162.", "2": "6 is the 2nd term given in the question, not the 1st."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0268' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The series has first term 1 and ratio 3, so the first six terms are 1, 3, 9, 27, 81, 243. Adding them gives 364. The formula agrees: sum = (rⁿ − 1) ÷ (r − 1) = (3⁶ − 1) ÷ 2 = 728 ÷ 2 = 364.', wrong = '{"1": "243 is the 6th term on its own, not the sum of six terms.", "2": "121 = 1 + 3 + 9 + 27 + 81 is the sum of only the first five terms.", "3": "1093 = (3⁷ − 1) ÷ 2 is the sum of the first seven terms, one too many."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0269' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First replace each sign: − becomes ÷, + becomes ×, × becomes − and ÷ becomes +. The expression becomes 16 ÷ 4 × 3 − 2 + 5. By BODMAS, 16 ÷ 4 = 4 and 4 × 3 = 12, then 12 − 2 + 5 = 15.', wrong = '{"0": "5 comes from 12 − 2 − 5. It treats the last ÷ as minus, but ÷ means +.", "2": "19 comes from 12 + 2 + 5. It adds the 2, but × means −, so 2 must be subtracted.", "3": "10 forgets to change + into ×, giving 16 ÷ 4 + 3 − 2 + 5 = 4 + 3 − 2 + 5 = 10."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0270' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Replace the letters with signs: S is ÷, P is +, R is × and Q is −. The expression becomes 36 ÷ 4 + 5 × 3 − 8. By BODMAS, division and multiplication come before addition and subtraction: 36 ÷ 4 = 9 and 5 × 3 = 15, then 9 + 15 − 8 = 16.', wrong = '{"0": "34 works strictly left to right without BODMAS: 36 ÷ 4 = 9, 9 + 5 = 14, 14 × 3 = 42, 42 − 8 = 34.", "1": "19 comes from 9 + 15 − 5, subtracting 5 instead of 8.", "3": "32 comes from 9 + 15 + 8, adding 8 although Q means minus."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0271' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Apply both swaps to each option and test it. For 4 × 6 + 2 = 14, swapping + and × gives 4 + 6 × 2, and swapping 4 and 6 gives 6 + 4 × 2. By BODMAS, 6 + 8 = 14, so the equation is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "After both swaps it becomes 4 × 6 + 2 = 14. The left side is 24 + 2 = 26, not 14.", "1": "After both swaps it becomes 4 + 6 × 2 = 20. The left side is 4 + 12 = 16, not 20.", "2": "After both swaps it becomes 6 × 4 + 2 = 16. The left side is 24 + 2 = 26, not 16."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0272' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Test a rule on the complete rows: 3² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25 and 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169. So the third number is the sum of the squares of the first two. For row 3: 8² + 15² = 64 + 225 = 289.', wrong = '{"1": "23 is 8 + 15. Simple addition fails row 1, where 3 + 4 = 7, not 25.", "2": "120 is 8 × 15. Multiplying fails row 1, where 3 × 4 = 12, not 25.", "3": "256 is 16², but the rule needs 8² + 15² = 289. 256 would need 8² + 15² to equal 256, which it does not."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0273' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Test row 1: 9² − 4² = 81 − 16 = 65. Row 2: 6² − 1² = 36 − 1 = 35. So the third number is the difference of the squares of the first two. Row 3: 7² − 5² = 49 − 25 = 24.', wrong = '{"0": "12 is 7 + 5. Simple addition fails row 1, where 9 + 4 = 13, not 65.", "2": "74 is 7² + 5², a sum of squares. The rule uses the difference of squares.", "3": "35 is 7 × 5. Multiplying fails row 1, where 9 × 4 = 36, not 65."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0274' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Number the elements from the left: K 1, 4 2, @ 3, M 4, 7 5, # 6, 2 7, P 8, $ 9, 9 10. So the 10th element is 9. Counting three places to its right gives R (1st), % (2nd) and 5 (3rd).', wrong = '{"0": "% is only 2nd to the right of 9, one place short.", "1": "& is 4th to the right of 9, one place too far.", "3": "R is the element immediately to the right of 9, so it is only 1st to the right."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0275' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Check each number. 4 follows K and 7 follows M (letters, so they fail). 2 has # before it and P after it, and 9 has $ before it and R after it, so both qualify. 5 is followed by & and 3 follows T. Only two numbers, 2 and 9, meet both conditions.', wrong = '{"0": "1 misses one of the two valid cases, either # 2 P or $ 9 R.", "1": "3 wrongly includes % 5 &. The 5 has a symbol before it, but it is followed by a symbol, not a letter.", "2": "4 counts numbers that fail a condition, such as 4 and 7, which are preceded by letters."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0276' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Split the series into groups: *, # #, * * *, # # # #. The groups switch between stars and hashes and grow by one symbol each time. The 4th group of four hashes is complete, so the next group is five stars, and the next symbol is *.', wrong = '{"1": "# would make the hash group five long, but that group already has its full four symbols.", "2": "The question asks for the next single symbol. Two stars also goes beyond one position.", "3": "The next group is stars only, so no # comes next. The answer is a single star."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0277' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Written in reverse the alphabet is Z, Y, X, W, V, U, T, S, R, Q, P, O, … so the 12th letter from the left is O. Seven places to its right is the 19th letter: N, M, L, K, J, I, H. The answer is H.', wrong = '{"0": "V is 7 places to the left of O, not the right. It counts in the wrong direction.", "2": "G is the 20th letter, which is 8 places to the right of O, one too many.", "3": "I is the 18th letter, only 6 places to the right of O."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0278' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is double the previous one plus a number that grows by 1: 3 × 2 + 1 = 7, 7 × 2 + 2 = 16, 16 × 2 + 3 = 35, 35 × 2 + 4 = 74. The next step adds 5: 74 × 2 + 5 = 148 + 5 = 153.', wrong = '{"0": "148 just doubles 74 and leaves out the added number, which should be 5.", "1": "149 is 74 × 2 + 1. It adds 1 each time, but the added number grows by 1 at every step.", "3": "150 is 74 × 2 + 2, repeating the second step''s addition instead of using 5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0279' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Find the differences: 2 − 1 = 1, 6 − 2 = 4, 15 − 6 = 9, 31 − 15 = 16, 56 − 31 = 25. These are the squares 1², 2², 3², 4², 5². The next difference is 6² = 36, so the next term is 56 + 36 = 92.', wrong = '{"0": "87 is 56 + 31. It adds the previous term instead of the next square, 36.", "1": "81 is 56 + 25, repeating the last difference instead of moving on to 36.", "2": "97 is 56 + 41. It treats the differences as if they rose by a fixed amount, but they are squares."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0280' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Compare each term with the squares 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49: the terms are 1 + 1, 4 − 1, 9 + 1, 16 − 1, 25 + 1, 36 − 1, 49 + 1. So odd positions add 1 and even positions subtract 1. The 8th term is 8² − 1 = 63.', wrong = '{"1": "65 is 8² + 1. Position 8 is even, so 1 is subtracted, not added.", "2": "64 is just 8², leaving out the −1 for even positions.", "3": "61 is 50 + 11, repeating an earlier difference. The differences (1, 7, 5, 11, 9, 15, 13) do not repeat."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0281' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is one less than a perfect cube: 1³ − 1 = 0, 2³ − 1 = 7, 3³ − 1 = 26, 4³ − 1 = 63, 5³ − 1 = 124. Following the pattern, the next term is 6³ − 1 = 216 − 1 = 215. A quick check: the differences 7, 19, 37, 61 grow by 12, 18, 24, so the next difference is 91, and 124 + 91 = 215.', wrong = '{"0": "217 is 6³ + 1. The series subtracts 1 from each cube.", "2": "185 is 124 + 61, repeating the last difference. The differences 7, 19, 37, 61 keep growing, so the next is 91.", "3": "199 is 124 + 75. The differences grow by 12, 18, 24, so the next gap is 61 + 30 = 91, not 75."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0282' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is n³ + n², which can be written n² × (n + 1): 1 × 2 = 2, 4 × 3 = 12, 9 × 4 = 36, 16 × 5 = 80, 25 × 6 = 150. For the 6th term, n = 6, so the answer is 36 × 7 = 252. Check: 6³ + 6² = 216 + 36 = 252.', wrong = '{"0": "240 is 150 + 90. The differences 10, 24, 44, 70 grow by 14, 20, 26, so the next is 102, giving 252.", "1": "216 is 6³ alone. It leaves out the 6² = 36 part of n³ + n².", "3": "230 is 150 + 80, guessing the differences grow by 10. The real next difference is 102."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0283' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two series are interleaved. The 1st, 3rd, 5th and 7th terms are 3, 6, 12, 24, doubling each time. The 2nd, 4th, 6th and 8th terms are 20, 17, 14, 11, falling by 3. The 9th term belongs to the doubling series: 24 × 2 = 48.', wrong = '{"0": "8 continues the falling series (11 − 3), but the 9th position belongs to the doubling series.", "1": "36 is 24 + 12. The odd-position terms double, they do not grow by adding 12.", "2": "27 is 24 + 3, which mixes the +3 idea from the other series with the wrong sign and wrong series."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0284' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is one less than a square, with the squares falling: 11² − 1 = 120, 10² − 1 = 99, 9² − 1 = 80, 8² − 1 = 63, 7² − 1 = 48. The next is 6² − 1 = 35. Check: the differences 21, 19, 17, 15 fall by 2, so the next is 13, and 48 − 13 = 35.', wrong = '{"1": "33 is 48 − 15, repeating the last difference. The differences fall by 2, so the next is 13.", "2": "36 is 6² alone, forgetting to subtract 1 as every term does.", "3": "38 is 48 − 10, which follows no rule in the series."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0285' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is a whole number raised to its own power: 1¹ = 1, 2² = 4, 3³ = 27, 4⁴ = 256. The next number is 5, so the next term is 5⁵ = 5 × 5 × 5 × 5 × 5 = 3125. Note how fast such a series grows: each term is far larger than the one before.', wrong = '{"0": "625 is 5⁴. The power must equal the base, so the term is 5⁵.", "2": "1024 is 4⁵ (also 2¹⁰). It raises the wrong base to the power 5.", "3": "1250 is 2 × 5⁴, which does not fit the pattern of n raised to the power n."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0286' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is double the previous one minus 1: 4 × 2 − 1 = 7, 7 × 2 − 1 = 13, 13 × 2 − 1 = 25, 25 × 2 − 1 = 49. So the next is 49 × 2 − 1 = 97. Check: the differences 3, 6, 12, 24 double each time, so the next is 48, and 49 + 48 = 97.', wrong = '{"0": "73 is 49 + 24, repeating the last difference instead of doubling it to 48.", "1": "98 doubles 49 but forgets to subtract 1.", "3": "96 is 49 × 2 − 2, subtracting 2 instead of 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0287' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The terms are 1², 2³, 3², 4³, 5², 6³: odd positions hold the square of the position number and even positions hold its cube. The 7th position is odd, so the term is 7² = 49.', wrong = '{"0": "343 is 7³. Position 7 is odd, so it takes a square, not a cube.", "1": "36 is 6². Position 6 already used 6³ = 216, and the next number in the pattern is 7.", "2": "512 is 8³, which would be the 8th term, not the 7th."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0288' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is the product of three consecutive whole numbers: 1 × 2 × 3 = 6, 2 × 3 × 4 = 24, 3 × 4 × 5 = 60, 4 × 5 × 6 = 120, 5 × 6 × 7 = 210. The next starts from 6, so it is 6 × 7 × 8 = 336. Check: the differences 18, 36, 60, 90 lead to a next difference of 126, and 210 + 126 = 336.', wrong = '{"1": "300 is 210 + 90, repeating the last difference. The differences 18, 36, 60, 90 keep growing, so the next is 126.", "2": "330 is 210 + 120, guessing the differences rise by 30. The next difference is actually 126.", "3": "320 is 210 + 110, which comes from no consistent rule in the series."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0289' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From the third term on, each term is the sum of the two terms just before it, like the Fibonacci series: 1 + 3 = 4, 3 + 4 = 7, 4 + 7 = 11, 7 + 11 = 18. So the next term is 11 + 18 = 29.', wrong = '{"0": "25 is 18 + 7, adding the term from two places back instead of the one just before 18.", "2": "27 is 18 + 9, a guess at the differences rather than the sum rule.", "3": "36 doubles 18, but each term is the sum of the previous two, not double the last one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0290' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is double the previous one plus 1, which gives numbers of the form 2ⁿ − 1: 1, 3, 7, 15, 31, 63, 127, 255. The given 125 should be 63 × 2 + 1 = 127, so 125 is the wrong number.', wrong = '{"0": "63 = 31 × 2 + 1, so it fits the doubling-plus-one rule.", "1": "255 = 127 × 2 + 1, so it fits once 125 is corrected to 127.", "3": "31 = 15 × 2 + 1, so it fits the doubling-plus-one rule."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0291' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is 4 times the one before, a geometric series with ratio 4: 3 × 4 = 12, 12 × 4 = 48, 48 × 4 = 192, 192 × 4 = 768. The fourth term should be 192, so 190 is the number that does not fit. Note that 768 ÷ 4 = 192 confirms the correction.', wrong = '{"0": "48 = 12 × 4, so it follows the ratio of 4.", "1": "768 = 192 × 4, so it fits once 190 is corrected to 192.", "2": "12 = 3 × 4, so it follows the ratio of 4."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0292' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The terms are squares of the prime numbers in order: 2² = 4, 3² = 9, 5² = 25, 7² = 49, 11² = 121. A prime number has exactly two factors, 1 and itself. The next prime after 11 is 13, so the next term is 13² = 169.', wrong = '{"1": "144 = 12², but 12 is not a prime number.", "2": "225 = 15², but 15 is not prime, and the series would skip the prime 13.", "3": "196 = 14², but 14 is not a prime number."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0293' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Write the letters as positions: B = 2, E = 5, J = 10, Q = 17. The gaps are 3, 5, 7, rising by 2 (the positions are n² + 1). The next gap is 9, so 17 + 9 = 26, which is Z.', wrong = '{"0": "X (24) is Q + 7, repeating the last gap instead of increasing it to 9.", "2": "Y (25) is Q + 8, one short of the correct gap of 9.", "3": "W (23) is Q + 6, which gives a smaller gap than the last one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0294' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First letters: A, C, F, J move forward by 2, 3, 4, so the next move is 5: J + 5 = O. Second letters: Z, X, U, Q move back by 2, 3, 4, so the next move is 5: Q − 5 = L. The answer is OL.', wrong = '{"0": "N is J + 4, repeating the last step for the first letter instead of moving 5.", "1": "M is Q − 4, repeating the last step for the second letter instead of moving back 5.", "3": "P and K move both letters by 6, one step too far."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0295' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Write each letter as its place in the alphabet: Z 26, W 23, S 19, P 16, L 12, I 9. The steps back are 3, 4, 3, 4, 3, so they alternate between 3 and 4. The next step is 4, so 9 − 4 = 5, which is E.', wrong = '{"0": "F (6) is I − 3, repeating the step of 3. The steps alternate, so 4 comes next.", "1": "D (4) is I − 5, a step that never appears in the series.", "2": "G (7) is I − 2, a step that never appears in the series."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0296' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term has three parts. The first letter moves forward by 2 (A, C, E, G, I), the number rises by 2 (1, 3, 5, 7, 9) and the last letter moves back by 2 (Z, X, V, T, R). So the next term is I9R. The number also equals the first letter''s position.', wrong = '{"1": "H is G + 1 and S is T − 1. Both letters should move by 2, giving I and R.", "2": "The number rises by 2 each time, so after 7 comes 9, not 8.", "3": "S is T − 1. The last letter moves back by 2, so it should be R."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0297' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Shift each letter of MEDICINE one place forward to get NFEJDJOF, then write it backwards: FOJDJEFN, which matches the code. Doing the same to DOCTOR gives EPDUPS, and reversed this is SPUDPE.', wrong = '{"0": "EPDUPS shifts each letter forward by one but forgets to reverse the word.", "2": "SPUDPF has the wrong last letter. It comes from the first letter D, and D + 1 = E, not F.", "3": "QNSBNC shifts each letter one place backward before reversing, but the code shifts forward."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0298' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a mirror code each pair of positions adds up to 27, since A (1) pairs with Z (26). So H (8) → S (19), E (5) → V (22), A (1) → Z (26), R (18) → I (9) and T (20) → G (7). HEART is written SVZIG.', wrong = '{"0": "SVZIH ends in H, but T (20) pairs with 27 − 20 = 7, which is G.", "1": "IFBSU shifts each letter of HEART forward by one, which is a different code.", "3": "TVZIG starts with T, but H (8) pairs with 27 − 8 = 19, which is S."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0299' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Test the rule (sum of letter positions) × (number of letters). CAT: (3 + 1 + 20) × 3 = 24 × 3 = 72. DOG: (4 + 15 + 7) × 3 = 26 × 3 = 78. Both fit. BOOK: (2 + 15 + 15 + 11) × 4 = 43 × 4 = 172.', wrong = '{"0": "43 is only the sum of the letter positions in BOOK, without multiplying by the 4 letters.", "1": "129 is 43 × 3. It multiplies by 3, but BOOK has 4 letters.", "2": "176 is 44 × 4. The letter sum of BOOK is 43, not 44."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0300' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Compare positions: A (1) → F (6), C (3) → H (8), E (5) → J (10). Each letter moves 5 places forward. Applying the same shift to KMO: K → P, M → R, O → T, giving PRT.', wrong = '{"1": "LNP moves each letter of KMO forward by only 1 instead of 5.", "2": "QSU moves each letter forward by 6, one place too far.", "3": "PQR moves K by 5 but then M and O by only 4 and 3, so the shift is not the same for each letter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0301' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Find a rule that turns 5 into 124. Since 5³ = 125 and 125 − 1 = 124, the rule is: cube the number, then subtract 1 (n³ − 1). Applying the same rule to 3 gives 3³ − 1 = 27 − 1 = 26.', wrong = '{"0": "24 is 3³ − 3. That rule (n³ − n) gives 120 for 5, not 124.", "2": "27 is 3³, forgetting to subtract the 1 that the rule requires.", "3": "8 is 3² − 1, using a square instead of a cube. For 5 that rule gives 24, not 124."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0302' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '121 = 11², 169 = 13², 289 = 17² and 361 = 19², so each is the square of a prime number (a number whose only factors are 1 and itself). 225 = 15², and 15 = 3 × 5 is not prime. So 225 is the odd one out.', wrong = '{"0": "121 = 11², and 11 is prime, so it fits the group.", "1": "289 = 17², and 17 is prime, so it fits the group.", "3": "361 = 19², and 19 is prime, so it fits the group."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0303' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In BDF, HJL, NPR and TVX the letters are evenly spaced, each 2 places apart with one letter skipped (for example B, skip C, D, skip E, F). In MOR, M (13) to O (15) is 2 places but O (15) to R (18) is 3 places, so it breaks the pattern.', wrong = '{"0": "B, D, F are 2 places apart each time, so BDF fits.", "1": "N, P, R are 2 places apart each time, so NPR fits.", "2": "T, V, X are 2 places apart each time, so TVX fits."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0304' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '27 = 3³, 64 = 4³, 125 = 5³ and 343 = 7³ are perfect cubes. 196 = 14² is a perfect square, but it is not a cube, since 5³ = 125 and 6³ = 216. So 196 is the odd one out.', wrong = '{"1": "27 = 3 × 3 × 3 = 3³, so it is a perfect cube like the others.", "2": "125 = 5 × 5 × 5 = 5³, so it is a perfect cube like the others.", "3": "343 = 7 × 7 × 7 = 7³, so it is a perfect cube like the others."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0305' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the complete columns the bottom number is the product of the three above: 4 × 5 × 3 = 60 and 2 × 7 × 4 = 56. So 6 × 3 × ? = 54, which gives 18 × ? = 54 and ? = 54 ÷ 18 = 3.', wrong = '{"0": "With 2, the product is 6 × 3 × 2 = 36, not 54.", "2": "With 4, the product is 6 × 3 × 4 = 72, not 54.", "3": "9 is 54 ÷ 6, dividing out only one known number. With 9 the product is 6 × 3 × 9 = 162."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0306' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Test the known pairs: 2² + 1 = 5, 3² + 1 = 10, 4² + 1 = 17 and 9² + 1 = 82. Every pair fits the same rule: square the input, then add 1. For the input 6: 6² + 1 = 36 + 1 = 37.', wrong = '{"0": "36 is 6² alone, forgetting to add the 1 that every pair uses.", "1": "26 is 5² + 1, applying the rule to 5 instead of 6.", "3": "35 is 6² − 1, subtracting 1 instead of adding it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0307' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The 5th letter from the left is E, at position 5. The 9th letter from the right is at position 26 − 9 + 1 = 18, which is R. The letters strictly between them are positions 6 to 17, so the count is 17 − 6 + 1 = 12.', wrong = '{"0": "13 comes from 18 − 5, which counts one of the end letters as well as the letters between.", "1": "11 leaves out one of the letters in the gap from F to Q.", "2": "14 counts both end letters, E and R, along with the 12 letters between them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0308' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For each pair, compare the gap in the word with the gap in the alphabet. O and M are 2 places apart in FORMULATE (R between them) and 2 places apart in the alphabet (N between them), so they form a pair. Checking every other pair finds no match, so the answer is 1.', wrong = '{"1": "Only O and M have matching gaps. No second pair of letters in FORMULATE has the same gap in the word and the alphabet.", "2": "O and M do fit: one letter between them in the word (R) and one in the alphabet (N).", "3": "Three pairs is too many. Checking all pairs, only O and M match."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0309' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is the previous one multiplied by 1/3: (1/2) × (1/3) = 1/6 and (1/6) × (1/3) = 1/18. In other words, the denominators 2, 6, 18 are multiplied by 3. The next term is 1/(18 × 3) = 1/54.', wrong = '{"0": "1/36 doubles the denominator 18, but the denominators are multiplied by 3.", "2": "1/24 adds 6 to the denominator, but the denominators are multiplied, not added to.", "3": "1/72 multiplies the denominator by 4 instead of 3."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0310' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is three times the previous one minus 1: 1 × 3 − 1 = 2, 2 × 3 − 1 = 5, 5 × 3 − 1 = 14, 14 × 3 − 1 = 41. So the next term is 41 × 3 − 1 = 123 − 1 = 122. Check: the differences 1, 3, 9, 27 triple each time, so the next is 81, and 41 + 81 = 122.', wrong = '{"0": "123 is 41 × 3, forgetting to subtract 1.", "1": "82 doubles 41, but each step multiplies by 3.", "3": "121 is 41 × 3 − 2, subtracting 2 instead of 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0311' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is 1.5 times the previous one: 4 × 1.5 = 6, 6 × 1.5 = 9 and 9 × 1.5 = 13.5. So this is a geometric series with ratio 3/2, and the next term is 13.5 × 1.5 = 20.25. Check: the differences 2, 3, 4.5 also grow by 1.5 times, giving 6.75.', wrong = '{"0": "18 is 13.5 + 4.5, repeating the last difference. The differences 2, 3, 4.5 also grow by 1.5 times.", "1": "19.5 is 13.5 + 6, a guess at the differences. The next difference is 4.5 × 1.5 = 6.75.", "2": "27 doubles 13.5, but the ratio is 1.5, not 2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0312' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Find the differences between terms: 11 − 6 = 5, 21 − 11 = 10, 36 − 21 = 15 and 56 − 36 = 20. They rise by 5 each time, so the next difference is 25. The next term is 56 + 25 = 81.', wrong = '{"1": "76 is 56 + 20, repeating the last difference instead of increasing it to 25.", "2": "86 is 56 + 30, skipping the difference of 25.", "3": "71 is 56 + 15, a smaller gap than the last one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0313' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Write each letter as its place in the alphabet: C = 3, G = 7, L = 12, R = 18. The gaps are 4, 5, 6, growing by one each time. The next gap is 7, so 18 + 7 = 25, which is Y.', wrong = '{"0": "X (24) is R + 6, repeating the last gap instead of using 7.", "2": "Z (26) is R + 8, one place too far, since the next gap is 7.", "3": "W (23) is R + 5, a smaller gap than the last one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0314' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each term is 3 times a square: 3 × 1² = 3, 3 × 2² = 12, 3 × 3² = 27, 3 × 4² = 48, 3 × 5² = 75. The next is 3 × 6² = 108. Check: the differences 9, 15, 21, 27 rise by 6, so the next is 33, and 75 + 33 = 108.', wrong = '{"0": "102 is 75 + 27, repeating the last difference instead of 33.", "1": "105 is 75 + 30, using a difference that does not follow the +6 pattern.", "3": "111 is 75 + 36, adding 3 more than the next difference of 33."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0315' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In (2, 8), (3, 27) and (4, 64) the second number is the cube of the first: 2³ = 8, 3³ = 27, 4³ = 64. For the pair (5, 120), 5³ = 125, not 120, so it breaks the rule.', wrong = '{"0": "2³ = 2 × 2 × 2 = 8, so (2, 8) follows the rule.", "1": "3³ = 3 × 3 × 3 = 27, so (3, 27) follows the rule.", "2": "4³ = 4 × 4 × 4 = 64, so (4, 64) follows the rule."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0316' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ornithology is the scientific study of birds. In the same way, entomology is the scientific study of insects. Both names come from Greek words for the thing studied: ornis means bird and entomon means insect.', wrong = '{"1": "The study of word origins is etymology, a word easily confused with entomology.", "2": "Fossils are studied in palaeontology, not entomology.", "3": "Fish are studied in ichthyology, not entomology."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0317' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Statement I gives P > Q but says nothing about R, and statement II gives R > P but says nothing about Q. Together they give R > P > Q, so R is the tallest. Both statements are needed.', wrong = '{"0": "Statement I alone leaves R unplaced, so R could be taller or shorter than P.", "1": "Statement II alone leaves Q unplaced, so Q could be taller than R.", "3": "Neither statement alone fixes the order of all three people."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0318' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Statement I allows two primes between 20 and 30: 23 and 29. Statement II allows many numbers (1, 5, 9, 13, …, 29, …). Together, 29 ÷ 4 leaves remainder 1 while 23 ÷ 4 leaves remainder 3, so x = 29. Both are needed.', wrong = '{"0": "Statement I alone leaves two possible values, 23 and 29.", "1": "Statement II alone allows endless values such as 5, 9, 13 and 17.", "3": "Neither statement alone gives a single value of x."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0319' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The day after Wednesday is Thursday, and the day before Friday is also Thursday. So statement I alone answers the question, and statement II alone also answers it. Each statement alone is sufficient.', wrong = '{"0": "Statement I alone is enough, but so is statement II, which also gives Thursday.", "1": "Statement II alone is enough, but so is statement I, which also gives Thursday.", "2": "Neither statement needs the other, since each one gives Thursday by itself."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0320' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If Ali is 12th from the top and 18th from the bottom, the total is 12 + 18 − 1 = 29, because Ali is counted in both numbers. Statement II gives only a difference between boys and girls, not a total. So statement I alone is sufficient.', wrong = '{"1": "A difference of 5 fits many class sizes, such as 15 boys and 10 girls or 20 boys and 15 girls.", "2": "Statement I already gives the total on its own, so statement II is not needed.", "3": "Statement II alone cannot give the total, so ''each alone'' is wrong."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0321' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In statement II, ''clear'' appears in both phrases and ''te'' appears in both codes, so clear = te. Then in ''clear sky'' = ''po te'', sky must be po. Statement I alone has three words and three codes with no way to match them.', wrong = '{"0": "Statement I alone gives three words and three codes but no way to tell which code belongs to which word.", "2": "Statement II alone already fixes sky = po, so statement I is not needed.", "3": "Statement I alone is not enough, so ''each alone'' is wrong."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0322' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Statement II puts the park 3 km east of the house, and statement I puts the school 3 km north of the park. Together, the school is 3 km east and 3 km north of the house, which is north-east. Neither statement alone links the school to the house.', wrong = '{"0": "Statement I places the school relative to the park, but says nothing about where the house is.", "1": "Statement II places the park relative to the house, but says nothing about the school.", "3": "Neither statement alone connects the school with Hamza''s house."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0323' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The 10 people who speak both languages are counted in both the 40 and the 25, so the group size is 40 + 25 − 10 = 55. When 5 people who speak only English leave, 55 − 5 = 50 people remain.', wrong = '{"0": "55 is the size of the group before the 5 people leave.", "1": "60 comes from 40 + 25 − 5, forgetting to subtract the 10 people counted twice.", "3": "45 comes from 55 − 10, removing 10 people although only 5 left."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0324' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'G''s father is D. D is B''s son and E is C''s daughter, and B and C are both children of A, so they are siblings. Children of siblings are first cousins, so E is D''s cousin.', wrong = '{"0": "A sister would share D''s parents. E''s parent is C, while D''s parent is B.", "1": "An aunt is a parent''s sister, one generation above. E is in the same generation as D.", "2": "A niece is a sibling''s daughter, one generation below. E is in the same generation as D."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0325' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Start with P, Q, R, S, T, U. After P and U swap, the row is U, Q, R, S, T, P. The 2nd book from the right is T; moving it to the far left gives T, U, Q, R, S, P. The 4th book from the left is R.', wrong = '{"1": "S is 5th from the left in the final row T, U, Q, R, S, P.", "2": "Q is 3rd from the left in the final row.", "3": "U is 2nd from the left in the final row."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0326' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some night-shift nurses work in the emergency ward. Being night-shift nurses, they have first-aid training, and being in the emergency ward, they are not trainees. So at least these nurses are people with first-aid training who are not trainees.', wrong = '{"0": "Only the night-shift nurses are known to have first-aid training. Other emergency staff, such as day-shift doctors, may not.", "2": "Nothing is said about trainees outside the emergency ward. Some of them might have first-aid training.", "3": "Nothing links trainees to the night shift, so this cannot be concluded."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0327' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The library is closed at weekends, so the visit was on a weekday. On Mondays it closes at 2 pm, so at 4 pm it would already be shut. On Tuesday to Friday it is open until 5 pm, so Hamid''s visit must have been on one of those days.', wrong = '{"0": "On Monday the library closes at 2 pm, so nobody could be inside at 4 pm.", "1": "The library opens only on weekdays, so it is closed on Saturday.", "3": "One visit at 4 pm says nothing about how often Hamid goes to the library."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0328' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every member of the debating club plays chess, and Zara is a member of the debating club. So Zara must play chess. Whether she is among the half of chess players in the science society cannot be known from the statements.', wrong = '{"0": "Only half of the chess players are in the science society, and Zara may be in the other half.", "1": "The ''half'' describes chess players in general, not debaters. The debaters could all be in, or all be out of, the science society.", "2": "This reverses the first statement. All debaters play chess, but some chess players may not debate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0329' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some graduates are poets, and no poet is lazy, so those poet-graduates are not lazy. That makes conclusion II certain. Conclusion I does not follow: the graduates who are poets may or may not include any doctors.', wrong = '{"0": "Conclusion I is not certain, because the graduates who are poets need not include any doctor.", "2": "Only conclusion II is certain. Conclusion I is merely possible.", "3": "Conclusion II does follow: the graduates who are poets cannot be lazy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0330' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some boxes are bags, and no bag is a cup. So the boxes that are bags cannot be cups, which means some boxes are not cups (I follows). The boxes that are not bags could still be cups, so ''no box is a cup'' is not certain.', wrong = '{"1": "Conclusion II is not certain, because boxes that are not bags could be cups.", "2": "Conclusion II is only possible, not certain, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Conclusion I is certain, because the boxes that are bags cannot be cups."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0331' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All cats are pets and all pets are animals, so all cats are animals. Since cats exist, some animals are cats, so conclusion II follows. ''All animals are cats'' turns an ''all'' statement round, which is not allowed, so conclusion I does not follow.', wrong = '{"0": "''All cats are animals'' does not mean ''all animals are cats''. Dogs and birds are animals too.", "2": "Conclusion I does not follow, so ''both'' is wrong.", "3": "Conclusion II does follow, from turning ''all cats are animals'' into ''some animals are cats''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0332' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some keys are locks and all locks are doors, so those keys are doors. No door is a window, so those keys are not windows, and some keys are not windows (I). The same keys are doors, so some doors are keys (II). Both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "Conclusion II also follows, because the keys that are locks are also doors.", "1": "Conclusion I also follows, because the key-locks are doors and no door is a window.", "3": "Both conclusions follow through the keys that are locks."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0333' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All pencils are sticks and no stick is a ring, so no pencil is a ring. That is the same as saying no ring is a pencil, so conclusion I follows. Conclusion II says some rings are sticks, which directly contradicts ''no stick is a ring''.', wrong = '{"1": "Conclusion II is false: ''no stick is a ring'' also means no ring is a stick.", "2": "Conclusion II does not follow; it is actually ruled out.", "3": "Conclusion I follows by combining the two statements."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0334' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'We know only that some players, the singers, are dancers. The other players may or may not dance, so neither I nor II is certain alone. But either every player is a dancer or at least one is not: one of the two must be true. So ''either I or II'' follows.', wrong = '{"0": "Conclusion I is not certain, because players who are not singers might not be dancers.", "1": "Conclusion II is not certain, because all the players might happen to be dancers.", "3": "I and II form a complementary pair: they cannot both be false, so one of them must hold."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0335' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some mangoes are bananas, and no apple is a mango. So the bananas that are mangoes cannot be apples, which makes ''some bananas are not apples'' certain (I). Apples are not linked to bananas by any statement, so II is not certain.', wrong = '{"1": "Apples and bananas are linked only through mangoes, and no apple is a mango, so II is not certain.", "2": "Conclusion II is not certain, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Conclusion I follows through the bananas that are mangoes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0336' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some chairs are sofas and all sofas are beds, so those chairs are beds, which means some beds are chairs (I follows). All tables are chairs, but the chairs that are sofas need not include any table, so ''some tables are beds'' is not certain.', wrong = '{"1": "Conclusion II is not certain, because the tables may not be among the chairs that are sofas.", "2": "Conclusion II is only possible, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Conclusion I follows through the chairs that are sofas, which are beds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0337' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All notes are papers, so the notes themselves are papers, and some papers are notes (II follows). Conclusion I does not follow: the coins that are not notes could still be papers, since nothing links coins to papers.', wrong = '{"0": "Conclusion I is not certain, because coins that are not notes might still be papers.", "2": "Conclusion I is not certain, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Conclusion II follows by turning ''all notes are papers'' into ''some papers are notes''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0338' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All bricks are blocks, and no tile is a brick. So the blocks that are bricks cannot be tiles, which means some blocks are not tiles (II follows). Conclusion I does not follow: a tile could still be a block that is not a brick.', wrong = '{"0": "Conclusion I is not certain, because a tile could be a block that is not a brick.", "2": "Conclusion I is not certain, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Conclusion II follows through the bricks, which are blocks but not tiles."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0339' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All files are folders and some folders are drives, but the folders that are drives need not include any file. So ''some files are drives'' is not certain. Nothing says every drive is a file, so II is not certain either. Neither conclusion follows.', wrong = '{"0": "Conclusion I is not certain, because the folders that are drives may not be files.", "1": "Conclusion II is not certain; some drives may not even be folders, let alone files.", "2": "Neither conclusion is certain, so ''both'' is wrong."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0340' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All poets are writers and no writer is a singer, so no poet is a singer (I). Some singers are actors, and those singer-actors cannot be writers because no writer is a singer. So some actors are not writers (II). Both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "Conclusion II also follows, through the actors who are singers.", "1": "Conclusion I also follows, because every poet is a writer and no writer is a singer.", "3": "Both conclusions follow directly from the statements."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0341' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All circles are shapes and some shapes are squares, but nothing says whether circles and squares overlap. So neither I nor II is certain alone. Yet ''some circles are squares'' and ''no circle is a square'' cannot both be false, so either I or II follows.', wrong = '{"0": "Conclusion I is not certain on its own, because the shapes that are squares may not be circles.", "1": "Conclusion II is not certain on its own, because some circles might be squares.", "3": "I and II form a complementary pair, so one of them must be true."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0342' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Decode each part: A × B means A is B''s brother, B + C means B is C''s son, and C − D means C is D''s wife. So B is the son of C and D, and A, being B''s brother, is also their son. A is D''s son.', wrong = '{"0": "D is a parent of A''s brother, so A is D''s child, not D''s brother.", "2": "A nephew is a sibling''s son. D is A''s parent, not A''s uncle.", "3": "A grandson is two generations below. A is only one generation below D."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0343' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hina''s mother''s father-in-law is the father of Hina''s father, that is, her paternal grandfather. His only son is Hina''s father. The only son of Hina''s father is Hina''s brother.', wrong = '{"0": "Hina''s father is the grandfather''s only son. The man is that person''s son, one generation lower.", "1": "A cousin would be the child of an uncle or aunt, but the grandfather has only one son, Hina''s father.", "3": "An uncle would be another son of the grandfather, but the grandfather has only one son."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0344' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A''s father is B''s son, so B is A''s grandparent. C is B''s daughter, so C is the sister of A''s father, which makes her A''s paternal aunt (phuppo). D, C''s husband, is therefore A''s uncle by marriage (phupha).', wrong = '{"0": "A''s father is B''s son. D is C''s husband, a different person who married into the family.", "1": "B is A''s grandparent. D belongs to the generation of A''s parents, not grandparents.", "2": "A brother-in-law is a sibling''s husband or a spouse''s brother. D is one generation above A."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0345' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Q is P''s wife and the mother of R. T is R''s wife. The wife of one''s son is one''s daughter-in-law, so T is Q''s daughter-in-law. S, the daughter of R and T, is Q''s granddaughter.', wrong = '{"1": "T married into the family through R. She is not Q''s own child.", "2": "A sister-in-law is in the same generation as Q. T is one generation below, as the wife of Q''s son.", "3": "S, the daughter of R, is Q''s granddaughter. T is S''s mother."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0346' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'P $ Q: P is Q''s father. Q # R: Q is R''s sister, so R is also P''s child. R @ S: R is S''s husband, so R is male. S is the wife of P''s son, which makes S P''s daughter-in-law.', wrong = '{"0": "Q is P''s daughter. S married into the family through R.", "2": "S is the wife of R, P''s son. She is not P''s own wife.", "3": "A sister would be in P''s generation. S is one generation below P."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0347' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'M ◆ N: M is N''s mother. N ★ O: N is O''s brother, so M is also O''s mother. O ▲ P: O is P''s son. O has M as mother and P as a parent, so P is O''s father and M''s husband.', wrong = '{"0": "O, not P, is M''s son. P is O''s parent, in M''s own generation.", "1": "P is the parent of M''s children, in the same generation as M, so not her father.", "3": "P is the father of M''s children, which makes him her husband, not her brother."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0348' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When they swap, A moves into B''s old seat. So B''s seat was 25th from the left, and we know it was 20th from the right. A seat counted from both ends gives total = 25 + 20 − 1 = 44, since that seat is counted twice.', wrong = '{"0": "45 is 25 + 20, forgetting that B''s seat is counted from both ends and must be subtracted once.", "1": "40 is 25 + 20 − 5, subtracting 5 instead of 1.", "2": "35 is 15 + 20, using A''s old position, which is not the same seat as B''s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0349' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Saad is 7 ranks below Rahim, so his rank from the top is 14 + 7 = 21. Rank from the bottom = total − rank from top + 1 = 45 − 21 + 1 = 25. So Saad is 25th from the bottom.', wrong = '{"1": "24th is 45 − 21, forgetting to add 1 when converting the rank.", "2": "21st is Saad''s rank from the top, not the bottom.", "3": "32nd is 45 − 14 + 1, which is Rahim''s rank from the bottom, not Saad''s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0350' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From the clue about U, R is older than U and U is older than T, so R > U > T. T is older than S, so the full order is R > U > T > S. R is older than all the others, so R is the oldest. Notice that ''R is older than S'' is already implied.', wrong = '{"0": "T is younger than U, so T cannot be the oldest.", "2": "U is younger than R, so U cannot be the oldest.", "3": "The clue about U places R above T and U, so the oldest can be determined."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0351' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A scored more than B and C, and D scored less than C but more than B, so A > C > D > B. E scored the least of all. The full order from top is A, C, D, B, E, so B is second lowest.', wrong = '{"0": "D scored more than B, so D is third lowest, not second lowest.", "1": "C is second highest, just below A, so far from the bottom.", "3": "E is the lowest scorer, not the second lowest."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0352' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If Uzma is to Rida''s right, she is 9 + 4 + 1 = 14th from the left, and the row has 14 + 16 − 1 = 29 children. If she is to Rida''s left, she is 9 − 5 = 4th from the left, giving 4 + 16 − 1 = 19 children. Both arrangements work, so 19 or 29.', wrong = '{"0": "29 assumes Uzma must be to Rida''s right, but she could also be to her left.", "1": "19 assumes Uzma must be to Rida''s left, but she could also be to her right.", "2": "28 and 30 come from miscounting the 4 children between them by one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0353' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'M and O are both heavier than N, and P is lighter than N. But no statement compares M with O. Either of them could be the heavier, so the heaviest person cannot be determined.', wrong = '{"1": "M is heavier than N, but M might still be lighter than O.", "2": "O is heavier than N, but O might still be lighter than M.", "3": "N is lighter than both M and O, so N cannot be the heaviest."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0354' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rules form a chain: generator on → lights work → guard stays awake. The guard fell asleep, so by the contrapositive the lights did not work. If the lights did not work, the generator cannot have been on. So the generator was off.', wrong = '{"0": "If the lights had worked, the guard would have stayed awake, but he fell asleep.", "2": "If the generator had been on, the lights would have worked and the guard would have stayed awake.", "3": "If the generator had been on, the lights would have worked, so they cannot have failed with it on."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0355' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Unless it rains, the match will be played'' means ''if it does not rain, the match is played''. The match was not played, so by the contrapositive it cannot be true that it did not rain. Therefore it rained.', wrong = '{"0": "If it had not rained, the match would have been played, but it was not.", "1": "The statement says the match is always played on a day without rain, so another reason on a dry day is ruled out.", "3": "The contrapositive gives a definite conclusion: it rained."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0356' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chand goes. If Bilal went, Chand would not go, so Bilal does not go. If Ali went, Bilal would go, but Bilal does not, so Ali does not go either. Working backwards with contrapositives gives: Ali does not go.', wrong = '{"0": "If Ali went, Bilal would go, and then Chand could not go. But Chand goes.", "1": "If Bilal went, Chand would not go. But Chand goes.", "2": "Neither Ali nor Bilal can go when Chand goes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0357' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Only students who pass get a certificate'' means passing is a necessary condition: no student without a pass gets a certificate. Sara got a certificate, so she must have passed the test.', wrong = '{"1": "Passing is necessary, not sufficient. Some students who passed might still not get a certificate.", "2": "Nothing is said about marks or ranks, only about passing.", "3": "This contradicts the statement, which says only those who pass get one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0358' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rule is: if a test is easy, then more than half the class passes. In the last test fewer than half passed, so the result of the rule did not happen. By the contrapositive, the test was not easy.', wrong = '{"0": "If the test had been easy, more than half the class would have passed.", "2": "The rule says nothing about preparation, so this cannot be concluded.", "3": "The contrapositive gives a definite conclusion: the test was not easy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0359' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some students are athletes and no athlete smokes, so at least those students do not smoke. ''All students smoke'' would make those athletes smokers, which contradicts the statements. So this one is definitely false.', wrong = '{"0": "Club members are students, and some students are athletes, but the athletes may or may not be club members. This could be true or false.", "1": "Club members might or might not smoke, since only athletes are known not to. This could be true or false.", "3": "This is definitely true, because the student-athletes do not smoke."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0360' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'D and E are on back-to-back days, and Wednesday is B''s, so D and E take Monday-Tuesday or Thursday-Friday. If they took Monday-Tuesday, A and C would get Thursday and Friday, with C on Friday, which is not allowed. So D is Thursday, E Friday, A Monday and C Tuesday.', wrong = '{"0": "A opens before C, so A takes Monday and C takes Tuesday.", "1": "D opens on Thursday, the day before E, not on Tuesday.", "2": "E opens on Friday, the day after D, not on Tuesday."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0361' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some students who study at night score well. Everyone who scores well gets a scholarship, so those night-studiers who score well also get one. Therefore some students who study at night get a scholarship.', wrong = '{"1": "Only some night-studiers are known to score well, so not all are sure to get a scholarship.", "2": "This reverses the second statement. Scholarship holders may also include day-studiers who scored well.", "3": "Nothing is said about day-studiers; some may score well and get scholarships."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0362' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every shirt is either cotton or silk. No cotton shirt has buttons, so the shirt with buttons is not cotton. It must therefore be silk, and every silk shirt is expensive. So the buttoned shirt must be expensive.', wrong = '{"0": "No cotton shirt has buttons, so the buttoned shirt cannot be cotton.", "2": "The shirt is silk, and every silk shirt is expensive, so it cannot be cheap.", "3": "Every shirt is cotton or silk, not both."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0363' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If 5 machines make 5 parts in 5 minutes, each machine makes 1 part in 5 minutes. With 100 machines working at the same time, each makes its own 1 part in 5 minutes, giving 100 parts in 5 minutes.', wrong = '{"1": "100 minutes assumes one machine makes all 100 parts by itself.", "2": "20 minutes multiplies the time by 100 ÷ 5 without reason. More machines share the work, so time does not grow.", "3": "1 minute assumes each machine works faster when there are more of them, which is not stated."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0364' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each full day and night the snail gains 3 − 2 = 1 m. After 7 days and nights it is at 7 m. On the 8th day it climbs 3 m to reach 10 m and gets out before it can slip back. So it escapes on the 8th day.', wrong = '{"0": "10 days assumes it gains only 1 m every day. On the last day it climbs out and does not slip back.", "2": "On the 7th day it climbs from 6 m to only 9 m, and slips back to 7 m that night.", "3": "By the 8th day it has already reached the top, so the 9th day is too late."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0365' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The number of cuts is one less than the number of pieces. Cutting a log into 3 pieces needs 2 cuts, so each cut takes 6 ÷ 2 = 3 minutes. Cutting it into 6 pieces needs 5 cuts, so the time is 5 × 3 = 15 minutes.', wrong = '{"0": "12 minutes doubles the time, as if 6 pieces needed twice as many cuts as 3 pieces. It needs 5 cuts, not 4.", "1": "18 minutes assumes one cut per piece, that is 6 cuts at 3 minutes each.", "3": "10 minutes uses 2 minutes per cut, from dividing 6 by 3 pieces instead of 2 cuts."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0366' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Count from today. The day before yesterday is today − 2. Three days after that is today − 2 + 3 = today + 1, which is tomorrow. So tomorrow is Friday, and today must be Thursday.', wrong = '{"0": "If today were Wednesday, three days after the day before yesterday would be Thursday.", "1": "Friday is tomorrow, the day three days after the day before yesterday, not today.", "2": "If today is Thursday, Tuesday is the day before yesterday, not today."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0367' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Work backwards, undoing each step: the machine doubles then subtracts 3, so reverse it by adding 3 then halving. Before the second pass: (31 + 3) ÷ 2 = 17. Before the first pass: (17 + 3) ÷ 2 = 10. Check: 10 → 17 → 31.', wrong = '{"1": "17 is the number after the first pass, not the number fed in at the start.", "2": "8.5 is 17 ÷ 2. It halves 17 but forgets to add 3 first.", "3": "12 gives 12 × 2 − 3 = 21, then 21 × 2 − 3 = 39, not 31."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0368' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'On a die each face touches four others and is opposite one. Opposite faces add up to 7, so the face opposite 4 shows 7 − 4 = 3. Opposite faces never touch, so 3 can never be next to 4, while 1, 2, 5 and 6 all touch it.', wrong = '{"0": "1 is opposite 6, not 4, so it is one of the four faces touching 4.", "2": "5 is opposite 2, not 4, so it is one of the four faces touching 4.", "3": "6 is opposite 1, not 4, so it is one of the four faces touching 4."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0369' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The 3 cm cube is cut into 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 small cubes. Removing the outer layer from every side leaves an inner block of (3 − 2)³ = 1³ = 1 cube. Only this centre cube has no face on the outside, so 1 cube has no paint.', wrong = '{"0": "The centre cube is hidden on all six sides, so it receives no paint. The count is not zero.", "1": "6 is the number of cubes with exactly one painted face, one at the centre of each face.", "3": "8 is the number of corner cubes, each with three painted faces."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0370' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ali does not study a science, so Ali studies English. Bina cannot take Physics or Biology, and English is taken, so Bina studies Chemistry. Dua cannot take Biology, so Dua studies Physics, which leaves Biology for Chand.', wrong = '{"0": "Bina studies Chemistry, the only subject left that she is allowed.", "1": "Chand studies Biology, the last subject remaining.", "2": "Ali does not study a science, so Ali studies English."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0371' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Number seats 1 to 7 from the left; S is in seat 4. If P were in seat 1, Q would be in seat 3, T would need seat 2, and R would have no free seat beside T. So P is in seat 7, U in seat 1 and Q in seat 5. R and T fill seats 2 and 3, and V must take seat 6, second from the right.', wrong = '{"1": "Q sits in seat 5, which is 3rd from the right, next to S.", "2": "P sits at the right end itself, not second from it.", "3": "R sits next to T in seats 2 and 3, to the left of S."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0372' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'B says D did it and D says B is lying, so exactly one of B and D is telling the truth. That uses up the single true statement, so A and C are both lying. C lying about ''I did not do it'' means C broke the window. Check: A and B are false and D is true, so exactly one truth.', wrong = '{"0": "If A did it, C''s ''I did not do it'' and D''s ''B is lying'' would both be true, giving two truths.", "2": "If B did it, A, C and D would all be telling the truth, giving three truths.", "3": "If D did it, B and C would both be telling the truth, giving two truths."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0373' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Suppose A is a knight. Then B is a knave, and B''s claim that they are the same type is false, as a knave''s should be. This works. If A were a knave, B would be a knight, but then B''s true claim would make them the same type, a contradiction. So A is a knight and B is a knave.', wrong = '{"0": "If both were knights, A''s statement ''B is a knave'' would be false, which a knight cannot say.", "1": "If both were knaves, B''s statement ''we are the same type'' would be true, which a knave cannot say.", "3": "If B were a knight and A a knave, B''s statement that they are the same type would be false, which a knight cannot say."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0374' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Count odd days (days left over after full weeks). Years 1 to 1600 give 0, the next 300 years give 1, and 1901 to 1946 (46 years, 11 of them leap) give 46 + 11 = 57, which is 1 odd day. 1 January to 14 August 1947 is 226 days, which is 2 odd days. Total 0 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 4, which is Thursday.', wrong = '{"0": "Friday would need 5 odd days; the count is 4.", "1": "Wednesday would need 3 odd days, one fewer than the 4 counted.", "2": "Sunday would need 0 odd days, but the count gives 4."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0375' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An ordinary year has 365 days, which is 52 weeks and 1 day, so the same date moves forward one weekday each year. From 15 August 2025 to 15 August 2027 no 29 February is passed, because 2026 and 2027 are not leap years. So Friday moves on 2 days to Sunday.', wrong = '{"1": "Saturday adds only one year''s shift; two years have passed.", "2": "Monday adds three days, as if a 29 February were included. The next leap day is in 2028.", "3": "The weekday moves forward each year, so it cannot still be Friday."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0376' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Days of the week repeat every 7 days. 250 = 35 × 7 + 5, so 250 days is 35 full weeks plus 5 days. Counting 5 days on from Thursday: Friday, Saturday, Sunday, Monday, Tuesday. The day will be Tuesday.', wrong = '{"0": "Monday is only 4 days after Thursday; the remainder is 5.", "2": "Wednesday is 6 days after Thursday, one day too many.", "3": "Thursday would need 250 to be a multiple of 7, but it leaves remainder 5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0377' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A calendar can be reused when the year starts on the same weekday and has the same number of days. 2021 began on a Friday and is not a leap year. Odd days: 2021 to 2026 give 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 7, a whole week, so 2027 also starts on Friday and has 365 days.', wrong = '{"0": "2025 starts on a Wednesday, not a Friday.", "1": "2032 is a leap year with 366 days, so it cannot match the 365 days of 2021.", "3": "2029 starts on a Monday, not a Friday, so its dates fall on different days."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0378' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A non-leap year has 365 days = 52 weeks + 1 day. The 52 weeks give 52 Sundays. If the one extra day is also a Sunday, which happens when the year starts on a Sunday, there are 53 Sundays. So the greatest number is 53.', wrong = '{"0": "52 is the smallest possible number, not the greatest.", "1": "54 would need 2 extra Sundays beyond the 52 weeks, but only 1 extra day exists.", "2": "Every year has at least 52 full weeks, so it has at least 52 Sundays."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0379' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The hour hand moves 0.5° per minute and the minute hand 6° per minute. At 7:20 the hour hand is at 7 × 30 + 20 × 0.5 = 220° and the minute hand at 20 × 6 = 120°. The angle is 220 − 120 = 100°, the same as |30 × 7 − 5.5 × 20|.', wrong = '{"1": "90° treats the hour hand as pointing exactly at 7. In 20 minutes it moves 10° further.", "2": "110° moves the hour hand 20° past 7 instead of 10°.", "3": "80° moves the hour hand 10° backwards from 7 instead of forwards."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0380' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At 4:00 the hour hand is 4 × 30 = 120° ahead of the minute hand. The minute hand gains 6 − 0.5 = 5.5° per minute. It catches up after 120 ÷ 5.5 = 240/11 = 21 9/11 minutes, so the hands meet at 21 9/11 minutes past 4.', wrong = '{"0": "At 4:20 the hour hand has moved 10° past 4, so the hands are still 10° apart.", "2": "At 4:22 the minute hand has already passed the hour hand by 1°.", "3": "21 3/11 is 234/11 minutes. At that time the hands are still 3° apart."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0381' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a clock seen in a mirror, actual time = 11:60 − mirror time, that is, both times add up to 12:00. So actual time = 11:60 − 3:40 = 8:20. Check: 3:40 + 8:20 = 12:00.', wrong = '{"0": "9:20 comes from 12:60 − 3:40, using the wrong starting value.", "1": "8:40 keeps the minutes as 40 instead of mirroring them to 20.", "3": "4:20 reads the hour as 4 without mirroring the hour hand; only the minutes are flipped."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0382' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 12 hours the hands are at right angles 22 times, which is twice an hour except near 3 o''clock and 9 o''clock, where the two cases share one moment (3:00 and 9:00). A full day has two 12-hour periods, so 22 × 2 = 44 times.', wrong = '{"0": "48 assumes exactly two right angles every hour, ignoring the shared cases at 3 and 9.", "1": "24 assumes only one right angle each hour.", "2": "22 is the count for 12 hours, not a full day of 24 hours."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0383' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From 8:00 am to 2:00 pm is 6 hours. Gaining 5 minutes each hour, the clock gains 6 × 5 = 30 minutes. A clock that gains runs ahead, so it shows 2:00 pm + 30 minutes = 2:30 pm.', wrong = '{"1": "2:05 pm adds the gain for only one hour instead of six.", "2": "2:25 pm counts 5 hours instead of 6 from 8 am to 2 pm.", "3": "1:30 pm puts the clock behind, but a clock that gains runs ahead."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0384' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A grid of 3 rows and 4 columns has 4 horizontal lines and 5 vertical lines. Every rectangle is fixed by choosing 2 horizontal and 2 vertical lines: C(4, 2) × C(5, 2) = 6 × 10 = 60 rectangles, squares included.', wrong = '{"0": "12 counts only the smallest 1 × 1 squares.", "2": "20 counts only the squares of all sizes: 12 + 6 + 2.", "3": "72 overcounts. With 4 horizontal and 5 vertical lines the exact count is 6 × 10 = 60."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0385' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each handshake joins one pair of people, so n people make C(n, 2) = n(n − 1) ÷ 2 handshakes. Setting n(n − 1) ÷ 2 = 45 gives n(n − 1) = 90, and 10 × 9 = 90, so there were 10 people.', wrong = '{"0": "9 people make 9 × 8 ÷ 2 = 36 handshakes, not 45.", "1": "15 people make 15 × 14 ÷ 2 = 105 handshakes.", "3": "45 is the number of handshakes, not the number of people."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0386' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A cube with exactly two painted faces lies on an edge of the big cube but not at a corner. The 4 cm cube has 4 small cubes on each edge, and removing the 2 corners leaves 4 − 2 = 2. With 12 edges, the total is 12 × 2 = 24.', wrong = '{"0": "8 is the number of corner cubes, which have three painted faces.", "1": "12 allows only one cube per edge, but each edge has two non-corner cubes.", "2": "32 is 24 + 8, wrongly adding the corner cubes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0387' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A polygon with n sides has n(n − 3) ÷ 2 diagonals, because each corner joins to n − 3 others and each diagonal is counted twice. Setting n(n − 3) ÷ 2 = 20 gives n(n − 3) = 40, and 8 × 5 = 40, so n = 8.', wrong = '{"1": "A 10-sided polygon has 10 × 7 ÷ 2 = 35 diagonals.", "2": "A 7-sided polygon has 7 × 4 ÷ 2 = 14 diagonals.", "3": "A 20-sided polygon has 20 × 17 ÷ 2 = 170 diagonals."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0388' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'No three points on a circle lie on one straight line, so any 3 of the 8 points form a triangle. The number of ways to choose 3 points from 8 is C(8, 3) = (8 × 7 × 6) ÷ (3 × 2 × 1) = 336 ÷ 6 = 56.', wrong = '{"0": "24 is 8 × 3, which is not a count of choices of three points.", "2": "336 = 8 × 7 × 6 counts each triangle 6 times, once for each order of its corners.", "3": "28 = C(8, 2) counts pairs of points, that is, lines, not triangles."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0389' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fill the digits in order. The first digit cannot be 0, so it has 9 choices (1 to 9). The second can be any of the 9 digits not yet used, including 0. The third has 8 choices left. Total = 9 × 9 × 8 = 648.', wrong = '{"0": "720 = 10 × 9 × 8 allows 0 as the first digit, which does not make a three-digit number.", "1": "900 counts all three-digit numbers from 100 to 999, including those with repeated digits.", "3": "504 = 9 × 8 × 7 wrongly leaves 0 out of the second and third places."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0390' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bilal walks 10 m north, turns right to walk 6 m east, turns right again to walk 18 m south, then turns left (east) for 2 m. East: 6 + 2 = 8 m. South: 18 − 10 = 8 m. Distance = √(8² + 8²) = 8√2 ≈ 11.3 m, towards the south-east.', wrong = '{"0": "8 m south ignores the 8 m he has moved east.", "1": "16 m adds the two 8 m parts directly instead of using Pythagoras'' theorem.", "2": "He ends up 8 m south of the start, so north-east is the wrong direction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0391' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the early morning the sun is in the east, so shadows fall towards the west. Asif''s shadow falls to his right, so west is on his right. A person with west on the right is facing south.', wrong = '{"1": "Facing north, west would be on his left, so the shadow would fall to his left.", "2": "Facing east, towards the sun, his shadow would fall behind him.", "3": "Facing west, his shadow would fall in front of him."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0392' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Start facing north. A 90° clockwise turn gives east. A 135° anticlockwise turn from east passes north (90°) and goes 45° more, to north-west. A 180° turn reverses this to south-east.', wrong = '{"0": "North-west is where he faces before the final 180° turn.", "2": "South-west is where he would face before the last turn if the 135° turn were made clockwise.", "3": "North-east is the final direction if the 135° turn is made clockwise instead of anticlockwise."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0393' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Let the ages be 3x and 5x. After 8 years, (3x + 8) ÷ (5x + 8) = 5 ÷ 7. Cross-multiplying: 7(3x + 8) = 5(5x + 8), so 21x + 56 = 25x + 40, and 4x = 16, x = 4. A is 3 × 4 = 12 years. Check: 20 : 28 = 5 : 7.', wrong = '{"0": "If A were 15, B would be 25, and after 8 years the ratio would be 23 : 33, not 5 : 7.", "1": "20 years is B''s present age (5 × 4), not A''s.", "3": "If A were 9, B would be 15, and after 8 years the ratio would be 17 : 23, not 5 : 7."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0394' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Take the starting price as Rs 100. A 20% rise gives 100 + 20 = 120. A 20% cut is then 20% of 120 = 24, giving 120 − 24 = 96. The price falls from 100 to 96, a 4% decrease overall.', wrong = '{"0": "The 20% cut is taken from a larger amount (120), so it removes more than the 20 that was added.", "1": "The final price, 96, is lower than the start, so there is no increase.", "2": "The fall equals 20% of 20%, which is 4%, not 2%."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0395' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Work by rates. A does 1/10 of the job per day and B does 1/15. Together: 1/10 + 1/15 = 3/30 + 2/30 = 5/30 = 1/6 of the job per day. So together they need 6 days.', wrong = '{"1": "12.5 is the average of 10 and 15. Working together must be faster than either person alone.", "2": "5 days would need 1/5 of the job per day, but together they do only 1/6.", "3": "25 adds the times instead of adding the work rates."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0396' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First convert the speed: 72 km/h × (1000 ÷ 3600) = 20 m/s. To cross the bridge completely, the train must cover the bridge length plus its own length: 300 + 200 = 500 m. Time = 500 ÷ 20 = 25 s.', wrong = '{"0": "15 s is 300 ÷ 20, using only the bridge length and forgetting the train''s own length.", "2": "10 s is 200 ÷ 20, using only the train''s length.", "3": "7 s is about 500 ÷ 72, dividing by the speed in km/h without converting to m/s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0397' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'There are 12 months, so 12 people could all be born in different months. A 13th person must then share a month with someone. This is the pigeonhole principle: with more items than boxes, some box holds two. So 13 is the smallest number that guarantees a match.', wrong = '{"0": "With 12 people, each could be born in a different month, so a match is not certain.", "1": "Two people can easily be born in different months.", "3": "24 people do guarantee a match, but 13 already does, so 24 is not the smallest."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0398' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Think of the worst case. The first 10 socks drawn could all be white. After that only black socks remain, so 2 more draws give two black socks. Total = 10 + 2 = 12.', wrong = '{"0": "3 socks guarantee a matching pair of some colour, but they could be two white socks and one black.", "1": "11 socks could be 10 white and just 1 black.", "2": "4 socks could all be white, giving no black sock at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0399' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Use inclusion-exclusion: students in at least one subject = 50 + 40 + 30 − 15 − 10 − 12 + 5 = 88. The 5 studying all three are subtracted three times in the pairs, so they are added back once. Students in none = 100 − 88 = 12.', wrong = '{"1": "17 forgets to add back the 5 who study all three, giving 83 in at least one subject.", "2": "7 adds the 5 back twice, giving 93 in at least one subject instead of 88.", "3": "Only 88 students take at least one subject, so 12 must take none."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0400' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only Biology = total Biology − (Biology and Chemistry) − (Biology and Physics) + (all three). The 5 in all three are inside both overlaps, so they are removed twice and must be added back once: 50 − 15 − 12 + 5 = 28.', wrong = '{"0": "23 is 50 − 15 − 12, forgetting to add back the 5 who take all three.", "2": "33 adds the 5 back twice instead of once.", "3": "35 is 50 − 15, removing only the Biology and Chemistry overlap."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0401' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''tea'' is in sentences 1 and 3, whose only shared code is ''la'', so tea = la. ''good'' is in sentences 2 and 3, whose only shared code is ''ka'', so good = ka. ''hot'' and ''is'' are in sentences 1 and 2, sharing ''pa'' and ''ra''. The code left over in sentence 2 is ''sa'', so milk = sa.', wrong = '{"0": "ka is the code for ''good'', shared by sentences 2 and 3.", "1": "pa is the code for ''hot'' or ''is'', which appear in sentences 1 and 2.", "3": "ra is the code for ''hot'' or ''is'', which appear in sentences 1 and 2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0402' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''red'' is in sentences 1 and 2, which share only ''mal'', so red = mal. ''is'' is in sentences 1 and 3, which share only ''tik'', so is = tik. Sentence 1 is ''pen is red'' = ''dop tik mal'', so the remaining code ''dop'' must mean ''pen''.', wrong = '{"0": "''is'' is coded tik, the code shared by sentences 1 and 3.", "1": "''red'' is coded mal, the code shared by sentences 1 and 2.", "2": "''blue'' appears only in sentence 3 and is coded zor. It is not in sentence 1, where dop appears."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0403' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'T is on floor 5, so R (even floor) is on 2 or 4. If R were on 2, P (beside R but not on 1) would be on 3, leaving floors 1 and 4 for Q and S, which are not one above the other. So R is on 4 and P on 3. Q and S take floors 2 and 1, with Q above, so S is on floor 1.', wrong = '{"1": "R lives on floor 4, the only even floor that fits all the clues.", "2": "Q lives on floor 2, directly above S on floor 1.", "3": "P lives on floor 3, and the clues also say P is not on floor 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0404' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'C sits next to A, so C is not opposite A. B and E sit opposite each other, so neither of them can be opposite A. That leaves D or F, and the clue says F is not opposite A. So D sits opposite A, with F beside D and B and E filling the remaining opposite pair.', wrong = '{"0": "The last clue states that F does not sit opposite A.", "2": "B sits opposite E, so B cannot also be opposite A.", "3": "C sits immediately to A''s right, so C is A''s neighbour, not opposite."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0405' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every label is wrong, so the box labelled ''Apples and oranges'' holds only one kind of fruit, and one fruit shows which. If it is an apple, that box is Apples. The box labelled ''Oranges'' cannot be oranges or apples, so it is the mixed box, and the box labelled ''Apples'' holds oranges.', wrong = '{"0": "The box labelled ''Apples'' could hold oranges or the mix. Drawing an orange would not show which.", "1": "The box labelled ''Oranges'' could hold apples or the mix. Drawing an apple would not show which.", "3": "One fruit from the box labelled ''Apples and oranges'' is enough to label all three correctly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0406' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Physics student is not from Lahore, and not from Quetta, because the Chemistry student is from Quetta. So the Physics student is from Karachi, which is Babar. Sana does not study Physics and Amna does not study Biology, so Amna studies Chemistry (Quetta) and Sana studies Biology (Lahore).', wrong = '{"0": "Babar, from Karachi, is the Physics student. Amna studies Chemistry and is from Quetta.", "1": "The Chemistry student is from Quetta, not Lahore, and that student is Amna.", "2": "Amna does not study Biology, so she cannot be the Biology student from Lahore."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0407' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The minute hand gains one full lap on the hour hand every 12/11 hours, so in 12 hours the hands meet 11 times, counting noon (midnight starts the next cycle). The meetings are at noon and at about 1:05, 2:11, …, 10:54. Leaving out noon gives 10. There is no meeting between 11 and 12 except at 12 itself.', wrong = '{"1": "11 includes the meeting at noon (or midnight), which the question tells us not to count.", "2": "12 assumes the hands meet once every hour, but there is no meeting between 11 and 12 o''clock.", "3": "22 is the number of meetings in a full 24-hour day."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0408' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A strong argument is relevant to the question and points to an important result. Argument I is strong because phones make cheating easy, which harms the fairness of the exam. Argument II is weak: a student can call parents before or after the exam, so this need does not outweigh fairness.', wrong = '{"1": "Calling parents can wait until the exam is over, so argument II gives no serious reason against a ban.", "2": "Argument II is weak, so both cannot be strong.", "3": "Argument I is strong: preventing cheating is a direct and important reason for the ban."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0409' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Both arguments point to real and important results. Argument I names a genuine benefit: students who miss once would not lose a whole year. Argument II names genuine problems: higher costs and the difficulty of making two papers equally hard, which affects fairness. So both are strong.', wrong = '{"0": "Argument II is also strong, because cost and equal difficulty are serious practical and fairness concerns.", "1": "Argument I is also strong, because a second chance without losing a year is a real benefit.", "3": "Both arguments are relevant and important, so neither is weak."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0410' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Argument I is strong because harm to children''s health is a serious and directly relevant reason for a school''s decision. Argument II is weak: what children like is not a good reason for a school to sell something that harms them.', wrong = '{"1": "Children''s liking for junk food is not a strong reason when their health is at stake.", "2": "Argument II is weak, so both cannot be strong.", "3": "Argument I is strong, since protecting health is a central concern of a school."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0411' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Neither argument is strong. Argument I is irrelevant: what famous athletes can do says nothing about whether ordinary students need to swim. Argument II misses the point: students who already swim would simply meet the requirement, so it is no reason against it.', wrong = '{"0": "The skills of famous athletes have no bearing on whether all students should learn swimming.", "1": "Students who already swim would meet the requirement easily, so this does not argue against it.", "2": "Both arguments are weak, so they cannot both be strong."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0412' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Argument II is strong: it points to real costs, extra staff and security the college cannot afford, against little benefit, since few students would come at night. Argument I is weak because the number of books has nothing to do with how long the library should stay open.', wrong = '{"0": "Having many books does not show that the library needs to be open all night.", "2": "Argument I is weak, so both cannot be strong.", "3": "Argument II is strong, since it weighs real costs against low use."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0413' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each argument points to a real strength of one measure. Argument I is right that one common test compares all candidates on the same scale. Argument II is right that FSc marks reflect two years of work, not one day''s performance. Both are relevant and important, so both are strong.', wrong = '{"0": "Argument II is also strong, because two years of results show consistent effort.", "1": "Argument I is also strong, because a single common test compares all candidates fairly.", "3": "Both arguments raise relevant and important points."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0414' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Before taking any serious action, the principal needs the facts: how many students fell ill, what they ate and when. This shows whether the canteen is really the cause and how serious the problem is. Every other step depends on this information.', wrong = '{"0": "Closing the canteen permanently before knowing the facts is premature and may punish the wrong party.", "2": "Dismissing the cook without evidence is unfair, and the cause may lie elsewhere.", "3": "Ignoring a possible health risk until parents complain is irresponsible."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0415' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A new clinic should go where many people have poor access to health care. So the most useful information is how many people live in each area and how far they are from the nearest existing clinic. Together these show where a clinic would help the most people.', wrong = '{"0": "The number of shops says little about health needs or access to care.", "1": "The officer''s own convenience is not a sound reason for choosing a clinic site.", "3": "New roads alone do not show where people lack health care."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0416' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A good course of action is practical and deals directly with the problem. Testing the pump''s water and pausing its use protects the children and finds the cause, so course I follows. Removing every hand pump in the province is far out of proportion to a problem at one pump.', wrong = '{"1": "Removing every pump in the province is an overreaction and would leave many people without water.", "2": "Course II is extreme and impractical, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course I is a sensible, direct response to the illness."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0417' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The complaint is about two questions, so the fair and direct response is to have subject experts review them and correct the key if needed. Cancelling and repeating the whole test would burden every candidate and is out of proportion to two disputed items.', wrong = '{"1": "Cancelling the whole test is far too drastic a response to two disputed questions.", "2": "Course II is out of proportion, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course I directly answers the complaint."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0418' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Dengue spreads through mosquitoes that breed in standing water. Course I tackles the cause by removing breeding sites, and course II deals with the patients already falling ill by preparing beds and platelet testing. Both are practical and needed, so both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "Course II is also needed to care for the growing number of patients.", "1": "Course I is also needed to stop mosquitoes breeding and cut new cases.", "3": "Both courses are practical responses to the rise in dengue."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0419' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Visible cracks could mean danger, so keeping heavy vehicles off until engineers inspect the bridge protects lives; course I follows. Jailing the designer before any inquiry assumes blame without evidence and does nothing to make the bridge safe.', wrong = '{"1": "Punishing someone before an inquiry is unjustified and does not fix the bridge.", "2": "Course II is not justified, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course I is a sensible safety step while the bridge is checked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0420' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Course II tackles the likely reasons students skip breakfast, such as lack of time, money or awareness, by explaining why it matters and making it easy and cheap. Course I is extreme: punishing students for a personal eating habit is harsh and hard to enforce.', wrong = '{"0": "Making breakfast compulsory and punishing students is extreme and difficult to enforce.", "2": "Course I is not practical, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course II is a practical way to help students eat breakfast."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0421' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Neither course is sensible. Closing every branch because sales fell near one competitor is an extreme overreaction. Stopping a lawful competitor simply for competing is unfair and not the government''s role. The chain should instead improve its own service or prices.', wrong = '{"0": "Closing all branches over falling sales at one place is an overreaction.", "1": "A lawful business cannot be shut down just because it competes successfully.", "2": "Both courses are extreme, so neither follows."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0422' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Long waits come from too many patients arriving at once and too few staff where queues are longest. A token and appointment system spreads patients through the day, and moving staff to the busiest departments speeds up the worst queues. Both are practical, so both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "Course II is also a practical way to cut waiting times in the busiest departments.", "1": "Course I is also practical, since appointments spread patients through the day.", "3": "Both courses address the long waiting times."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0423' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Failing one subject in a first-term exam calls for extra help or study, not drastic steps. Expelling the student is far too harsh. Dropping all his other subjects would only make him fail those instead. Neither course follows.', wrong = '{"0": "Expulsion is far too harsh a response to one failed subject.", "1": "Dropping all other subjects creates a bigger problem than it solves.", "2": "Both courses are extreme, so neither follows."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0424' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Course II reduces the pollution itself by enforcing emission rules on brick kilns and factories, major winter sources. Course I is extreme: closing every school permanently does not reduce pollution, and at most short closures on the worst days could be considered.', wrong = '{"0": "Closing all schools permanently is extreme and does nothing to reduce the pollution.", "2": "Course I is not justified, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course II tackles a main source of the winter pollution."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0425' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The problem can be fought from two sides. Inspections and testing (course I) remove fake medicines from sale, and teaching people to check registration numbers and packaging (course II) helps buyers protect themselves. Both are practical, so both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "Course II also helps, because informed buyers can avoid fake medicines.", "1": "Course I is also needed to remove fakes from the market.", "3": "Both courses are practical and useful against fake medicines."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0426' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The fire was caused by an electrical fault, so having all the wiring checked by a qualified electrician deals directly with the cause and prevents another fire; course I follows. Stopping science teaching does nothing to fix faulty wiring.', wrong = '{"1": "Stopping science lessons does not fix the electrical fault.", "2": "Course II does not address the cause, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course I deals directly with the cause of the fire."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0427' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The bird is declining because its wetland is being drained. Declaring the wetland protected stops further draining, and paying farmers to keep the land wet gives them a reason to cooperate without losing income. Both tackle the cause, so both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "Course II also tackles the cause by giving farmers an alternative to draining.", "1": "Course I also protects the habitat directly by law.", "3": "Both courses address the loss of the wetland habitat."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0428' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The accidents involve riders without helmets, so strictly enforcing the helmet law reduces serious head injuries; course I follows. Banning motorcycles in the city would harm the many people who depend on them for work and travel, and is out of proportion.', wrong = '{"1": "A total ban on motorcycles is extreme and impractical.", "2": "Course II is not practical, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course I directly addresses the problem of riders without helmets."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0429' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A clear attendance record and leave procedure makes absences visible and teachers accountable. Finding the reasons for the absences may reveal problems, such as transport or health, that the college can fix. Both are reasonable steps, so both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "Course II is also useful, because knowing the reasons helps solve the problem.", "1": "Course I is also needed to control unreported absences.", "3": "Both courses are reasonable responses to the unreported absences."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0430' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A heat wave can cause heatstroke. Advising people to stay indoors at midday and drink plenty of water helps prevent it, and preparing hospitals deals with the cases that still occur. Both are sensible precautions, so both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "Course II is also a sensible precaution for cases that still occur.", "1": "Course I is also needed to prevent illness in the first place.", "3": "Both courses are practical responses to the forecast."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0431' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Results falling for three years point to some cause, so the sensible first step is to find what has changed, such as staff, syllabus or teaching time. Replacing every teacher at once, before the cause is known, is drastic and may not solve anything.', wrong = '{"1": "Replacing all staff before finding the cause is drastic and may not help.", "2": "Course II is premature, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course I is the sensible first step, since the cause must be known before acting."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0432' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'People are living in a building declared unsafe. Moving the families to a safe place protects their lives now, and an engineers'' assessment followed by repair or demolition deals with the building itself. Both are needed, so both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "Course II is also needed to deal with the unsafe building.", "1": "Course I is needed to protect the families immediately.", "3": "Both courses follow from the danger: one protects people, the other deals with the building."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0433' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A few cases of food poisoning point to a possible hygiene problem in the kitchen. Inspecting the kitchen finds and fixes the likely cause, so course I follows. Shutting the hostel permanently over a few cases is extreme.', wrong = '{"1": "Permanent closure is far out of proportion to a few cases.", "2": "Course II is extreme, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course I addresses the likely cause of the illness."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0434' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Graduates are unemployed because their skills do not match the jobs available. Training linked to local industries helps them find work, so course I follows. Stopping degrees would not create any jobs and would make the situation worse.', wrong = '{"1": "Stopping degrees does not create jobs and harms future students.", "2": "Course II is harmful, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course I is a practical step towards employment."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0435' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With less water in the dam, the supply must be stretched. Encouraging careful use reduces demand, and checking the main pipes for leaks stops water being lost before it reaches homes. Both help conserve the limited supply, so both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "Course II also saves water by stopping leaks.", "1": "Course I also saves water by reducing demand.", "3": "Both courses help conserve the limited supply."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0436' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The problem is confusion, not the portal itself. A simple step-by-step guide and a helpline remove the confusion while keeping the benefits of online admission, so course II follows. Scrapping the portal for paper-only admissions is an overreaction.', wrong = '{"0": "Scrapping the whole online system is an overreaction to confusion.", "2": "Course I is extreme, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course II directly addresses the confusion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0437' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The side effects were found in children, so telling doctors not to prescribe the medicine to children until a review is done protects them; course II follows. A worldwide ban for all patients goes beyond the evidence and could harm adults who benefit.', wrong = '{"0": "The evidence concerns children only, so banning it for all patients goes too far.", "2": "Course I goes beyond the evidence, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course II directly protects children until the review is done."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0438' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The cheating happened at a few centres, so investigating those centres and acting against those involved targets the problem; course II follows. Making every student in the province retake the exam would punish many honest students.', wrong = '{"0": "A province-wide retake is out of proportion to cheating at a few centres.", "2": "Course I is unfair to most students, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course II addresses the cheating directly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0439' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Identifying the fungal disease and then treating or removing the infected trees controls its spread; course II follows. Cutting down every tree on campus, healthy ones included, is extreme and destroys what the action is meant to protect.', wrong = '{"0": "Cutting down healthy trees as well is extreme.", "2": "Course I is not justified, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course II targets the disease directly by diagnosing and treating it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0440' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A few complaints about rude staff call for an inquiry and staff training. Dismissing all the staff punishes those who did nothing wrong, and closing the terminal harms every passenger. Neither course follows.', wrong = '{"0": "Dismissing every staff member over a few complaints is extreme.", "1": "Closing the terminal punishes all passengers for a staff problem.", "2": "Both courses are extreme, so neither follows."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0441' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A 10% rise in the price of one textbook is a small problem. Students giving up textbooks would harm their studies, and price controls on every book in the country are far out of proportion. Neither course follows.', wrong = '{"0": "Students still need their textbooks, so they should not stop buying them.", "1": "Controlling the price of every book is out of proportion to one price rise.", "2": "Both courses are extreme, so neither follows."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0442' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Losing one match is normal in sport and does not show a deep problem with the team or its coaching. Disbanding the team or firing the coach at once are hasty overreactions to a single result. Neither course follows.', wrong = '{"0": "Disbanding the team after one loss is extreme.", "1": "Dismissing the coach after a single match is hasty.", "2": "Both courses are overreactions, so neither follows."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0443' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only some patients want evening hours. Opening all clinics only in the evening or closing morning clinics would harm the patients who now come in the morning. Adding some evening hours would be sensible, but neither course given follows.', wrong = '{"0": "Opening only in the evening ignores the patients who come in the morning.", "1": "Closing morning clinics harms the patients who use them now.", "2": "Both courses ignore the needs of other patients, so neither follows."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0444' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Anatomy is essential knowledge for every doctor, so removing it from the course is not acceptable. Extra tutorials and practical sessions help students handle a difficult but necessary subject, so course II follows.', wrong = '{"0": "Anatomy is central to medicine and cannot be dropped from the course.", "2": "Course I is not acceptable, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course II directly helps students with the difficult subject."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0445' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The candidates fainted in a hot hall with no working fans. Making sure halls are ventilated before exam day removes the cause, and first-aid staff with drinking water help anyone who still feels unwell. Both are practical, so both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "Course II is also needed for candidates who still feel unwell on the day.", "1": "Course I is needed to fix the cause, the poorly ventilated hall.", "3": "Both courses are practical responses to the fainting."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0446' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The inquiry found the cause was fatigue from working three shifts in a row. Limiting how long signal staff can work without rest directly prevents a repeat, so course I follows. Stopping all trains until every signalman is replaced punishes everyone for one rota failure.', wrong = '{"1": "Halting the whole railway is out of proportion to the cause the inquiry found.", "2": "Course II is extreme, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course I addresses the cause identified by the inquiry."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0447' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The possible risk applies to long-term daily use and is not yet confirmed. A review of the evidence, with a label update if confirmed, is a proportionate response; course I follows. Sending everyone who took one dose to hospital is unnecessary and would cause panic.', wrong = '{"1": "The risk concerns people taking the drug daily for years, not those who took a single dose.", "2": "Course II goes far beyond the evidence, so both cannot follow.", "3": "Course I is a proportionate response to new evidence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0448' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Course I fixes the root problem, poor internet access, but this takes time. Course II keeps students learning in the meantime through printed packs and phone-based lessons. Both are practical and work together, so both follow.', wrong = '{"0": "Course II is also needed, since better internet access will take time.", "1": "Course I is needed to fix the underlying lack of internet access.", "3": "Both courses address the problem, one in the long term and one right away."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0449' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A direct cause is the event that immediately brings about the result. A freshly mopped, wet floor with no warning sign reduces grip underfoot and leaves visitors unaware of the danger, which directly leads to slipping.', wrong = '{"1": "The size of the hospital does not make a floor slippery.", "2": "The time when visitors come does not cause anyone to slip.", "3": "The colour of the walls has nothing to do with slipping."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0450' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Mould is a fungus that grows fastest in warm, moist conditions. Storing bread in a warm, damp room gives mould exactly what it needs, so the bread spoils within two days. This is the most likely cause.', wrong = '{"0": "Paper bags let moisture escape, which slows mould rather than speeding it up.", "2": "Opening early in the morning has no effect on how quickly mould grows.", "3": "Almost all bread is made with wheat flour, so this cannot explain this bakery''s problem."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0451' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A traffic signal tells drivers when to stop and when to go. With the signal broken all week, drivers from different directions could enter the crossing together, making collisions much more likely. So the broken signal most likely contributed to the accidents.', wrong = '{"0": "The name of a crossing has no effect on traffic.", "1": "The colour of the cars has nothing to do with whether they collide.", "3": "The accidents happened across the whole week, so no particular day explains them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0452' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The common cold is an infection caused by viruses. Cold weather may help these viruses spread, for example because people crowd together indoors, but the cold air itself does not cause the illness. So the more accurate statement is that colds are caused by viruses, not by cold air.', wrong = '{"0": "Cold air cannot turn into a virus; viruses come from infected people.", "1": "Colds also occur in other seasons, not only in winter.", "2": "Clothing keeps the body warm but does not give immunity to viruses."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0453' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To test whether the pen makes a difference, Kashif must compare his marks with and without it over many tests taken under similar conditions. Many tests average out chance, and similar conditions rule out other causes. This fair comparison is the best test of the belief.', wrong = '{"1": "Friends'' opinions are not evidence about whether the pen affects marks.", "2": "A single test is too few to show any pattern; the result could be chance.", "3": "Buying another pen does not test the belief about the lucky pen at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0454' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Crops need water to grow, and the officer found the field got almost no water during the growing season. That alone explains the failure. A cat crossing a field has no way of harming crops, so the black cat story is a false belief.', wrong = '{"0": "A cat has no effect on crops, so the two cannot be equal causes.", "2": "The officer''s finding is a sound cause that can be checked, so there is no reason to call it wrong.", "3": "There is no evidence that black cats affect crops; this is superstition."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0455' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A message that builds positive action gives people a good reason and a simple step they can take. Linking clean streets to children''s health and asking each person to keep their own street clean with the new bins does both, without insulting anyone.', wrong = '{"0": "Insulting people makes them defensive rather than willing to help.", "1": "Saying nothing can be done discourages any effort to clean up.", "3": "Blaming only the government takes away people''s sense that they can help."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0456' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A positive response turns failure into learning. Working out what went wrong and trying again with that problem fixed improves the project and builds the team''s skills. It is the most constructive choice.', wrong = '{"0": "Giving up for good stops any learning from the mistake.", "1": "Blaming one member in public harms teamwork and fixes nothing.", "2": "Pretending the failure never happened means the same mistake may be repeated."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0457' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A sharp rise in tax makes cigarettes more expensive, and when prices rise people tend to buy less. The tax rise came first and sales fell afterwards. So statement I is the cause and statement II is its effect.', wrong = '{"1": "The fall in sales came after the tax rise, so it cannot have caused it.", "2": "The tax rise directly explains the fall in sales; no third event is needed.", "3": "Price and sales are clearly linked, so the statements are causally related."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0458' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When many farmers switch to vegetables, more vegetables reach the local market. A larger supply with the same demand pushes prices down. So the switch (I) is the cause and the fall in vegetable prices (II) is its effect.', wrong = '{"1": "Falling vegetable prices would discourage farmers from growing vegetables, not cause a switch to them.", "2": "The switch itself explains the price fall; no separate common cause is needed.", "3": "Supply and price are directly linked, so the statements are related."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0459' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Severe winter smog in Lahore explains both statements. Smog irritates the lungs, so more people are admitted with breathing problems, and authorities close schools to protect children from it. Both are effects of the same cause, not of each other.', wrong = '{"0": "Hospital admissions are not what makes authorities close schools; the smog is.", "1": "Closing schools does not cause breathing problems.", "3": "The two statements are linked through a shared cause, the smog."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0460' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When petrol becomes much more expensive, driving costs more. Many people then switch to cheaper public transport such as the metro bus. So the petrol price rise (I) is the cause and the increase in metro bus riders (II) is its effect.', wrong = '{"1": "More people riding the metro bus would not push petrol prices up.", "2": "The petrol price rise itself explains the shift; no separate cause is needed.", "3": "Travel costs affect how people choose to travel, so the statements are related."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0461' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Many students falling ill means they cannot sit their exams properly. The college postponed the exams in response to the illness. So the illness (I) is the cause and the postponement (II) is its effect.', wrong = '{"1": "Postponing exams cannot cause a stomach infection.", "2": "The illness itself led to the postponement; no third event is needed.", "3": "The postponement is a clear response to the illness."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0462' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Floods damaged the sugarcane crop, so less cane was available to make sugar. A smaller supply pushes the price up. So the flood damage (II) is the cause and the price rise (I) is its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "Higher sugar prices cannot damage crops in a flood.", "2": "The flood damage is itself the direct cause of the price rise.", "3": "Crop supply directly affects the price of sugar."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0463' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Freezers need electricity to stay cold. When the power failed for twelve hours, freezers in shops stopped working and the frozen food thawed and spoiled. So the power failure (I) is the cause and the spoiled food (II) is its effect.', wrong = '{"1": "Spoiled food cannot cause a city-wide power failure.", "2": "The power cut itself is the direct cause; no third event is needed.", "3": "Freezers depend on electricity, so the two are clearly linked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0464' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With government doctors on strike, patients who would normally go to government hospitals had to seek care elsewhere. They went to private clinics, which became overcrowded. So the strike (I) is the cause and the crowding (II) is its effect.', wrong = '{"1": "Crowded private clinics did not cause government doctors to strike.", "2": "The strike itself explains the crowding; no separate cause is needed.", "3": "Patients turned to private clinics because of the strike, so the two are linked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0465' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Because canal water was short, farmers pumped far more groundwater. Pumping out large amounts lowers the water underground, so the village wells fall. So the heavy pumping (II) is the cause and the drop in well water (I) is its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "Falling wells did not cause the extra pumping; the canal water shortage did.", "2": "Statement II is itself the direct cause of statement I.", "3": "Pumping groundwater clearly lowers the water level in wells."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0466' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Untreated factory waste can contain poisons and use up the oxygen in water, which kills fish. The waste was released upstream, so it flowed to where the fish died. So the waste (II) is the cause and the fish deaths (I) are its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "Dead fish do not make a factory release waste.", "2": "The waste is itself the direct cause of the fish deaths.", "3": "Water pollution and fish deaths are clearly linked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0467' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Many students depend on college buses to get to classes. When the bus service stopped, those students found it hard to reach the college, so attendance fell. So stopping the buses (II) is the cause and the fall in attendance (I) is its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "The natural reading is that the bus service stopped first and attendance fell as a result, not the other way round.", "2": "Stopping the bus service is itself the direct cause of the fall.", "3": "Transport directly affects whether students can attend, so the two are linked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0468' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A severe cold spell explains both statements. Frost forms in freezing weather and damages crops, and families burn more gas at home to keep warm. Both are effects of the same cold weather; neither causes the other.', wrong = '{"0": "Damage to crops does not make households burn more gas.", "1": "Burning gas at home does not cause frost in the fields.", "3": "Both share a common cause, the severe cold, so they are related."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0469' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heavy rain explains both statements. People buy umbrellas to keep dry, and low-lying streets flood because rainwater collects there. Both are effects of the same cause, the rain.', wrong = '{"0": "Buying umbrellas does not flood streets.", "1": "Flooded streets alone do not create a need for umbrellas; the falling rain does.", "3": "Both follow from heavy rain, so they are related."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0470' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An unusually early cold spell explains both statements. Schools start winter holidays early because of the cold, and people buy gas heaters to keep warm. Both are effects of the same cause; neither causes the other.', wrong = '{"0": "School holidays do not make people buy heaters; the cold does.", "1": "Heater sales do not make schools close their doors early.", "3": "Both come from the early cold, so they are related."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0471' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A college cricket team winning a cup and the price of wheat flour rising have no causal link. Neither can cause the other, and no single event would reasonably explain both. The two statements are not causally related.', wrong = '{"0": "A cricket win cannot change the price of flour.", "1": "Flour prices do not decide the result of a cricket match.", "2": "No single event would reasonably cause both a cricket win and a flour price rise."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0472' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Opening a new hospital and a rise in mango exports from Multan are unrelated events. A hospital does not affect mango trade, and mango exports do not cause hospitals to open. The statements are not causally related.', wrong = '{"0": "A hospital does not increase mango exports.", "1": "Mango exports did not cause the hospital to open.", "2": "No obvious single cause explains both a new hospital and higher mango exports."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0473' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A hockey team losing a match and more students passing the MDCAT are unrelated events. Neither can affect the other, and no single cause explains both. The two statements are not causally related.', wrong = '{"0": "A hockey loss does not affect MDCAT results.", "1": "MDCAT results do not affect a hockey match.", "2": "No single cause would reasonably explain both a hockey loss and better MDCAT results."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0474' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heavy snowfall blocked the road near Murree, so vehicles could not move and travellers were stuck overnight. So the snowfall (II) is the cause and the stranded travellers (I) are its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "Stranded travellers cannot cause snowfall.", "2": "The snow-blocked road is itself the direct cause; no third event is needed.", "3": "A blocked road clearly strands travellers, so the two are linked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0475' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Exam results are processed on the board''s computer system. A cyber-attack on that system would disrupt the work and delay the results. So the attack (II) is the cause and the late announcement (I) is its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "Announcing results late does not cause a cyber-attack.", "2": "The attack is itself the direct cause of the delay.", "3": "Processing results depends on the computer system, so the two are linked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0476' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Students and cooks ate and drank from the same hostel kitchen, and both groups fell ill with the same illness at the same time. The most likely explanation is a shared source, such as contaminated food or water, that made both ill. So both are effects of a common cause.', wrong = '{"0": "Students passing the illness to the cooks is unlikely; both groups point to a shared source.", "1": "Sick cooks could spread germs, but both groups falling ill together points most strongly to a shared contaminated source.", "3": "Two groups in the same place falling ill with the same illness are very likely linked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0477' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Along a coast, fresh groundwater sits next to salty seawater underground. When too much fresh water is pumped out, seawater moves in to fill the space. This is called saltwater intrusion, and over a few years it makes well water salty.', wrong = '{"0": "What people eat does not change the water in the wells.", "1": "Building a road does not make groundwater salty.", "2": "More rainfall would add fresh water underground and make wells less salty, not more."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0478' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A cause comes before an event and helps bring it about. Driving for 16 hours without rest makes a driver tired and slow to react, a common cause of crashes. The other options all happened after the crash, as results of it.', wrong = '{"1": "Taking injured passengers to hospital is a result of the crash.", "2": "Closing the road for hours is a result of the crash.", "3": "Ordering an inquiry is a response to the crash."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0479' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An effect comes after an event and results from it. Tube wells that run on electricity cannot pump without power, so the water supply stops because of the power cut. The other options are possible causes of the cut.', wrong = '{"0": "An overloaded transformer is a possible cause of the power cut, not its effect.", "2": "Storm damage to power lines is a possible cause of the cut.", "3": "Demand greater than supply is a possible cause of the cut."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0480' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A fire needs a source of ignition to start. Sparks from faulty wiring provide that ignition. The cotton was fuel that helped the fire spread, and the blocked exits made it more dangerous, but neither started it.', wrong = '{"0": "Blocked exits made escape harder, but they did not start the fire.", "1": "Cotton burns easily, but it needs something like a spark to ignite it.", "3": "Night-time does not start a fire; it only affects who is present."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0481' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The chain is: heavy rain → river overflows → crops destroyed → less food → higher prices. The immediate cause is the step just before the result. Food prices rose because the crops were destroyed and less food was available.', wrong = '{"0": "The rain is the starting cause at the beginning of the chain, not the immediate one.", "1": "The flood led to the crop loss, which then raised prices, so it is an earlier step.", "2": "Being near the river is a background condition, not a cause of the price rise."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0482' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If full moons caused more emergencies, two years of records should show more patients on those nights. The hospital found no difference, so its evidence does not support the belief, at least for this hospital.', wrong = '{"1": "The records show no extra emergencies on full-moon nights.", "2": "There is no reason to doubt records just because they disagree with a popular belief.", "3": "People can sincerely hold a mistaken belief without lying."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0483' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'One person''s case cannot disprove a pattern found across very large numbers of people. Large studies show that smokers, on average, live shorter and less healthy lives. An uncle who lived to 95 is an exception, not proof that smoking is safe.', wrong = '{"0": "There is no reason to doubt the uncle smoked; his case simply does not prove the general claim.", "2": "Smoking harms people of all ages, not only the old.", "3": "This is untrue, since many people who reach 95 have illnesses, and it does not answer the argument."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0484' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A constructive way of thinking looks for causes that can be changed. Finding which topics cost Saad marks and planning focused practice turns the setback into a clear plan for improving his score next time.', wrong = '{"0": "One result does not decide every future exam.", "1": "Blaming the test leaves nothing to learn and nothing to improve.", "3": "Preparation clearly affects results, so luck is not the only factor."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0485' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Trust is built by honest, evidence-based reasons. This argument admits the real but minor side effect, explains that a short fever shows the body is responding, and gives strong evidence that vaccines prevent far more illness than they cause.', wrong = '{"0": "Calling parents foolish insults them and does not persuade them.", "1": "This is untrue, since mild side effects like fever are common, and parents who saw the fever will not believe it.", "2": "An appeal to authority alone is weaker than explaining the evidence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0486' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Tests found harmful bacteria in the city''s water supply. Learning this, families bought water filters to make their water safe. So the test result (II) is the cause and the purchase of filters (I) is its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "Buying filters does not put bacteria into the water supply.", "2": "The test result is itself the direct cause of buying filters.", "3": "Water quality directly affects whether people buy filters."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0487' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Dengue is spread by Aedes mosquitoes, which become much less active and breed less when nights are cool. When night temperatures dropped below 15 °C in October, mosquito activity fell, and so did dengue cases. So the cooler weather (II) caused the fall in cases (I).', wrong = '{"0": "Fewer dengue cases cannot lower the temperature.", "2": "The cooler weather is itself the direct cause of fewer cases.", "3": "Temperature strongly affects mosquito activity and dengue spread."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0488' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Traders went on strike against a new tax, and a traders'' strike means they keep their shops shut. So the strike (II) is the cause and the week of closed shops (I) is its effect.', wrong = '{"0": "Closed shops did not cause a tax protest; the strike came first.", "2": "The strike itself explains the closures; no third event is needed.", "3": "A traders'' strike clearly means closed shops, so the two are linked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0489' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Town size is a common cause. Bigger towns have more buildings and people, so they have more fires and more fire damage, and they also need more fire engines. The link between engines and damage comes from size, not from the engines causing damage.', wrong = '{"1": "The colour of fire engines is irrelevant to fire damage.", "2": "Fire engines using water does not explain why towns with more of them have more damage.", "3": "The age of some engines does not explain the pattern across towns."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'LR-0490' AND answer = 0;
